window.DDJ_COMM={"intro":["This commentary goes through the Tao Te Ching chapter by chapter, as the globe carries it: Wang Bi’s text, 81 chapters in 201 passages. Each chapter opens with one sentence on what it says, then moves three times.","Exegesis: what the characters say, and where the grammar or the manuscripts fork. Analysis: how the chapter is built, and what the English keeps or softens. Discussion: what is at stake, and where a reader can push back.","Five witnesses are cited by name. Wang Bi (226–249) wrote the commentary through which the book was read for most of its history; his text is the one carved on the globe. Heshang Gong’s commentary, of the Han by tradition, reads the book as self-cultivation, and the chapter titles are his. Fu Yi’s “ancient text” was collated in the Tang. The two silk manuscripts from Mawangdui were buried in 168 BCE, with the De half first. The Guodian bamboo slips, buried around 300 BCE, hold material from 31 chapters and are the oldest Laozi there is.","“The Daozang plain text” means the unannotated Tao Te Ching in the Ming Daoist canon (DZ 664), not the Daozang print of Wang Bi that the globe carries.","Where this commentary quotes the silk manuscripts, Heshang Gong or Fu Yi, the readings have been checked against the texts shown in the comparison square. Readings from the Guodian slips, and references to the Zhuangzi, Han Feizi, Mencius, the Analects and other classics, are given from the standard editions and have not yet been checked line by line. They are flagged as such until they are.","The commentary and the English translation were written with Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, and checked line by line against the Chinese."],"ch":{"1":{"lead":"The first chapter is a warning label on its own book: whatever can be said of the Way is not the lasting one, and names work the same way.","ex":["道可道 uses one character three times. The middle 道 is a verb: to speak, and also to walk or to lead. So the line reads both “the Way that can be told” and “the way that can be taken as a way”.","常 means constant in the sense of lasting, not timeless. The silk copies have 恆 instead. 常 replaced it once the personal name of the Han emperor Wen, Liu Heng 劉恆, made the character taboo.","Two lines can be cut two ways. 無名天地之始 is either “nameless: the beginning of heaven and earth” or “nothing is the name of heaven and earth’s beginning”. 常無欲以觀其妙 is either “always without desire, to watch its subtlety” or “in constant nothing, wishing to watch its subtlety”.","The second cuts are Song-dynasty readings, favoured by Wang Anshi among others. The silk text rules out the latter one. It writes 恆無欲也, with a particle after 欲, so desire belongs to the phrase before it.","So the plain reading stands, and it is the stranger one. Desire is not condemned. Without desire you watch the 妙, the fine and barely visible. With desire you watch the 徼, the border where things end.","Wang Bi glosses 徼 as where things come to rest. The silk copies read 所噭, which some translators take as what one is after. On that reading, with desire you see what you are looking for.","玄 is a colour before it is a concept: black with red in it, by the oldest dictionary’s account. 玄之又玄 has the grammar of chapter 48’s 損之又損, “lessen it and lessen it again”, where the repeated word is a verb. Read that way the phrase is an instruction: darken it, then darken it again."],"an":["The globe cuts the chapter into four passages, and they step down a ladder: Way and name, nameless and named, two ways of looking, the dark and its gate. Each step is a pair. Then the pairs are refused as final: “These two come out of the same place.”","Which two is left open. It could be nameless and named, or the desireless view and the desiring one. The silk text is terser here: 異名同謂, different names, same thing meant.","The English keeps the plain cuts and keeps 玄 as “dark”, which is right. It also keeps the purpose in 以觀, “in order to watch”: “always free of desire, watch its mystery.” The Chinese describes two deliberate practices of attention, not a result.","The book’s feminine imagery starts here. 母, mother, is the named. And 始, beginning, is written with the woman radical; the oldest dictionary defines it as the start of a woman. Some commentators hear a pair in that: maiden for the unnamed, mother for the named.","The gate, 門, closes the chapter and returns in chapters 6, 10 and 52. A gate is an emptiness you pass through, which makes it the right last word.","Placement matters too. In the silk copies the De half comes first, so the book opened with chapter 38 and this chapter began the second half. Guodian, older still, has no chapter 1 at all. Opening on a doubt about speech was the received arrangement’s decision."],"di":["The obvious objection is old. The Tang poet Bai Juyi asked why, if those who know do not speak, the old man wrote five thousand characters.","The chapter has an answer, and it sits in the lines people skip. It does not say the Way cannot be spoken. It says the spoken one is not the constant one, and then it calls the named “the mother of the ten thousand things”. Naming is generative: it is how a world of separate things gets born.","Likewise desire. A reader who comes for renunciation finds a text that gives desire its own field of vision: edges, outcomes, what things come to. The error is not wanting. The error is taking the edges for the whole.","Two eyes, then: one for the subtle, one for the edges. The chapter is less a ban on language than an instruction for holding it: use the name, and keep watching what it leaves out. The live question for any reader is which eye they habitually close."]},"2":{"lead":"Chapter 2 says every value arrives with its opposite, and draws a working rule from that: do the work, and do not move into it.","ex":["The first sentence is harsher in Chinese than in most English. 斯惡已 can mean “there is already ugliness”, as the globe has it. It can also mean “this is ugly”: once everyone agrees what beauty is, the agreement is the ugly thing.","Six pairs follow, each joined by 相, “each other”. Being and nothing 相生, give birth to each other. Chapter 40 will say something else: being is born from nothing, one way. The book holds both and never reconciles them.","The globe reads 長短相較, “long and short measure each other”, which is Wang Bi’s text. Both older copies have 形, “shape each other”, and 形 keeps the rhyme with 生, 成 and 傾. The silk and bamboo reading is probably the older one; 較 is the one the tradition read.","音 and 聲 are not synonyms. 聲 is raw sound. 音 is sound that has taken a pattern, a note; the old Record of Music defines it that way. The globe does this to the text itself: every character that crosses the middle is plucked as a tone.","The silk copies end the list with one more word: 恆也, “this is constant”. So the mutual making of opposites is itself the constant thing. That answers chapter 1’s question about what lasts.","Then the sage enters, for the first time in the book, carrying two new terms: 無為, not-doing, and 不言之教, teaching without words.","萬物作焉而不辭 forks. 辭 is “to decline”, so the sage “turns none of them away”, as the globe has it. The silk copy reads 弗始 instead: the things arise and the sage “does not begin them”, claims no authorship.","生而不有, “gives life without owning”, is missing from both older copies. It probably drifted in from chapters 10 and 51, where it also stands."],"an":["The chapter is one argument in three passages, hinged on 故 and 是以, “so” and “therefore”. Naming a good makes a not-good. So opposites make each other. Therefore the sage pushes neither side.","This is the first justification 無為 gets, and it is a point about language, not about idleness. If every “good” produces its shadow, campaigning for the good produces shadow too. Not-doing means declining to force one side of a pair.","The chapter is also about where one lives. Three words of dwelling frame the sage: 處, to stay in, at the start; 居, to settle in, near the end; 去, to leave, as the last word. The sage stays in not-doing and refuses to settle in finished work.","The English catches this exactly: “finishes the work and doesn’t settle into it.” In the old pronunciation 居 and 去 rhymed, and the last line plays them against each other. Because the sage does not settle in it, it does not leave.","Heshang Gong’s title, “Nourishing the Person”, fits badly. The mismatch is worth keeping in mind. The titles record how one Han reader used the book: as a manual for the body."],"di":["Is this relativism? Not quite. The text never says beauty and ugliness are the same. It says they are born together, and its target is narrower than values as such. The target is 天下皆知, what everyone under heaven knows: the fixed public label.","A reader can push back that some distinctions should be forced. A doctor does push health against sickness. The chapter’s reply would concern manner: the sage “acts without leaning on it”. The action stays. The claim on it goes.","The closing rule is the most portable sentence in these nine chapters. It reads as advice to anyone who makes or raises anything: a parent, a teacher, a builder. The work stays yours on one condition, that you do not move into it.","The oldest evidence supports its weight. The chapter is on the Guodian slips, among the earliest Laozi material there is."]},"3":{"lead":"Chapter 3 turns the last chapter’s insight into policy: stop ranking, pricing and displaying, and the scramble they cause stops too. It is also the first chapter that can be read as sinister.","ex":["不尚賢, “do not exalt the worthy”, is a slogan turned inside out. 尚賢, “exalt the worthy”, was a Mohist doctrine and a chapter title in the Mozi. The line answers a live debate of the Warring States.","見 in 不見可欲 is read xiàn, “to display”. The fault is not that desirable things exist. The fault is showing them.","Three causes, three effects. Rank breeds 爭, contention. Rare goods breed 盜, theft. Display breeds 亂, turmoil in the heart. Desire here is not a private appetite. It is produced socially, by what gets honoured and shown.","Then four verbs in two pairs: 虛其心，實其腹，弱其志，強其骨. Empty the heart, fill the belly; weaken the will, strengthen the bones. What thinks and wants is reduced. What simply lives is fed.","其, “their”, is the crux. If it means the people’s, this is a ruler’s programme. Heshang Gong reads it inward: the sage governs the state the way he governs his own body, so the first heart emptied is his own.","常使民無知無欲 is blunter than the globe’s “free of cunning and craving”. The Chinese says: without knowledge, without desire. The softer reading is defensible, since 知 can mean knowingness, the cleverness the next line attacks.","But the hard reading has support inside the book. Chapter 65 says the ancients who followed the Way did not enlighten the people. They kept them simple.","為無為 is the first time not-doing appears as something one does: the verb 為 takes its own negation as object. 治 means governed, in order, and also healed. The globe’s “nothing is out of order” keeps the widest sense."],"an":["The chapter applies chapter 2 to a society. There, calling something good made the not-good. Here, exalting someone as worthy makes rivals. The logic is identical. Only the scale changes.","The globe’s three passages are diagnosis, treatment and formula. The treatment works by subtraction at the source. It does not punish thieves. It stops pricing the thing they would steal.","The silk copy ends more plainly: make the knowing not dare, just do not act, and nothing is ungoverned. The received 為無為 tightens that remark into a formula, and the formula returns at the head of chapter 63.","“Don’t put the worthy on a pedestal” is lively and exact. 賢 is a moral word, “the worthy”, which is why the line stung Confucians and Mohists alike."],"di":["Two readings sit in the same sentences, and history has used both.","The first is control. Keep them fed, keep them ignorant, make the clever afraid to move. Han Feizi, the Legalist, wrote the earliest surviving commentary on this book. The reading has a pedigree.","The second is a refusal to manufacture envy. On this reading the chapter describes a mechanism: honour a few, prize the scarce, display the desirable, and you get rivalry, theft and unrest by design. A reader today meets all three on any screen: the ranked, the scarce and the displayed.","The test between the readings is who does the emptying, and to whom. Done to others, it is management. Done to oneself, 虛其心 is the practice the whole book circles. Chapter 16 opens with it: 致虛極, take emptiness to its limit.","The text does not let a reader choose cleanly. It is addressed to rulers, and it means what it says. That discomfort is part of reading it honestly."]},"4":{"lead":"Chapter 4 describes the Way as an empty vessel with no pedigree, and it does so almost entirely in the grammar of “seems”.","ex":["沖 stands for 盅. The oldest dictionary defines 盅 as an empty vessel and quotes this very line to illustrate it. So the image is a cup: the Way is a cup that use never fills.","That is backwards on purpose. Using a cup should empty it. This one is poured into and never fills, because it has no bottom. Chapter 45 turns the image over: great fullness seems empty, and its use never runs out.","Count the hedges. 或 is “perhaps”, 似 is “seems”, 似或 is “seems perhaps”, 象 is “looks like”. In forty-two characters the text commits to almost nothing. The silk copy has one hedge fewer: it reads 又, “again”, for the first 或.","The vocabulary is genealogical throughout. 宗 is an ancestor, the head of a lineage. 子 is a child. 先 is a forebear. The chapter puts the Way into a family tree and the tree fails: ancestor of everything, child of no one known, older than 帝.","帝 is Di, the high god of Shang and Zhou religion and the supreme ancestor. The character appears nowhere else in the book. The English says “the Lord on High”: the top of the ancestral order, not its maker. “God” with a capital would import a creator.","The four middle lines recur word for word in chapter 56. Many editors think they strayed here, since they break the parallel between 淵兮 and 湛兮. If so, they strayed early. The silk copies already have them in this chapter.","其 in those lines is ambiguous. Either the Way blunts what is sharp in things, or it blunts its own edge and dims its own light. The globe splits the difference: three lines act on things, and the last turns inward, “becomes one with the dust”.","湛 is the stillness of deep, clear water. The globe’s “Clear and still, it seems almost to be there” is exact, down to the double hedge."],"an":["Three passages, three questions: what is it for, what is it like, where does it come from. Use, likeness, origin. Only the first gets a firm answer.","The hedging is the method. Chapter 1 said the spoken Way is not the constant one. Chapter 4 shows what speech under that constraint sounds like: nearly every predicate carries a “seems”.","若存, existing “as if”, comes back twice. Chapter 6 says the valley spirit is barely there, 綿綿若存. Chapter 41 uses 若存若亡, half there and half gone, for the middling student’s hold on the Way. The same phrase covers how the Way is and how it is half-grasped.","Two words here, 盈 and 銳, full and sharp, return in chapter 9 as advice: do not fill, do not sharpen. What the Way is in chapter 4, a person is told to be in chapter 9."],"di":["The last line is theological audacity said quietly. In a world where every power had ancestors, the text places something before the highest ancestor and declines to say what begot it. “I don’t know” is the first thing the book’s “I” ever says.","和光同塵, “soften the light, merge with the dust”, became a four-character idiom for keeping a low profile. It has been used both ways: for humility, and for going along with a rotten age. The text itself only describes the Way: it is the highest thing, and it does not shine.","A reader can ask whether something described only by “seems” is anything at all. The chapter’s wager is that the hedge is accuracy, not evasion. This is what honest speech about a source sounds like."]},"5":{"lead":"Chapter 5 says the world plays no favourites, and then says why it can afford not to: it is hollow, and the hollow does not run out.","ex":["仁 is the Confucian virtue, humaneness: a love that starts with kin and grades outward. 天地不仁 denies it to the cosmos, and the next line denies it to the sage. The globe’s “not kind” is plain and loses the target. This line is aimed at a school.","芻狗 are straw dogs, made for sacrifice. The Zhuangzi describes their career. Before the offering they are kept in boxes and wrapped in embroidery, and the priest fasts before handling them. Afterward passers-by walk on them and they are burned as kindling.","So the image is not contempt. It is honour in season and no clinging after. Wang Bi read the phrase differently, as two words: heaven does not grow grass for animals or dogs for people, yet animals eat the grass and people eat the dogs. Both readings land in the same place: no special favour.","橐龠 is a bellows: 橐 the leather bag, 龠 the pipe. 龠 is also a flute, so the space between heaven and earth is close to a wind instrument. 虛而不屈 is “empty and never exhausted”, or “empty and never collapsed”. 屈 allows both.","In 多言數窮, 數 is either “quickly” or “reckoning”. The silk copies read 多聞, “much learning”, where the received text has 多言, “many words”. On that version the line is aimed at erudition, not talk.","中 is not the Confucian mean. It is the middle of the bellows: the hollow. “Better to keep to the centre” means keep to the empty part."],"an":["The three passages were probably three separate sayings. The Guodian slips have only the bellows. The straw dogs and the warning about words are absent there.","Whoever joined them saw a real connection. The bellows is empty and never runs out. The talker is full and soon runs dry. 不屈 and 窮, unexhausted and exhausted, face each other across the passage break.","The first passage belongs with them too. Humaneness is a content: a preference, a filling. Heaven and earth can treat all things evenly because they hold nothing. The space between them is a hollow that breathes.","The title names the theme these nine chapters keep returning to: the cup of chapter 4, the valley of chapter 6, and later the hub, the pot and the room of chapter 11. Emptiness is never mere absence in this book. It is always in use."],"di":["不仁 can be heard three ways. Indifferent: nature does not care, the tragic reading. Impartial: nature has no favourites, the egalitarian one. Unsentimental: each thing is honoured in its season and released, the ritual one. The straw dog supports the third most strongly.","The second line is harder. Would anyone want a ruler, or a friend, who treats them as a straw dog? The line guards against one specific vice: love as favouritism and possession. Chapter 2 had the same thought in a gentler key: “gives life without owning”.","It remains a line to argue with, and the book argues with it. Chapter 8 counts 仁 among the good things. Chapter 67 names compassion, 慈, as the first of three treasures. The book attacks the Confucian word and keeps the tenderness.","The question the chapter leaves with a reader is practical. Can you let a thing have had its season?"]},"6":{"lead":"Chapter 6 gives the book’s central image in twenty-five characters: the source of the world is an opening, female, and still open.","ex":["谷 is a valley: low, hollow, receiving. 神 is spirit, the numinous. What never dies here is an emptiness. The silk copies write 浴 for 谷, a loan.","Heshang Gong reads the line as an instruction. He takes 谷 as “nourish”: nourish the spirits and you will not die. In his version 玄 is heaven and the nose, 牝 is earth and the mouth, and the passage is about breathing. Later breath practice leans on this reading.","牝 is a stock-breeder’s word. It means a female animal, a mare or a cow, as 牡 means the male. It is not the word for a woman, and it is not a goddess. The gate of the dark female reads as a birth canal.","So the chapter joins two things that should not fit: gate and root. A gate is an opening. A root is a base. Heaven and earth are rooted in an opening.","綿綿 is silk floss: fine, continuous, unbroken. 若存, “as if there”, is chapter 4’s hedge again. The globe’s “Barely there, it goes on and on” carries both halves.","用之不勤 forks. 勤 is “exhausted”, which gives the globe’s “it never wears out”. 勤 is also “toil”, which gives “use it without strain”. Heshang Gong takes the second: breathe gently, never forcing."],"an":["The globe keeps this chapter as a single passage, and it should. It is one sentence of definition, built by chaining. The valley spirit is the dark female. The dark female’s gate is the root. The root persists. 是謂, “this is called”, does the linking twice.","Every key word has appeared already or will return. 玄 and 門 come from chapter 1 and its “gate of every mystery”. The mother of chapter 1 becomes the female animal here. The inexhaustible hollow is chapter 5’s bellows. The valley returns in chapters 15, 28 and 41.","The passage may be older than the book. The Liezi quotes these exact lines and credits them to a Book of the Yellow Emperor. The Guodian slips do not have the chapter."],"di":["The claim about origin is unusual. Creation here is not an event in the past. The gate is open now, and heaven and earth are rooted in it now. The world is being born continuously, out of something that looks like nothing.","The feminine is this book’s signature among early Chinese texts, and it deserves a careful reading. It is a principle, not a politics. Chapter 28 tells the ruler to know the male and keep to the female: the female is a position a man is told to take. Nothing here speaks about women.","Still, the choice of image is a real choice. Other traditions begin with a word, a command or a craftsman. This one begins with a birth that no one orders. And it picks the plainest term available, the one used for livestock, where it could have picked a queen.","The practical reading and the cosmological one do not compete. The valley may be the hollow at the centre of the world, or the breath kept thin and unforced. The rule is the same either way: what is not grasped does not run out."]},"7":{"lead":"Chapter 7 argues that what does not live for itself outlasts what does, and it ends on the most suspicious sentence in these nine chapters.","ex":["天長地久 became an idiom for anything meant to last, love above all. Bai Juyi’s Song of Lasting Regret ends by turning it around: 天長地久有時盡，此恨綿綿無絕期. Heaven and earth, long as they last, will end; this regret goes on and on. His word for “goes on” is 綿綿, taken from chapter 6.","不自生 is the hinge. It can mean “don’t live for themselves”, as the globe has it, or “do not give themselves life”. Either way, heaven and earth do not make their own continuance their project. 長生, “long life”, later became the Daoist term for longevity and immortality.","身 is body, person, self. The sage 後其身, puts the self behind, and 外其身, puts it outside, treats it as external. The results are 身先 and 身存: the self in front, the self preserved. The silk copies have 退, “withdraws”, for the first verb.","The last line is the problem. 私 is not “self”. It is private interest: what is one’s own as against what is shared. 非以其無私邪？故能成其私 asks: is it not because he has no private interest that he can accomplish his private interest?","The English keeps the ledger: “Isn’t it because they want nothing for themselves / that what is theirs comes to them?” A softer version, with no self in it and the self coming to fulfilment, would make a spiritual sentence. The Chinese is balder: no private ends, therefore private ends achieved."],"an":["The argument is an analogy in two passages, exactly as the globe divides it. Heaven and earth do not live for themselves, so they last. The sage puts the self last, so the self arrives.","Yield and thereby gain is the book’s most repeated shape. Chapter 66 says rivers and seas rule the hundred valleys by lying below them. Chapter 67 makes “not daring to be first in the world” the third of its three treasures.","There is a tension with chapter 23, where even heaven and earth cannot make a storm last. The two fit once the object is noticed. What heaven and earth do not force endures. What they force is over before noon.","Heshang Gong’s title, “Sheathing the Light”, leans one way. A sheathed blade is still a blade. The later idiom 韜光養晦, hide your light and bide your time, is frankly tactical."],"di":["If the sage puts himself last in order to come first, has he put himself last? The objection is old. Zhu Xi, the great Song Confucian, judged that the teaching slides into stratagem.","There are three ways to answer. The cynical one accepts the charge: this is advice for getting ahead, and it works. The descriptive one says the line reports a consequence, not a motive. A self that is not defended has nothing to lose, so it survives.","The third says the 私 that is fulfilled is not the 私 that was given up. The small one yields. A larger one, identified with the whole, is what arrives.","The text does not choose, and its question mark matters: it asks, it does not assert. A fair test separates strategy from nature. Strategy stops when it stops paying. Heaven and earth do not check.","The book probably knows both readings and trusts the practice to sort its readers. Someone who yields as a tactic is still watching the score, and it shows."]},"8":{"lead":"Chapter 8 picks water as the nearest thing in the world to the Way, for two reasons: it helps everything, and it goes where no one else will.","ex":["上善若水: the highest good is like water. 幾於道 is exact about the limit of the image. 幾 means “near”. Water is close to the Way. It is not the Way.","處眾人之所惡: it stays in the places the crowd detests. 惡, read wù, is stronger than the globe’s “avoids”. The low place is not merely unfashionable. People find it shameful.","Seven lines of three characters follow, each built X 善 Y. In dwelling, the good is ground. In the heart, depth. In giving, humaneness. In speech, good faith. In governing, order. In work, ability. In movement, timing.","The globe turns them into imperatives: “Live close to the ground. Keep the heart deep.” That is a fair choice. The Chinese neither requires it nor forbids it.","Each quality belongs to water as much as to a person. Water seeks the ground. It pools deep; 淵 is chapter 4’s abyss. It moves when the season lets it. 時, timing, appears nowhere else in the book.","The third line is a surprise: 與善仁, in giving, humaneness. Chapter 5 has just said that heaven, earth and the sage are 不仁. The silk copies remove the problem. One reads 予善天, “in giving, be like heaven”. The other goes straight from giving to good faith. Neither has 仁, which looks like a later, Confucian-leaning hand.","The silk copies differ on the second line too. Where the received text says water 不爭, “does not contend”, one copy says it 有靜, “has stillness”. The received text chose the word that closes a ring with the last line."],"an":["The chapter is a ring. 不爭 stands near the beginning and at the end, and the seven qualities sit inside it. The globe’s three passages follow the joints: the image, the seven, the verdict.","Water was the shared image of the age, and each school saw its own virtue in it. Confucius stood by a river and saw time passing without rest. Mencius praised water for having a source and filling every hollow before moving on. Sunzi said an army’s form should be like water’s: it avoids the high ground and strikes the empty place.","The Laozi’s water is none of these. It is not persistence, progress or tactics. It is position: the willingness to be below. Chapter 66 draws the political conclusion. Chapter 78 adds the edge the image lacks here: nothing is softer than water, and nothing beats it at wearing down the hard.","Water mattered beyond metaphor in this milieu. A short cosmology found with the Guodian slips, The Great One Gives Birth to Water, makes water the first thing born."],"di":["Why does not competing remove blame? Because blame needs a rival, and no one competes for the bottom. The place everyone detests is the one place that is always free.","That logic has a dangerous use. Told to those already at the bottom, “be like water” is a way of keeping them there. The book’s addressee matters here. It speaks to rulers and the powerful. Going low is asked of the high.","Non-contention is also not passivity. The water of this chapter 善利萬物, is good at benefiting everything. It works constantly. What it lacks is the claim.","無尤, “no blame”, is a modest reward. The chapter does not promise that water wins. It promises that nothing can be held against it. For a text so often read as a manual for success, that is worth noticing."]},"9":{"lead":"Chapter 9 gives four pictures of too much and one rule: when the work is done, step back.","ex":["持而盈之 is to hold a vessel and fill it. The globe drops the holding (“Better to stop than to fill it to the brim”), and with it part of the picture: a brimming cup cannot be carried. 已 means stop, and the stopping comes before the brim.","Readers have long tied the line to the tilting vessel that rulers kept beside their seat. The Xunzi tells the story. Confucius sees one in a temple and has water poured in. Empty it leans, half full it stands straight, full it overturns.","揣而銳之 is to hammer an edge to its keenest. The thinnest edge is the first to go. 盈 and 銳, full and sharp, are the two words chapter 4 denied of the Way: it is never full, and it blunts the sharp. Here the same two become advice.","金玉滿堂, “gold and jade fill the hall”, survives as a New Year blessing, written on red paper and hung by the door. The blessing quotes half a sentence. The other half says no one can guard it. The silk copies have a smaller picture, 金玉盈室: a room, not a hall, and 盈 again.","In 自遺其咎, 遺 is to bequeath. The proud leave themselves a legacy of trouble. The globe’s “you bring the trouble on yourself” keeps the sense and loses the inheritance.","功遂身退: the work accomplished, the person withdraws. 退 is to step back, not to vanish. Wang Bi’s gloss is the seasons: each finishes its work and moves on. Heshang Gong’s text makes it three steps: work done, name made, self withdrawn.","天之道, “the Way of heaven”, appears here for the first time. It appears for the last time in the book’s final sentence."],"an":["Four images, four failures: the cup spills, the edge dulls, the hall is robbed, the proud fall. Then one line turns from warning to rule. The globe’s two passages mark that turn.","The chapter completes a thought begun in chapter 2. There the sage “finishes the work and doesn’t settle into it”, and the reason given was gain: what is not claimed is not lost. Here the same act is seen from the side of risk: what is clung to is destroyed.","It is one of the oldest attested chapters. The Guodian slips have it, ending on the same sentence.","Later chapters give the virtue two names. 知足 is knowing what is enough. 知止 is knowing when to stop. Chapter 44 joins them.","Heshang Gong’s title is the most opaque so far. 運 is to turn and 夷 is level: roughly, turning back toward the even."],"di":["In Chinese political memory this chapter became survival advice. Fan Li helped the king of Yue destroy his enemy and then left by boat; his colleague Wen Zhong stayed and was made to kill himself. Zhang Liang helped found the Han and then withdrew; Han Xin did not, and was executed. Both pairs are stock illustrations of the last line.","That history shows the limit of reading the chapter as prudence. Prudence asks only what is safe. The last line claims more: stepping back is 天之道, the pattern by which seasons give way. Summer does not retire to avoid punishment.","The hard part is the word 遂, “accomplished”. Nothing in the chapter says how to know the work is done. The brim is easy to see in a cup. It is hard to see in a career, a project, or a place in someone else’s life.","The chapter’s one hint is in its first line: stop before full. Anyone who waits to be sure has already held on too long."]},"10":{"lead":"Chapter 10 is the book’s first practice text: six questions, each asking whether a power can be held without gripping it.","ex":["Every line ends 能…乎, “can you?”. The form was in circulation. The Inward Training chapter of the Guanzi asks in a row: can you concentrate, can you be one, can you stop? The Zhuangzi puts a similar catechism in Laozi’s own mouth, ending “can you be a baby?”","營魄 is the pair of souls, the airy one and the bodily one, whose parting is death. The same phrase, 載營魄, opens a spirit journey in the Songs of Chu. 抱一, “holding to the one”, asks the two to stay together.","In 專氣致柔, 專 stands for 摶, to gather or roll into a ball. The Inward Training uses the same phrase. 氣, breath, appears here for the first time and only twice more in the book.","In 滌除玄覽, the received 覽, “vision”, is the silk copy’s 監, “mirror”. The globe translates the mirror: “Wiping clean the dark mirror”. The mind as a mirror to be kept clean has a long afterlife, from the Zhuangzi to the mirror verses of the Platform Sutra.","The fourth question turns to the state without warning: 愛民治國, loving the people and governing the country. Wang Bi’s text asks 能無知乎, “can you do it without cleverness?”, and his commentary explains that governing without cleverness means setting cleverness aside. Many modern editions swap this question with the sixth. Wang Bi’s commentary shows he read 無知 here and 無為 at the end.","天門, heaven’s gate, is the nostrils for Heshang Gong and the hinge between order and disorder for Wang Bi. 能為雌乎 asks: can you take the female part? Wang Bi’s own text has 無雌, “without the female”, which makes poor sense. The silk has 為.","The last question in the silk copy is 能毋以知乎: can you do it without using knowledge? That 以 matters. The question is not about being ignorant. It is about what you see by.","The bracketed lines recur at the end of chapter 51, and many modern editors judge them misplaced here. The globe’s brackets record that judgment. The silk copy already has most of them in this chapter, so the misplacing, if it is one, is very old.","玄德, “dark virtue”, is the first appearance of 德 in the book. In 長而不宰, 宰 is to preside, and literally to carve meat: to raise without carving up."],"an":["The six questions run from inside to outside: souls, breath, mind, then people, heaven’s gate, the four quarters. The same discipline is asked of a body and of a kingdom. Later tradition called the pattern inner sage, outer king.","Each question couples a power with a restraint. Hold the one, and do not split. Gather breath, to softness. Polish, to spotlessness. Govern, without doing. Open and close, as the female. See everywhere, without cleverness. The grammar of all six is full strength with an open hand.","The newborn is the first of the book’s infants. It returns in chapters 20, 28 and 55, where its weak bones and firm grip are the point.","The form matters as much as the content. A command can be obeyed once. A question has to be asked again. A chapter of questions is built for repetition, which is what a practice is.","The English is strong here. “Gathering your breath until it is soft” gives 專氣致柔 as a process with a direction. “Leading without ruling” for 長而不宰 is clean, though it loses the knife."],"di":["This chapter anchors the oldest way of using the book: as instruction for the body. Heshang Gong reads every line as breath and spirit. Some modern scholars argue the whole book grew out of circles that practised this kind of inner cultivation.","What keeps it from being only a meditation manual is question four. The text will not let the inner work stay private. A ruler’s breath and a ruler’s policy are one subject.","The honest answer to each question is “not for long”. That is not a failure of the chapter. The questions do not grade. They locate. Each names the exact place where grip returns: the split, the strain, the smudge, the meddling, the pushing, the cleverness.","A reader can push back on the newborn. Infancy is not a state an adult can choose, and praising it can excuse a refusal to grow up. The book’s infant is narrower than that. It is undivided strength: a grip with no clench in it. The question asks for that quality and nothing else."]},"11":{"lead":"Chapter 11 is the book’s one worked proof: three made things, each of which works only because of the part that is missing.","ex":["三十輻共一轂: thirty spokes share one hub. The number is real. The old manual of crafts gives a wheel thirty spokes and says they figure the days of the month.","In 當其無, 當 is “right at” and 其無 is “its nothing”. The phrase gives the nothing an owner. This is not nothingness in general. It is the hub’s hole, where the axle goes; the pot’s hollow; the gap in the wall. A shaped absence.","埏埴 is to knead clay. 鑿 is to chisel: doors and windows are cut out of a wall, so the room is partly made by removal. Three trades stand behind the three examples: the wheelwright, the potter and the builder.","The conclusion sets two words against each other. 有之以為利: what is there gives 利, benefit, the thing that can be owned and sold. 無之以為用: what is not there gives 用, use, the working.","Wang Bi’s gloss is tight. What is there can benefit only by relying on what is not there for its use.","The title is a pun. 無用 means “the use of nothing”, and in ordinary Chinese it means “useless”. The Zhuangzi later builds a theme on the phrase: everyone knows the use of the useful, and no one knows the use of the useless."],"an":["The chapter is an induction: three cases and a 故, “so”. The globe’s four passages are exactly the three cases and the rule. Nothing else in the first half of the book argues this plainly.","The examples move outward from the body: a wheel that carries you, a pot you hold, a room that holds you. In each the maker works on the something, and the user uses the nothing.","The chapter corrects any reading that inflates 無 into a mystical void. The 無 of chapter 40, from which being is born, sounds cosmic. Here it is the space in a cup. The book wants both heard together.","It also gives 有 its due. The last line does not rank nothing above something. Without clay there is no hollow, and a hole needs its rim. This is chapter 2’s 有無相生, being and nothing giving birth to each other, shown in the workshop.","The English chooses essence over use: “The empty centre is what makes it a wheel.” The Chinese says less, and more exactly: at its nothing, there is the cart’s use. The translation saves “use” for the last line, where it lands: “what isn’t there gives the use”.","The globe applies the chapter to sound. When the reading reaches the character 無, no syllable is sung, and the low tone under the voice drops away for a moment. The word is performed as a gap."],"di":["The idea travelled. Frank Lloyd Wright said he found his own principle of architecture already stated here, by way of Okakura’s Book of Tea: the reality of a building is the space inside it.","It also drew fire. In the third century Pei Wei wrote an essay in praise of being, against readers who had made too much of nothing. His point is fair. The hollow of a pot is no use without the pot.","The chapter survives the objection because it never claimed otherwise. Its claim is narrower and harder to dodge: attention goes to what is there, and function comes from what is not. Makers, buyers and critics all look at the clay.","The application is wide: the rest in music, the margin on a page, the unscheduled hour, the thing not said. In each case the temptation is the same, to fill it. The chapter’s answer is that filling destroys the use."]},"12":{"lead":"Chapter 12 claims that the full range of a pleasure destroys the sense that enjoys it, and that the sage therefore sides with the belly against the eye.","ex":["The “fives” are sets, not samples. The five colours, five notes and five flavours are the complete palette, the complete scale and the complete table of a court. 五色 does not mean colour. It means every colour at once, arranged for display.","In 口爽, 爽 is not the modern “refreshing”. It means to go wrong, to be spoiled: a palate that can no longer taste. The globe’s “numb the tongue” is right.","馳騁畋獵 is galloping and hunting, the sport of the nobility. 心發狂: the heart breaks out in madness. 難得之貨, goods hard to get, comes back from chapter 3. 行妨: conduct is obstructed and goes crooked.","The received order is tidy: eye, ear, mouth, then heart, then conduct. Perception, mind, action. The silk copies have a rougher order: colours, hunting, rare goods, flavours, notes. Someone later gathered the three fives together.","為腹不為目: for the belly, not for the eye. Wang Bi’s gloss is the best line written on this chapter. Whoever is for the belly uses things to feed himself. Whoever is for the eye is put to work by things.","The silk copies read 是以聖人之治也: “so in the sage’s governing”. There the line is policy, the same policy as chapter 3’s full bellies. The received text dropped the phrase, and the line became personal ethics.","去彼取此, “lets go of that and takes this”, is a refrain. It closes chapters 38 and 72 as well. “That” is always the far and showy thing. “This” is what is at hand."],"an":["Two passages: five warnings and one choice. The warnings share a grammar, X 令人 Y, “X makes a person Y”. The person is passive in every line. Things act, and people are acted on.","The chapter follows from chapter 11. There a vessel is useful because it is empty. Here a sense is useless once it is full. A saturated eye is a filled cup.","Belly and eye differ in one decisive way. The belly has a stop signal. It fills, and it says so. The eye never reports that it has seen enough. To be “for the belly” is to side with the appetite that can be satisfied.","“Racing and hunting drive the heart wild” has the right speed. One detail of the globe meets this chapter head on. It sings every chapter on a five-note scale, the 五音 this line warns about. Its defence is in its design: one character at a time."],"di":["Is this a case against art? Mozi condemned music too, on grounds of cost. The Laozi’s ground is different and more interesting: perception itself. The trouble is not that colour is wicked or expensive. The full set, pressed on a person, blunts the organ.","Connoisseurship is the test case. A trained palate can taste more, and also less: the plain thing no longer registers. 口爽 names that loss exactly.","A reader can object that “belly, not eye” is a philistine’s motto: bread over beauty. The text supports a narrower reading. 目 is the organ of display and comparison, the one chapter 3 had in mind with “Don’t show off what stirs desire”. The choice is against seeing as wanting, not against seeing.","The practical question the chapter leaves is one of dose. It does not ask which pleasures are permitted. It asks at what quantity each one stops being perceived at all."]},"13":{"lead":"Chapter 13 says that being favoured is as unsettling as being disgraced, traces both to having a self at stake, and then says who can safely be given the world.","ex":["The chapter is a small commentary. It quotes two sayings, then explains each with 何謂, “what does it mean?”. The book is caught here in the act of glossing older proverbs.","驚 is written with the horse radical. It is the word for a horse taking fright. Favour and disgrace alike make a person shy like a horse.","The explanation runs 寵為下: favour is the low thing. Wang Bi, both silk copies and the Guodian slips agree. A late, smoothed text reads 寵為上，辱為下, favour high and disgrace low, and many popular editions follow it. Heshang Gong has 辱為下, disgrace is the low thing.","The older reading is the sharper one. To be favoured is to be someone’s dependant. Favour comes from above, so receiving it marks you as below, and you hold it in fear of losing it. That is why getting it startles as much as losing it.","貴大患若身: value great trouble as you value your self. 身 is body, person, and the self that has a standing to protect. The gloss is blunt. I have great trouble because I have a 身. Without one, what trouble could I have?","The final sentence is the hardest so far in the book. The globe reads it as care: “whoever values the world as their own body can be trusted with the world”. The silk copies add one word, 於, and reverse it: whoever values his own person above ruling the world can be entrusted with the world.","The Zhuangzi quotes the line in that comparative form, and tells stories to match. Sage kings offer the throne to men who decline it for the sake of their health, and those are the men judged fit to hold it.","寄 and 託 both mean to lodge something in another’s keeping. The world is deposited with a ruler, never owned. Neither character appears anywhere else in the book."],"an":["The globe’s four passages follow the logic: two sayings, first gloss, second gloss, application. The application is political, as so often. A chapter about private agitation ends by choosing a ruler.","The link between the two halves is dependence. Favour and disgrace both put a person’s balance in someone else’s hands. Having a 身 in the social sense, a position that can rise and fall, is what gives others that handle.","This is the third chapter on 身. In chapter 7 the sage puts it behind and outside, and so keeps it. In chapter 9 it steps back when the work is done. Here it is the root of trouble and also the thing a fit ruler prizes. The book does not despise the body. It distrusts the standing.","The English follows Wang Bi: “Favour puts you beneath someone.” The smoothed reading would give “Favour lifts you up, disgrace brings you down”, a commonplace. The older reading is the sharper one, and the chapter tightens around it."],"di":["Two ethics meet in the last sentence. On one, the person fit to rule loves the world as his own body. Later Confucians made a creed of that: the humane person forms one body with heaven, earth and all things.","On the other, the person fit to rule cares more for his own life than for the throne, and is safe for that reason. He will not spend lives, his own first, on glory. He is also immune to favour, because there is nothing he wants from above.","The older witnesses support the second reading. It is the less flattering one, and the more political: give power to those who do not need it.","The line about having no body is often taken as the chapter’s teaching. It is a diagnosis, not a prescription: “If I had no body, what trouble could I have?” But there is a body. The chapter refuses ambition, and it refuses escape. What is left is the plain work of valuing the right thing.","Its most usable insight is the first one. Praise is not the opposite of blame. Both startle, and for the same reason."]},"14":{"lead":"Chapter 14 puts the Way through every test an object could pass: sight, hearing, touch, top and bottom, front and back. It fails them all. Then the chapter says: hold it anyway.","ex":["Three senses try and fail. 視 is to look, 聽 to listen, 搏 to grasp. Each failure is given a name: 夷, 希, 微. 夷 is level, featureless. 希 is sparse, rare. 微 is minute.","The silk copies assign the names differently. The unseen is 微, minute, and the ungrasped is 夷, smooth. That fits better. What the eye misses is too small. What the hand misses offers no purchase.","不可致詰: they cannot be questioned to the bottom. So 混而為一, they run together as one. 混 is the word chapter 25 uses for the thing formed in confusion before heaven and earth.","其上不皦，其下不昧: its top is not bright, its bottom is not dark. Every object in the world is lit from above and shadowed below. This has no gradient, so it is not a body in space.","繩繩 is “cord upon cord”: continuous, unbroken. 復歸於無物, it returns to no-thing, is the first 復歸, “return”, in the book.","無狀之狀，無物之象: the shape of no shape, the image of no thing. Han Feizi explains 象 with an elephant, which is what the character depicts. Few people had seen a live one, so they pictured the animal from its bones. Whatever is imagined from traces, he says, is called 象.","惚恍, “elusive, blurred”, is named here and unfolded in chapter 21. The globe uses the same two English words in both places.","The last lines fork on one character. The globe has 執古之道, “hold to the ancient Way”. Both silk copies have 執今之道: hold to the Way of now, to steer what exists now, and so know the ancient beginning.","御 is to drive a chariot. 紀 is the leading thread of a skein, the end you pull to unwind the rest. “The thread of the Way” is exact."],"an":["The four passages test four kinds of presence. The senses: nothing. Position, high and low: nothing. Sequence, front and back: nothing. Then the turn: 執, hold. The chapter empties the object and keeps the handle.","The imagery of continuity is all fibre. Chapter 6 had 綿綿, silk floss. This chapter has 繩繩, cord, and 紀, thread. The Way has no substance in these images. It has only unbrokenness.","The two texts are each consistent. In Wang Bi’s text both chapters start from antiquity: here 執古之道, in chapter 21 自古及今. In the silk copies both start from now: 執今之道 and 自今及古. Modern editions that mix the two, as the first version of the globe did, end up facing both ways.","The English names are plain and well chosen: “invisible”, “faint”, “subtle”. “These three can’t be pinned down” is good for 不可致詰. “Steer” for 御 keeps the chariot."],"di":["Western readers projected onto this chapter early. In 1823 the French sinologist Abel-Rémusat argued that 夷希微, yi-xi-wei, spelled the Hebrew name of God. The idea is long dead. It is worth remembering whenever a capital letter creeps into a translation.","The real question is how anyone holds what cannot be seen, heard or touched. The chapter’s answer is in its last word. You cannot hold the thing. You can hold the thread: the continuity between what is here and where it came from.","Which end of the thread you hold is the difference between the two texts. The received reading gives authority to antiquity: take the old Way and apply it. The silk reading gives it to the present: attend to how things work now, and the beginning becomes knowable from here.","The second fits the book’s own method better. Chapter 21 will ask how the speaker knows the beginning of everything, and answer in two characters: 以此, by this."]},"15":{"lead":"Chapter 15 admits its subjects cannot be known and then paints them seven times. The portrait is of people who are careful first and open second.","ex":["The globe opens 古之善為士者, “those of old who were good at being 士”: men of service, officers. That is the reading of Wang Bi and the Guodian slips. Silk copy B and Fu Yi have 善為道者, those good at the Way. The oldest version describes the ideal officer, not a master of mysteries.","強為之容: I force a likeness of them. 強 is the same reluctant forcing as in chapter 25, where the Way gets a name only under compulsion. 容 is bearing, outward look. The globe’s “I can only try to describe them” keeps the modesty and drops the strain.","Seven similes follow. Their first two words, 豫 and 猶, together make the modern word for hesitation, 猶豫. By tradition both were names of wary animals.","冬涉川 is fording a river in winter, each step testing the ice. 畏四鄰 is fearing neighbours on all four sides. 儼兮其若容, in Wang Bi’s text, is grave, with a composed face; most other editions read 客, grave as a guest. Three pictures of vigilance.","Then 渙兮若冰之將釋, “like ice about to melt”, and the portrait turns. 樸, uncarved wood, appears here for the first time in the book. 谷 is chapter 6’s valley. 濁 is muddy water. Three pictures of blankness.","The two questions are statements in the silk copy, without 孰能, “who can”. Muddy: still it, and it slowly clears. At rest: move it, and it slowly comes alive. 徐, “slowly”, is the working word in both.","The last line exists in three versions. Wang Bi’s text, which the globe carries, reads 蔽不新成: worn, and not made new. The silk copy reads 敝而不成: worn, and not completed. A modern emendation, 蔽而新成, “wear out and be made new”, swaps one character to match chapter 22. Every early copy that survives at this point has a “not”."],"an":["The seven portraits are arranged, not listed. Three of caution, one hinge, three of openness: tense, melting, plain. The ice is the middle term. Vigilance in this chapter is not rigidity. It thaws.","None of the seven says what the old masters knew or taught. Every one describes how they carried themselves. The Way shows as bearing, 容, the very word chapter 21 will use: the look of great virtue follows the Way alone.","The two middle lines are a rhythm, not a recipe for calm. Stillness clears. Then movement brings to life. Clear and alive do not happen at once. A reader who keeps only the first line has half the instruction.","不欲盈, “doesn’t want to be full”, brings back the cup of chapters 4 and 9. And 濁, the muddy water, prepares chapter 20, where the speaker calls his own heart muddled.","“Loosening, like ice about to melt” is the best line of the chapter in either language. “Grave, with a composed face” follows Wang Bi’s 容; the common reading 客 would give “formal, like a guest”."],"di":["The first three images are the surprise. The masters look nervous. This is not the easygoing sage of the posters. Wisdom here is attention under risk: knowing the ice is thin, and walking.","Muddy is praise. Clarity in this chapter is not where anyone starts. It is what stillness produces, slowly. It is not the end either, because the next line stirs the water again.","The three endings make three different promises. The globe’s says you will be renewed. The received text says you can stay worn without needing renewal. The silk says you can stay worn and unfinished. The last is the hardest, and it has the oldest witness: a person who does not need to be complete.","Chapter 22 does promise renewal, “Wear out, and you are renewed”, so the globe’s reading is not foreign to the book. But here the witnesses agree against it. The chapter that paints careful people ends, in its early forms, by letting them stay incomplete."]},"16":{"lead":"Chapter 16 names the constant that chapter 1 left open: everything that rises goes back. Seeing that is called clarity, and missing it is called disaster.","ex":["致虛極，守靜篤: take emptiness to its limit, keep stillness in earnest. 虛 and 靜, empty and still, became the two words for meditation in every later Daoist school. The Guodian slips read the second phrase 守中, “keep to the centre”: the hollow of chapter 5.","吾以觀復: by this I watch the return. 觀 is the watching of chapter 1. 復, “return”, is also a hexagram in the Changes: one unbroken line coming back under five broken ones, the figure of midwinter. Its commentary says that in Return one sees the heart of heaven and earth.","芸芸 is a plant word: lush, teeming. 各復歸其根: each goes back to its root. The picture is a field in autumn. The idiom 落葉歸根, falling leaves return to the root, comes from here. It is said of people who come home at the end of a life spent elsewhere.","復命 has two senses, and the chapter uses both. 命 is the life allotted to a thing, so 復命 is returning to one’s lot. It is also the court term for reporting back after a mission. Each thing goes home and hands in its commission. The globe’s “returning to your nature” takes the first sense.","妄作凶: acting recklessly, misfortune. 凶 is the diviner’s verdict, “inauspicious”. The sentence has the clipped form of an oracle.","The second chain has a contested link. The globe reads 公乃王，王乃天: fair, then kingly, then heaven. That is Wang Bi’s text and both silk copies’. A twentieth-century conjecture changes 王 to 全, “whole”, and many modern editions print it.","沒身不殆, “to the end of one’s days, no danger”, returns in chapter 52."],"an":["The chapter is two chains. The first goes down: root, stillness, lot, constant, clarity. The second goes up: constant, capacity, fairness, kingship or wholeness, heaven, Way, lasting. Down to the root, then up to heaven. The argument has the shape of a plant.","常 is the hinge. It ends the first chain and starts the second. Chapter 1 said only that the spoken Way is not the constant one. Here the constant gets content: it is the return.","The “I” stands still in order to see. 萬物並作, the ten thousand things rise together, and the watcher does not rise with them. Stillness here is a vantage point. From inside the growth, the going back cannot be seen.","The globe’s three passages mark the practice, the first chain and the second chain. The English handles the chains well, keeping each link’s last word as the next link’s first.","The emendation changes the politics. With 王, the ladder runs through a throne: the capacious are fair, the fair are fit to be king, the king matches heaven. With 全 the king disappears, and the ladder becomes a path of personal wholeness. Modern editions that print 全 here often print 人 for 王 in chapter 25 too. The globe keeps Wang Bi’s king in both."],"di":["What returns? Nothing here suggests rebirth. The picture is seasonal: things come out, flourish and go back. The chapter calls this 常, the constant, and says that knowing it is clarity.","The ethical step is the interesting one: 知常容, whoever knows the constant has room. If everything is on its way back to the root, there is less to defend and less to win. Tolerance in this chapter is not a virtue to be practised. It is what an accurate view of time does to a person.","The reverse is also claimed. Not knowing the constant produces 妄作, reckless action. On this account the frantic striving the book keeps warning against has one source: a refusal to see that things end.","A reader may doubt the promise of safety. The book makes it often: no danger, no blame, long life. It is the Laozi’s characteristic wager, that harm comes mostly from overreach. The wager is strong as a rule of thumb and false as a guarantee.","復命 is the phrase to keep. It lets an ending be described without loss: not something taken away, but a commission returned."]},"17":{"lead":"Chapter 17 ranks rulers by how little they are noticed, and puts the beloved ruler second.","ex":["太上 is “the very highest”. The next phrase, 下知有之, “those below know only that he exists”, is the reading of Wang Bi, both silk copies and the Guodian slips. A later text, popular today, reads 不知有之: they do not know he is there.","The difference is one stroke and one degree. In the popular text the best ruler is invisible. In the old text he is known of, and no more. People are aware there is a government. They do not feel it.","Then three more grades. 親而譽之: they love and praise him. 畏之: they fear him. 侮之: they despise him. The loved and praised ruler is the Confucian ideal, and here he comes second.","信不足焉，有不信焉 comes back word for word at the end of chapter 23. 信 is both trust and trustworthiness, so the line cuts two ways. A ruler who does not trust is not trusted. A ruler whose word is not good is not believed. The globe takes the first: “Trust too little, and you won’t be trusted.”","悠兮, the globe’s “Easy, unhurried”, is 猷呵 in the silk copy and 猶乎 on the Guodian slips: the wary word from chapter 15’s portrait. In the old text the best ruler is hesitant, not relaxed. He hesitates over words because he values them: 貴言.","我自然: “we are so of ourselves”. This is the first appearance of 自然 in the book, and it is spoken by ordinary people about their own lives. The word modern Chinese uses for nature begins as a claim of authorship: this came from us."],"an":["The four grades read like a survey of political thought. The unnoticed ruler is this book’s. The loved one is the Confucians'. The feared one is the Legalists'. The despised one is everybody’s failure.","Why is love second? Chapter 2 has the answer: when everyone knows good as good, the not-good is already there. A ruler whose goodness is on display has made the people’s wellbeing depend on him. Praise is the sign of that dependence.","The middle line is the hinge, and the globe is right to set it alone. Non-interference is possible only for someone who trusts. Close management is distrust made into policy, and it is answered in kind.","On the Guodian slips this chapter and the next are one piece of text. Chapter 18 begins there, and in the silk copies, with 故, “therefore”. The division into two chapters hides an argument that runs straight through.","“We did it ourselves” is as good as English gets for 我自然. The Chinese is quieter. It is less a boast of achievement than a shrug: that is just how we are."],"di":["This is the most quoted chapter in books about leadership, usually in the late wording. Its use there is fair. Anyone who teaches knows the test: the lesson worked if the students think they found it themselves.","The chapter is harder than its popular form. It does not say the best leader is modest about credit. It says the people are correct. With the pressure off, they did do it themselves. The ruler’s achievement is an absence.","The objection is serious. Power that is not noticed is power that is not questioned. A government no one feels can do a great deal unobserved. The chapter trusts that what goes unseen is restraint. A modern reader is entitled to ask for evidence.","The text’s own safeguard is the word 貴言. The ruler it praises is not hiding. He is sparing: slow to speak, slow to order, and so with little to conceal."]},"18":{"lead":"Chapter 18 reads the Confucian virtues as symptoms: each one becomes visible only when the thing it repairs has already broken.","ex":["Four lines share one mould: when X, there is Y. Three of the Ys are prizes of the Confucian school. 仁義 is humaneness and rightness, the pair Mencius built on. 孝慈 is the devotion of children and the kindness of parents. 忠臣 are loyal ministers.","廢 is to fall into disuse, like a road no one keeps up. “Forgotten”, the globe’s word, is close. The great Way is not attacked. It is left unwalked.","The second line breaks the mould. In the other three a bad condition yields something admired. Here something admired, 智慧, intelligence, yields something bad: 大偽, “great pretending”. The Guodian slips do not have this line. The oldest version is three lines of one shape.","六親 are the six relations: father and son, elder and younger brother, husband and wife. The tradition’s model of filial devotion is Shun, whose father and brother tried to burn him on a roof and bury him in a well. His virtue is famous because his family was murderous.","The same holds for loyal ministers. The canonical one is Bi Gan, who reproached a tyrant and had his heart cut out. No one is remembered for loyalty to a sound king.","The silk and bamboo copies put a word in each line that the received text lacks: 安, or 案. Most scholars read it as “then”. A minority read it as “how”: when the great Way is abandoned, how can there be humaneness? That would turn the chapter from an attack on the virtues into a defence of their source.","The silk copies also call the ministers 貞臣, “steadfast”, where the received text has 忠, “loyal”."],"an":["The chapter is chapter 2 applied to ethics. There, knowing good as good already implied the not-good. Here each named virtue implies the failure that called it out. Virtue with a name is evidence.","It prepares chapter 38, which stretches the same thought into a ladder: lose the Way and there is virtue, lose virtue and there is humaneness, then rightness, then ritual.","The 故, “therefore”, that opens the chapter in the old copies ties it to chapter 17. The best ruler leaves people saying “We did it ourselves”. Therefore, when that Way lapses, the virtues have to be named, taught and praised. Chapter 18 describes the world of chapter 17’s second-best ruler.","The globe keeps one passage, which suits four parallel lines. “Great pretending” for 大偽 is exact. “Kindness and justice” for 仁義 matches “not kind” in chapter 5 and keeps the translation consistent, at the price of the school’s name."],"di":["The chapter does not say kindness is bad. It says that a society which talks about kindness has a problem kindness is being asked to fix. In a family that works, no one is pointed out as the dutiful child.","Confucians answered sharply. Han Yu, in the ninth century, said Laozi belittled humaneness and rightness because his view was small: a man in a well, calling the sky small.","Han Yu has a point the chapter should grant. Bad times are the normal times, and in them the virtues are needed. Calling the medicine a symptom does not cure anyone.","The chapter has a point Han Yu should grant too. The medicine can become the diet. A culture can grow so proud of its loyal ministers and devoted children that it stops asking why it keeps needing them.","The next chapter draws the practical conclusion: drop the slogans, and people return to devotion and kindness on their own. What the book wants is the thing without its label."]},"19":{"lead":"Chapter 19 tells the reader to give up three things everyone admires, admits that this is not enough, and then offers four small positives. In its oldest form it attacks different things.","ex":["Three lines share a frame: 絕 X 棄 Y, cut off X and discard Y, then a benefit. The pairs are 聖智, sagehood and cleverness; 仁義, humaneness and rightness; 巧利, skill and profit.","絕聖 is the shock. 聖 is the first half of 聖人, the sage, the book’s hero in two dozen chapters. The received text tells the reader to cut off the very quality its ideal person is named for.","The Guodian slips open with this chapter, and there it reads differently. The three targets are 智辯, knowledge and disputation; 巧利, skill and profit; and 偽慮, artifice and scheming. Sagehood, humaneness and rightness are not attacked at all.","By the silk copies, a century or so later, the text already has 絕聖 and 絕仁棄義. The assault on the Confucian virtues is a later hardening of a chapter that began as an attack on cleverness and rhetoric.","In 此三者以為文不足, the silk copies say 此三言, “these three sayings”. The line is the text commenting on itself: as 文, as written formula, these three are not enough. So something is added for them to hang on, 令有所屬.","In 見素抱樸, 素 is undyed silk and 樸 is uncarved wood: the two raw materials before the dyer and the carpenter get to them. Modern Chinese joins them into one word for plainness, 樸素. 少私寡欲 is less private interest, fewer desires.","The last phrase is shared with an unexpected ally. Mencius says nothing nourishes the heart better than making the desires few, in the same two characters, 寡欲."],"an":["The globe’s two passages are the two halves of the argument: what to remove, and what to hold. The hinge between them is an admission of insufficiency, which is rare in any scripture.","The chapter finishes the thought of chapter 18. There the virtues were symptoms. Here the cure is to stop promoting them, and the thing itself comes back: 民復孝慈, people return to devotion and kindness. The family feeling is affirmed. Only the slogan is cut.","利 is used in both directions. 民利百倍: the people benefit a hundredfold. 絕巧棄利: discard profit. The word is good as what people receive and bad as what they chase.","The positives are comparatives. Not no self and no desire: less, and fewer. After three absolute “cut off” lines, the programme that follows is modest and practical.","“Be plain as raw silk. Hold to the uncarved wood. Less self. Fewer wants.” is the translation at its most compressed, and it matches the Chinese beat for beat. “Give up sagehood” keeps the scandal in view: the word cut off is the sage’s own."],"di":["Which chapter 19 is the real one? The question has no clean answer. The book grew in layers, and the globe carries the later layer. A reader should at least know that the line against humaneness and rightness is not in the earliest copy.","The Guodian target is worth taking seriously on its own. 辯, disputation, was a profession in the Warring States: men who argued for a living, for any side. “Give up cleverness and debate” is a different programme from “give up kindness”, and an easier one to defend.","The received version has its own force. It refuses to exempt its own ideal. If every promoted virtue casts a shadow, promoted sagehood casts one too. A book that praises the sage and says “cut off sagehood” is at least consistent about advertisements.","The promise of a hundredfold benefit deserves scepticism. Societies that have tried giving up learning have not prospered. What survives scrutiny is the last line, which promises nothing: plainer, simpler, less, fewer."]},"20":{"lead":"Chapter 20 is the book’s one sustained self-portrait: a speaker alone at everyone else’s feast, calling himself a fool, with a single affirmation held back to the last four characters.","ex":["絕學無憂, “give up learning, and your worries end”, may not belong here. Many editors attach it to the end of chapter 19, as a fourth 絕. On the Guodian slips it stands in another bundle altogether, after the lines of chapter 48 about learning that adds daily.","唯 is the respectful “yes”. 阿 is a careless or fawning one. One silk copy writes 訶, an angry shout, which changes the pair: between a meek yes and a bark of rebuke, how far is it?","The older copies also read 美與惡, beautiful and ugly, where the globe has 善之與惡, good and evil. That is chapter 2’s pair, and probably the original.","In 荒兮其未央哉, 未央 is “not yet at its midpoint”, so “endless”. The Odes use it of a night that will not finish. The line is a cry, and the self-portrait starts from it.","太牢 is the great sacrifice: ox, sheep and pig together, the grandest meal the rites allowed. In 未兆, 兆 is the crack in a heated oracle bone, the sign a diviner reads. The speaker is a bone not yet cracked. “Giving no sign” is right, and loses the bone.","In 未孩, 孩 is the old form of 咳, a baby’s laugh, and the silk copy writes 咳. 儽儽 is drooping, worn out; the silk has 纍, weary and bound. The globe’s “Drifting” makes the speaker freer than he is. He is tired.","沌沌 is the word for primal chaos, 混沌. 悶悶, “dull”, is chapter 58’s word for good government, and 察察, “sharp”, is its word for bad. The speaker describes himself in the crowd’s insults, and the insults are the book’s praise.","The sea and wind lines differ in the silk copies: 忽呵其若海，恍呵若無所止. Vague as the sea, blurred as if with nowhere to stop. 忽 and 恍 are the two words the next chapter uses for the Way itself.","The last line has one more word in both silk copies: 吾欲獨異於人, “I want to be different from others”. The difference is chosen.","食母 is a nursing mother; elsewhere it is the term for a wet nurse. The Daozang text spells the sense out: 貴求食於母, I prize seeking food from the mother."],"an":["The grammar is a drumbeat. 我獨, “I alone”, comes six times. 眾人 and 俗人, everyone and ordinary people, come five. Every statement about the speaker is made against a statement about them.","The frame is two meals. The crowd is at the great sacrifice. The speaker is at the breast. The unsmiling infant of the second passage is not a passing simile. It sets up the last word of the chapter.","無所歸, nowhere to return, lands hard in a book whose central motion is return. Four chapters ago everything went back to its root. This speaker has no home among people, because his return address is not among people.","The voice is the voice of Chu song, with 兮 at every breath. The same grammar appears in the Songs of Chu, in the mouth of the exile Qu Yuan: the whole world is muddy and I alone am clean, everyone is drunk and I alone am sober.","The Laozi’s speaker says the reverse. Everyone is bright, and I alone am dim. Qu Yuan’s claim to be the one clean man ends in the river. In that poem a fisherman answers him: when the water is muddy, wash your feet in it. Chapter 20 is on the fisherman’s side. Its speaker claims the mud.","Of some hundred and thirty characters, the affirmation is four: 貴食母. The English gives it the last line and the plainest words: “I treasure being fed by the mother.”"],"di":["This is the most seductive mirror in the book. Anyone who feels out of step can read 我獨異於人 and hear their loneliness promoted to depth.","The text has four checks against that. The self-description is unflattering: fool, dim, dull, stubborn, crude. The crowd is not despised; their feast is real and they enjoy it. The silk’s “I want” makes the difference a choice with a price, not a fate. And the difference is a diet, not a rank. He is not better. He is fed elsewhere.","The chapter also does not pretend the price away. 儽儽, 若遺, 無所歸: worn out, left behind, nowhere to go back to. These are not the words of someone above it all. The book lets its sage be lonely, and says so in his own voice.","What it will not do is leave him there. Being fed is half of a movement. The book’s last chapter supplies the other half: the sage does not hoard, and the more he gives the more he has. Chapter 20 without that ending is a mood. With it, the solitude is where the giving is supplied from."]},"21":{"lead":"Chapter 21 goes inside the blur that chapter 14 described from outside, and reports that it gets firmer the further in one goes. Then it answers the question of how this is known by pointing.","ex":["In 孔德之容, 孔 is “great” for Heshang Gong and “hollow” for Wang Bi. 容 is bearing, the outward look, the word chapter 15 used for its portraits. The line says the look of great virtue follows the Way and nothing else.","The English keeps 容: “The look of great virtue / follows the Way alone.” Virtue is being described by its appearance.","道之為物, “the Way, as a thing”, is a surprising phrase. For the length of a sentence the Way is called a 物, a thing among things.","恍惚 is flickering, vague; in modern Chinese it means dazed. The chapter says it three times and turns it over each time: 惟恍惟惚, 惚兮恍兮, 恍兮惚兮. The word order will not hold still. The English keeps the flip.","Four things are found 其中, “inside it”. 象, an image. 物, a thing. 精, an essence: the character is written with the rice radical and means the polished grain, the seed, the vital part. And 信.","信 is nearer “proof” than “trust”. It is the word for a token that verifies a message, something that can be checked and relied on. The English says “there is something sure in it”.","Wang Bi’s text, which the globe carries, reads 自古及今, “from ancient times until now”, and his commentary quotes it that way. The silk copies and Fu Yi read 自今及古, from now back to antiquity. The silk order rhymes: 古, 去 and 甫 chimed in the old pronunciation, and 今 does not.","甫 in 眾甫 is read as 父, and the silk copies write it so: “the fathers of the multitude”. Wang Bi glosses it as the beginning of things, which the globe follows. 閱 is to review, as a commander reviews troops. The silk has 順, to go along with.","以此, “by this”, answers the same question in chapters 54 and 57."],"an":["The middle of the chapter is a ladder: image, thing, essence, real, reliable. Each rung is more solid than the last. Ordinary knowing runs the other way: what is clear is trusted, and what is vague is doubted. Here the vague thing firms up as one goes in.","其中, “inside it”, is said four times, and it is the phrase that separates this chapter from chapter 14. There the Way was tested from outside and gave nothing: no sight, no sound, no grip. Here the report comes from within.","The chapter is also the answer to chapter 20. There the speaker was dim, dull and, in the silk text, 忽 and 恍: vague and blurred. Here the Way is 恍 and 惚. The observer has the qualities of the thing observed. A blurred object needs a blurred instrument, and the bright, sharp people of chapter 20 do not have one.","Chapter 20 ended on the mother. This one ends on the fathers. It is the only place in the book where the origin is plural and male.","The globe’s two passages divide the chapter where it turns from description to the question of knowledge: what is in it, and how I know."],"di":["以此 is one of the great evasions or one of the great answers, depending on the reader. Asked for his method, the speaker points. “This” can be the Way just described. It can be the present moment, since the knowing starts from now. It can be the practice of chapter 16: sit still and watch.","A sceptic is entitled to say that “there is proof in it” is not a proof. That is correct. The chapter is testimony. Its claim is conditional: go in, and it firms up. That can be tested only by going in, which is either a weakness in the argument or the nature of this kind of knowledge.","The line about the name deserves a second look. Chapter 1 doubted names, and chapter 25 will say the speaker does not know this thing’s name. Yet here “its name has never left”. What has persisted is not a correct label. It is the fact that people in every age have had to call it something.","The chapter’s working advice is in its direction of travel. It does not say to wait for clarity before trusting. It says the image comes first, then the thing, then the seed, and only then the proof."]},"22":{"lead":"Chapter 22 expounds an old proverb, “bend and stay whole”, and applies it to the four ways a person pushes himself forward.","ex":["Six pairs are joined by 則, “then”. Bent, then whole. Crooked, then straight. Hollow, then full. Worn, then new. Little, then gain. Much, then confusion. Five move from a low state to a high one. The sixth runs the other way and is the warning: 多則惑.","曲則全 is quoted at the end as what the ancients said. As in chapter 13, the chapter is a commentary on a saying. The saying lives on in the idiom 委曲求全, to bend for the sake of keeping things whole.","枉 is crooked, and also wronged. 窪 is a hollow in the ground, where water collects. 敝則新, worn then new, is the line the globe’s chapter 15 was emended to match.","抱一為天下式: holding to the one, the sage becomes the world’s 式, its model. The silk copies read 執一以為天下牧: grasping the one, he becomes the world’s shepherd.","Four lines follow on the pattern 不自 X 故 Y. Not showing himself, he is seen. Not asserting himself, he is evident. Not boasting, he has merit. Not proud, he lasts. The same four vices return as plain negatives in chapter 24.","Here the globe is missing a sentence, in both languages: 夫唯不爭，故天下莫能與之爭. Only because he does not contend, no one in the world can contend with him. The received text has it, and so do the silk copies. It is the summary the four lines were building to.","誠全而歸之 can be read two ways. “Be truly whole, and everything comes back to you”, as the globe has it. Or: he is truly kept whole, and returns whole. The second echoes a saying in the Record of Rites: parents bear a child whole, and the child should return himself whole."],"an":["The chapter rests on a principle stated outright only in chapter 40: reversal is how the Way moves. If things turn into their opposites, the low position is the one with a future.","In the silk copies chapter 24 stands directly before this one. Read in that order the pair is a diptych: first the four self-assertions and their failure, then the four restraints and their success. The received order puts chapter 23 between them and hides the hinge.","The title comes from the vocabulary of the Changes. 謙, modesty, is a hexagram, and the old Documents have the matching proverb: fullness invites loss, modesty receives gain.","The English is terse in the right places. “Have much, and you get lost” is better than most versions of 多則惑. The missing sentence is the one repair the chapter needs, and it would go just before the old saying."],"di":["The danger of the chapter is the word 枉. “Be wronged, and you will be set straight” is a cruel thing to say to someone who is being wronged. 委曲求全 in modern use often means exactly that: swallow it, keep the peace.","The four middle lines show who is being addressed. Showing off, self-righteousness, boasting and pride are the temptations of people with standing. The bending asked for here is the bending of the strong: not insisting on one’s dignity.","Is the promise true? Do the unassertive get the credit? Not reliably. What the chapter can defend is the cost side. Self-assertion invites contention, and contention is expensive even when it is won.","The last line keeps the chapter honest with one word, 誠: truly. Bending as a trick for getting ahead is not what the proverb describes. It has to be real to work, which means it cannot be done for the sake of working."]},"23":{"lead":"Chapter 23 argues from weather. Violence does not last even when heaven and earth do it, so a person should attend to what they practise, because they will become it.","ex":["希言自然 is four characters. 希 is “rare”, the word chapter 14 gave to what cannot be heard. 言 here is probably not conversation but orders: decrees, pronouncements. To speak rarely is 自然, so of itself.","飄風 is a gale and 驟雨 a cloudburst; the silk copies have 暴雨, a violent rain. Neither lasts a morning or a day. Who does this? Heaven and earth. And if even they cannot keep it up, how could a person?","從事 means to make something one’s occupation. It is still the ordinary word for what a person does for a living. 同於 is to become the same as. So: whoever makes the Way their work becomes one with the Way.","失 is loss, and more exactly losing it: in chapter 38 it is the verb for falling away from the Way. 德 beside it is a pun. The standard old gloss is 德者得也, virtue is getting. So the pair 德 and 失 is also gain and loss.","The silk copies bring that out, and they are much shorter. They have no line about the Way here and no “glad”. They read: one with gain, the Way gains him; one with loss, the Way loses him.","The received text adds 樂, “glad”, three times, and so produces the line the globe translates: “Become one with loss, and loss is glad to have you.” The last sentence, about trust, is also absent from the silk at this point. It seems to have been copied in from chapter 17."],"an":["The argument has two 故, two “so”. Storms are brief, so forcing is brief. Forcing is brief, so attend to what you make a practice of. The globe’s passage break falls at the second, where the subject changes from weather to people.","The middle section states a law of assimilation in three steps: practice, identity, reception. What you work at, you become. What you become takes you in. It is stated for the Way and for loss in the same grammar, with no change of tone.","Chapter 7 said heaven is long-lasting and earth endures. This chapter says heaven and earth cannot make a gale last. The two fit. What they do without forcing goes on. What they force is over by noon.","“To say little is what is so by itself” keeps the globe’s formula for 自然, the same words it uses in chapter 25. “A whirlwind doesn’t last the morning. A downpour doesn’t last the day.” could not be plainer or better."],"di":["“Loss is glad to have you” is the line people stop at, and it reads two ways.","As a warning, it is dry and exact. Make failure your occupation, and failure will make room for you. 從事 is the operative word: not suffering a loss, but working at it, daily, as one’s trade.","As a consolation, it leans on the storm. Gales pass. A person inside a loss who stops fighting it is, for that time, at one with it, and is received. The line can be read as permission to be where one is.","The two readings are not rivals, because the chapter has already drawn the line between them. A loss is weather. It arrives, it is violent, and it does not last the day. Loss as a practice is something else. It does not pass, because it is being kept up. The same sentence comforts the first and warns the second.","The tender reading depends on the later wording. The oldest text is flat bookkeeping: lose the Way, and the Way loses you. It is worth knowing that the warmth was added, and worth asking whether the people who added it had understood something."]},"24":{"lead":"Chapter 24 is chapter 22 in the negative: six ways of overreaching, each of which defeats itself, and two images of how they look from the Way’s side.","ex":["企 is to stand on tiptoe. The character survives in the modern words for planning and for enterprise, 企圖 and 企業: straining upward to see further. 跨 is to take too long a stride. One posture cannot be stood in, and the other cannot be walked in for long.","The silk copies have only the first image, written 炊者不立. They lack 跨者不行.","The four 自 lines mirror chapter 22 exactly. Showing oneself, 自見. Affirming oneself, 自是. Boasting, 自伐. Pride, 自矜. Each loses the very thing it reaches for: being seen, being acknowledged, merit, lasting.","餘食贅行 is leftover food and a 贅, a wen or swelling. 行 is read as 形, the body: “a growth on the body”, as the globe has it. They are two images of excess. One is too much taken in. The other is too much grown out.","物或惡之: things, perhaps, detest them. 或 is the hedge of chapter 4. 處, to stay in, is the dwelling word of chapters 2 and 8. The whole sentence returns at the start of chapter 31, where the thing detested is weapons."],"an":["The chapter thinks with the body. Tiptoe and overstride are postures a body can take and cannot hold. The four social vices are presented as the same kind of thing: positions that exceed a natural stance.","The disgust images continue the thought. A body knows leftovers and swellings as too much. The chapter asks the reader to feel self-display the same way.","In the silk copies this chapter comes directly before chapter 22, and the two make one argument: the four failures, then the four successes. The received order separates them with the chapter on storms. That placement has its own logic. Tiptoe is to standing what a gale is to weather.","The globe’s title follows the Daozang edition, which prints 若思. Most editions of Heshang Gong’s titles have 苦恩 here, “bitter favour”; the two differ by a few strokes in each character. The heading above translates the usual form.","The English is brisk: “On tiptoe, you can’t stand.” It also keeps 或 as “seem”: “Things seem to hate them”."],"di":["The obvious objection is that self-display works. People who put themselves forward are noticed, and in an economy of attention they are paid for it.","The chapter’s verbs answer with duration, not with denial. 不立, 不行, 不長: cannot stand, cannot walk, does not last. A person can go on tiptoe. The claim is that nobody lives there.","企 is the more interesting word for a modern reader, because it has become the word for enterprise. The posture the chapter warns against is the one a whole vocabulary of ambition is built on: reaching, stretching, getting ahead of one’s feet.","The chapter does not say to stand lower than one is. It says to stand on the whole foot."]},"25":{"lead":"Chapter 25 is the book’s fullest statement of what the Way is: something complete before heaven and earth, named only under pressure, whose greatness is a going out and a coming back.","ex":["有物混成: there is a thing, formed in confusion. 混 is the blending of chapter 14, where three unknowables ran together into one. The Guodian slips read 狀, “shape”, where the received text has 物, “thing”.","寂兮寥兮 is soundless and formless. 獨立不改: it stands alone and does not alter. The next phrase, 周行而不殆, “going round and never tiring”, is missing from the Guodian slips and from both silk copies. It was added later, and it sits a little oddly beside “stands alone”.","In 吾不知其名，字之曰道, two words for “name” are in play. 名 is the given name, used by parents and superiors. 字 is the courtesy name, used by everyone else out of respect. The speaker does not know this thing’s real name. “Way” is the polite form of address.","強為之名曰大: forced to give it a name, I say “great”. 強 is the strain of chapter 15’s portrait. Then a chain: 大曰逝，逝曰遠，遠曰反. Great means going on. Going on means far. Far means coming back. 逝 is the word Confucius used at the river for everything that passes.","The four greats are where modern editions part from the oldest witness. Wang Bi’s text, which the globe carries, the Daozang plain text and the Guodian slips all read 王亦大: the king is also great, and the king holds one of the four places. Fu Yi and both silk copies have 人亦大, “people are great too”, and silk copy A makes people one of the four as well.","The 人 reading has real support. Both silk copies have it, and so does Fu Yi’s “ancient text”, and modern editors like it because the next line begins 人法地. The Guodian slips, older than either, have 王, and so does Wang Bi. The globe follows Wang Bi.","法 is to take as one’s model or law. People model themselves on earth, earth on heaven, heaven on the Way, and the Way on 自然. 自然 is not a fifth thing above the Way. It means “so of itself”. Heshang Gong says it flatly: the Way’s nature is to be so of itself, and it has nothing to model."],"an":["The globe’s three passages are a cosmogony, a ranking and a chain. Each has its own grammar: description, list, and the linked form of chapter 16, where each link’s end is the next link’s start.","Greatness here is a route. Out, far, back. 反, return, is the word chapter 40 will make the Way’s own motion. The largest thing in the book is defined by the fact that it comes home.","The chain climbs and then closes. Person, earth, heaven, Way: each looks up to the next. The top looks to nothing outside itself. And 自然 has appeared once before, in chapter 17, in the mouths of ordinary people: we are so of ourselves. The Way and the villagers share one predicate.","The naming scene completes chapter 1. There the nameable name was not the constant one. Here the speaker shows how the name was given: reluctantly, as a courtesy, with a second name forced out after it.","The globe’s title, 名象, is again the Daozang’s. The usual one is 象元, “imaging the origin”. The English handles the hardest line well: “The Way follows what is so by itself” avoids the word “nature” and the mistake that comes with it. “I call it the Way” cannot carry 字, and nothing in English could."],"di":["With 人, the chapter says humanity is one of the four great things in the world. It is a fine sentence, and the silk copies already had it. With 王, it says the king is. That is the reading of the oldest copy, the Guodian slips, and of Wang Bi, in a book addressed to rulers.","The two can be reconciled without pretending. The king is great as the one human being whose task is to model himself on earth and heaven on everyone’s behalf. The oldest dictionary explains the character 王 itself that way: three lines for heaven, earth and humanity, and one stroke joining them.","道法自然 is often translated “the Way follows nature” and enlisted for ecology. The sentiment is harmless and the reading is wrong. 自然 became the noun for the natural world only in modern times. The line says the ultimate thing has no law outside itself.","The order of the chain is the practical point. It does not tell a person to be so of themselves. It tells them to model themselves on earth, which is low, steady and bears everything. “Be natural” skips three steps."]},"26":{"lead":"Chapter 26 makes the case for ballast. The heavy anchors the light, the still governs the restless, and a ruler who forgets this loses both.","ex":["Two axioms open the chapter. 重為輕根: the heavy is the root of the light. 靜為躁君: the still is lord of the restless. 躁 is haste, agitation, the fidget of someone who cannot wait.","聖人, the sage, is Wang Bi’s reading and the globe’s. The silk copies have 君子, the noble person: a Confucian’s word, which appears in only one other chapter of the book.","輜重 is the baggage train: the covered wagons that carry an army’s food and gear. Its second character is the “heavy” of the first line, so the phrase is a pun. The traveller goes all day and does not leave the heavy things. Sunzi gives the military fact: an army without its baggage train is lost.","榮觀 is a splendid sight, or a fine lodge, since 觀 can be a tower. 燕處超然: he sits at ease and stays above it. The last two characters became an idiom for detachment.","以身輕天下 is the crux. The globe takes it as conduct: to “behave lightly before the world”. The silk copies add 於 and make it a comparison: to hold one’s own person lighter than the world.","The last line varies. Wang Bi and the silk have 輕則失本, lose the base. Some modern editions print 失根, lose the root, which closes the ring with the first line. The Daozang plain text has 失臣, lose one’s ministers."],"an":["The globe keeps the chapter as one passage, and its shape is a ring: root and lord at the start, root and lord at the end. Between them stand one image and one question.","The earliest reader on record took it as hard politics. Han Feizi defines the terms: keeping control in one’s own hands is weight, and not leaving one’s position is stillness. His example is a king of Zhao who gave up his throne while alive, lost his power with it, and starved in a besieged palace. For Han Feizi the baggage is the state.","Weight looks like an odd virtue in a book that praises the soft and the weak. It is the same virtue seen from below. Water is soft, and it is heavy: it seeks the low place and stays. A root is not hard. It is what does not move.","The silk reading of the question joins this chapter to chapter 13. There the person fit to be given the world valued his own person above ruling it. Here the ruler to fear is the one who holds his person cheaper than the world. He will spend it, and other people’s with it.","“Heavy is the root of light. Still is the master of restless.” The English matches the Chinese for bluntness. Using the adjectives as nouns is exactly what the original does."],"di":["The traveller is the important figure, because he is moving. The chapter does not praise immobility. He travels all day. What he does not do is get ahead of his supplies.","A modern reader hears constant praise of the opposite: travel light, move fast, stay flexible. The chapter’s reply is logistical. Lightness is a fine thing for a day. Over a campaign, whoever outruns the wagons starves.","What counts as baggage is left open, and that is the useful question. It is whatever is heavy, slow, unglamorous and necessary: the things one would rather not carry and cannot do without.","The splendid views are the test. The chapter does not say the traveller refuses to look. He is at ease among them. He simply does not leave the cart."]},"27":{"lead":"Chapter 27 describes skill that leaves nothing behind: no tracks, no tools, and in the end no discarded people.","ex":["Five crafts are each perfected to the point where their usual equipment disappears. Good travelling leaves no ruts or footprints. Good speech has no flaw to be picked at; 瑕 is a flaw in jade. Good reckoning uses no 籌策, the bamboo rods that were the calculator of the age.","Good shutting uses no bolt, and cannot be opened. Good tying uses no cord, and cannot be undone. These two go past skill into paradox: the best fastening has nothing to unfasten.","善 changes meaning as the chapter goes. In the first five lines it means “good at”. By the third passage 善人 is “a good person”. Chinese does not distinguish the two, and the chapter uses that. Goodness is presented as a kind of skill.","無棄人, 無棄物: no person is thrown away, no thing is thrown away. The silk copies phrase the second half more concretely: among things, no goods are wasted.","襲明 is hard. 襲 is to put one garment over another, and so to inherit, to carry on, to cover. “Following the light” is the globe’s choice. “Light worn under a cloak” would be another. The silk copies have 曳明 and 申明: light drawn out, light extended.","資 is stock, capital, what one draws on. The not-good person is the good person’s 資. The globe’s “material” is fair. So is “resource”. The good person teaches the one who is not, and has that person to work with.","The silk copy reads the first half differently: the good person is the teacher of the good person. On that reading the good learn from each other and use the not-good as their material. That is close to a line in the Analects: among any three people walking, one will be my teacher, by good example or by bad."],"an":["The globe’s three passages are craft, care and teaching. The link is waste. The perfect craftsman wastes no motion and leaves no scrap. The sage, in the same way, leaves no one over.","This is chapter 5 from the other side. There heaven, earth and the sage had no favourites. Here the sage has no rejects. Impartiality in this book runs in both directions.","It also extends chapter 17. The ruler no one notices and the traveller who leaves no tracks are one figure. The mark of the best work is that it cannot be traced to the worker.","The English turns activities into people: “A good traveller leaves no tracks”, where the Chinese has “good travelling”. The gain is warmth. “No one is thrown away” is the strongest line in the chapter and needed no help."],"di":["“No one is thrown away” is a large claim, and the chapter attaches a condition that is easy to miss. It is the sage’s skill that leaves no rejects. The text does not say everyone is easy. It says that a person written off is a failure of craft on someone else’s part.","The teacher and the material need each other, and the chapter insists on both halves. Not valuing the teacher is one error. Not caring for the material is the other. A teacher has nothing to work on without the student who has not got it yet.","“Material” has an edge. It can sound as if the not-good exist for the good person’s use. The line that follows guards against that: 愛其資, love your material. And chapter 62 will ask outright what reason there could be to discard a person for not being good.","The tracelessness has the same shadow as in chapter 17. Work that leaves no mark cannot be checked. The chapter’s answer is in its examples: the door holds, and the knot holds. The test of invisible skill is whether the thing stays shut."]},"28":{"lead":"Chapter 28 says three times, in one mould, to know the strong side and stay on the weak one. Then it explains where tools and officials come from.","ex":["Each of the three stanzas has the same four steps. 知其 X, know the X. 守其 Y, keep to the Y. 為天下 Z, be the world’s Z. Then 復歸, return, to a fourth thing. The pairs are male and female, white and black, glory and disgrace.","知 and 守 are not rivals. The line does not say to be ignorant of the male. It says to know it and take the other position. 雄 and 雌 are the words for cock and hen, as 牝 in chapter 6 was the word for a mare.","谿 is a ravine, the bed a stream cuts. 谷 is the valley of chapter 6. Both are low places that collect what runs down. 式, model, sits between them.","The three returns are to 嬰兒, the newborn; 無極, the limitless; and 樸, the uncarved wood. 無極 occurs nowhere else in the book. It had a long future: the Song philosopher Zhou Dunyi opened his account of the cosmos with it.","The middle stanza is probably an insertion. The Zhuangzi quotes this passage with only two pairs: know the male and keep the female, know the white and keep the 辱. 辱 there means soiled, the opposite of white, as in chapter 41’s “great whiteness seems soiled”.","The silk copies stand halfway. They have three stanzas, but the pairs are male and female, white and soiled, white and black. There is no 榮, “glory”, in them at all. The received text turned the second 白 into 榮 and moved the wood to the end, where it could lead into the next line.","樸散則為器: when the wood is split up it becomes 器, vessels, tools. 器 is also the word for a person with one use. Confucius says the noble person is not a 器. Here the tools are what is left when the whole has been cut.","聖人用之，則為官長 is read two ways. The sage uses the tools and makes them heads of office, as the globe has it. Or the sage, by using the uncarved itself, becomes the head of the officials. Heshang Gong takes the second.","In 大制不割, 制 is to cut cloth to a pattern, and so to institute, to rule. 割 is to hack apart. The great tailoring does not sever. The globe’s “the best carving doesn’t cut” keeps the pun with the wood."],"an":["The stanzas are built by chaining. Each ends its first sentence on a phrase and begins the next with the same phrase: 為天下谿。為天下谿. The effect is a chant, and the globe sets each stanza as its own passage.","In the received order the three returns widen and then land. The newborn is a person. The limitless is the cosmos. The uncarved wood is material, and the coda picks it up at once and carries it into politics.","The coda is the book’s theory of specialisation. A society needs tools, so the wood is cut. The sage is the one who presides over tools without having become one. The best order is the one that shapes with the least severing.","The female is again a position. The stanza is addressed to someone who has the male role available and is told not to take it. This is the fullest statement of what chapters 6 and 10 began.","The English keeps the hard word. 守其辱 is “keep to disgrace”: 辱 is the word of chapter 13. A softer translation would say “the low place”, and lose the sting."],"di":["This is not a chapter about innocence. Each stanza begins with knowing. The restraint it describes is available only to someone who could do otherwise. A person with no power who “keeps to the female” is not practising this. They are being described by someone else.","守其辱 asks for more than modesty. It asks a person to remain where they are thought little of. Chapter 78 will say the same of rulers: whoever takes on the country’s filth is its true lord. That is a strong claim, and an easy one to abuse when it is preached downward.","Calligraphers took the middle stanza as a rule of composition. Count the white as black: the blank paper is part of the character. Whatever its origin in the text, the stanza has been useful to people who work with empty space.","大制不割 is the line for anyone who designs institutions. It concedes that cutting is necessary, since there will be tools and offices. It asks that the cut not sever people from the whole they were cut from. The test of a system is how much of the wood still shows in the tool."]},"29":{"lead":"Chapter 29 says the world cannot be taken and remade, because it is not raw material. It is a sacred vessel, and it moves on its own.","ex":["取天下而為之: to take all under heaven and work on it. 取天下 is the era’s phrase for winning the realm. 為之 is to do something to it, to make it over.","不得已 here means “will not succeed”: 不得, not get it, with a closing particle. The same three characters mean “having no choice” in the next two chapters. The globe translates each one correctly.","神器 is a spirit vessel. The model is the ritual bronze: the great tripods whose possession marked the rightful king. Such a thing is already made. It is not improved by tools, and it is not kept by clutching. In later Chinese 神器 simply means the throne.","Some modern editions add 不可執也, “it can’t be held”, restored from the parallel in the next sentence and from Wang Bi’s own commentary, which glosses both verbs. Wang Bi’s text, which the globe carries, does not have it. 為者敗之，執者失之 returns word for word in chapter 64.","Then four pairs of 或, “some”. Some go ahead, some follow. Some breathe warm, some blow cold. Some are strong, some are wasted. Some ride, some fall. The verbs are bodily: walking, breathing, health, carriage.","The sage removes three things: 甚, 奢, 泰. The extreme, the lavish, the grand. They are near synonyms, and the globe’s “excess”, “extravagance” and “too much” keep them near."],"an":["The globe’s three passages are claim, evidence and policy. The world cannot be worked on. Look: things alternate by themselves. So the sage only trims.","The middle passage is the argument. If things lead and follow, wax and waste, of their own accord, then any fixed design is fitted to a moment that is already passing. Whoever works on the world is working on last season’s world.","The policy is smaller than “do nothing”. 去, remove, is an action, and it has objects. Not-doing in this chapter means declining to build and continuing to prune.","The chapter follows from the last line of chapter 28. The great tailoring does not sever; the world is not to be worked on. The word 器 links them. There the cut wood became tools. Here the whole world is a vessel that must not be treated as one."],"di":["Readers from several camps have claimed this chapter: conservatives who distrust blueprints, libertarians who distrust planners, ecologists who distrust engineering. Each finds its own enemy in 為之.","The objection is obvious and serious. Some things should be remade. A world with slavery in it was not a sacred vessel to be left alone. “It moves on its own” has always been the favourite argument of whoever the present arrangement suits.","The chapter’s last line limits that use. What the sage removes is 甚, 奢 and 泰, and in this book those belong to the top. Chapter 75 says the people starve because those above eat too much in taxes. Trimming excess starts with the palace.","What remains is an attitude toward anything shared and inherited: a country, an institution, a language, a river. One can damage such a thing. One did not make it. 神器 names that asymmetry, and asks for the hands that go with it."]},"30":{"lead":"Chapter 30 is advice to a ruler’s counsellor about force. It rebounds, so use it only to get a necessary result, and stop there.","ex":["以道佐人主者 is one who assists a lord of men by the Way. The chapter is addressed to ministers, the class that actually read books. 不以兵強天下: he does not use arms to be strong over the world.","其事好還: such business likes to come back. 還 is to return, to rebound. The globe’s “That kind of thing comes back” is as blunt as the Chinese.","師之所處，荊棘生焉: where armies have camped, thorns and brambles grow. The fields are left untended. 大軍之後，必有凶年: after a great host, famine years. The second sentence is missing from both silk copies, and was probably a proverb drawn in later.","果 is fruit, and so result. 善有果而已, in Wang Bi’s text: the good one gets a result, and that is all. Other editions read 善者果而已. 果 comes six times in some thirty characters. Each time a restraint is attached: no pride, no boasting, no arrogance, only because there was no choice, no forcing.","矜 and 伐, pride and boasting, are the vices of chapters 22 and 24. There they belonged to anyone with standing. Here they belong to a victor.","物壯則老: a thing in full vigour then grows old. 壯 is the prime of strength, the word for a man of fighting age. The closing three clauses return unchanged at the end of chapter 55."],"an":["The chapter is not pacifist. It allows 果, the result, and it allows nothing else. Force is reduced to its function and stripped of everything that usually comes with it: glory, display, advantage, momentum.","強 frames the whole. Do not be strong over the world by arms. Do not use the result to seize strength. Get the result and do not force. Then the law: what is forced to its peak ages. Strength sought by arms is the one subject.","The last lines are puzzling as natural history. Ageing after one’s prime is natural, so why call it “going against the Way”? Because 壯 here is forced ripeness. The Way stays supple, and chapter 76 will say why: the soft and weak are the companions of life.","The chapter is on the Guodian slips in a shorter form. Its core is among the oldest material in the book: do not force the world by arms, get the result, and stop.","“The skilled get the result, and stop” is exact. “Things that peak grow old” turns 壯 into a verb, and gains by it."],"di":["Three early positions on war can be set side by side. Sunzi wants to win at the least cost, best of all without fighting. Mencius wants those skilled at war given the heaviest punishment. This chapter stands between them: get the result, and stop.","其事好還 is a law of recoil, and the modern world has its own word for it. Intelligence services call it blowback. The chapter saw it in abandoned fields.","History supplied an example not long after the slips were buried. Qin unified the states by arms in 221 BCE and was gone fifteen years later. Later readers took this chapter’s last line as that dynasty’s epitaph.","The hardest restraint is the fourth. 果而不得已: get the result because there was no choice. It asks the victor to hold, even in success, that it would have been better not to have had to. The next chapter turns that into ritual."]},"31":{"lead":"Chapter 31 says weapons are instruments of ill omen, that victory is not beautiful, and that an army should stand, and a victory be kept, by the rites of a funeral.","ex":["佳兵, “fine weapons”, is probably a copyist’s accident. The silk copies have no 佳: 夫兵者不祥之器, weapons are instruments of ill omen. Not the fine ones. All of them.","不祥 is ritual language: inauspicious, ill-omened. The globe’s “tools of bad luck” is plain, and it loses the temple. 器 answers chapter 29, where the world was a spirit vessel. Weapons are the other kind of vessel.","物或惡之，故有道者不處 is repeated from chapter 24. There the thing detested was showing off.","居則貴左，用兵則貴右: at home the left is the place of honour, and in war the right. The left is the side of life and the right the side of death, and the right is where the chief mourner stands. An army, by its seating, is already at a funeral.","恬淡 is calm and bland. 淡 is the tastelessness that chapter 35 will give the Way itself. Weapons are to be used without relish.","勝而不美: victorious, and not finding it beautiful. 而美之者，是樂殺人: whoever makes it beautiful delights in killing people. Mencius told a king the same thing from the other side: the one with no taste for killing is the one who can unite the world.","偏將軍 is the second in command and 上將軍 the commander. The commander stands on the right. 言以喪禮處之: “this says they are placed by the rites of mourning”. That 言, “this says”, is a commentator’s phrase. Some of this chapter looks like an old gloss absorbed into the text.","殺人之眾，以悲哀泣之: when many people have been killed, weep for them in grief. The line does not say “our dead”. It says people."],"an":["The chapter argues through seating. Where people stand says what an event is. By placing its commander in the mourner’s position, the army declares before the battle what the battle will be.","That is a striking use of ritual in a book that distrusts it. Chapter 38 will call ritual the thinning of loyalty and the beginning of disorder. Here ritual is the one thing strong enough to hold a victor’s face still.","The repetitions gave the chapter a bad name with editors. It says twice that weapons are ill-omened. It explains itself with 言. Wang Bi’s commentary is missing for it. For centuries it was suspected of being late. Then its second half turned up on the Guodian slips.","The globe’s four passages follow the turns: weapons, their use, victory, mourning. “Victory isn’t beautiful” and “Treat a victory like a funeral” are as good as English can do for these lines."],"di":["Most cultures give the victor a parade. This text gives him a funeral, and makes him chief mourner for the people he has killed.","It is not pacifism. 不得已而用之: when there is no choice, weapons are used. What the chapter regulates is the spirit of the use. No relish before, no beauty after. The standard is hard to meet and easy to state.","美之, “to make it beautiful”, is the phrase that reaches furthest. It covers the triumphal arch and the war poem, and now the film and the game. The chapter offers a test for any of them: does it make victory beautiful, or does it make one weep for the number.","The promise attached is doubtful. The chapter says those who enjoy killing will never have their way with the world. Many have. Mencius made the same wager, and it is better understood as what both of them needed to be true."]},"32":{"lead":"Chapter 32 joins the nameless Way to the uncarved wood, imagines a realm that orders itself around a ruler who keeps to it, and then admits that names must come and says where to stop.","ex":["The first six characters can be cut two ways. 道常無名。樸雖小: the Way is always nameless; the wood, though small. Or 道常無名樸: the Way is always a nameless uncarved block. Chapter 37 uses exactly that phrase, 無名之樸.","天下莫能臣: no one in the world can make it a subject. 臣 is a minister, a servant of the court. The uncarved has no handle. Only what has been cut into a tool can be employed. The silk copy reads 弗敢臣: none dares.","侯王, lords and kings, appear here for the first time. They return in chapters 37 and 39. 自賓 is to come as a guest of one’s own accord: to pay court unasked.","自 is the working word. 自賓 here, 自均 in the next line, and later 自化, 自正, 自富, 自樸: things transform, straighten, prosper and simplify by themselves. This is 自然, “so of itself”, turned into a grammar.","甘露, sweet dew, is an omen of perfect rule. Later emperors named reign periods after it. 民莫之令而自均: with no one ordering them, the people share it out evenly. Dew is the model. It falls on everything alike.","始制有名: when the cutting begins, there are names. 制 is the tailoring of chapter 28. The first cut in the wood makes things that can be named: ranks, offices, kinds. The globe keeps the image: “When cutting begins, there are names.”","知止, knowing when to stop, is the remedy. Not no names. Names, and a limit. The phrase returns in chapter 44.","The simile is ambiguous in Chinese. The Way in the world is like streams and valleys in relation to rivers and sea. Chapter 66 settles which is which: rivers and seas are where everything runs down to. The Way is the sea."],"an":["The chapter restates chapter 28 in terms of names. Uncarved is nameless. Cutting is naming. Governing is knowing when to stop cutting. The three passages are those three steps, with the sea added as a seal.","It is the book’s one explicit concession to institutions. 名亦既有: the names are already here. Nothing is said about getting rid of them. The whole of the advice is about the stopping point.","The first passage and the second pull against each other on purpose. One is a vision: guests arriving unbidden, dew, equal shares without orders. The other is administration: there are names now, so stop in time. The chapter holds the ideal and the workable in adjacent sentences.","小, small, will be picked up by chapter 34, where the Way “could be called small”. Smallness in these chapters is the look of something that asks for nothing."],"di":["The dream of the first passage is the root of every later Chinese anarchism: a people who share without being told. It should be read as a dream. Dew does not feed anyone, and equal sharing without orders has been rare.","The second passage is the more useful and the less quoted. Every system of names tends to grow. Law adds statutes, offices add titles, medicine adds diagnoses, and any craft adds terms. Each addition is reasonable. The danger, 殆, is in the sum.","Knowing when to stop is hard to turn into a rule, because the rule would be one more name. The chapter gives no criterion. It gives a direction: toward the wood. When the names have covered the thing they were cut from, it was time to stop some while ago.","The sea is the right image to end on. It does not summon the rivers. It is lower than they are."]},"33":{"lead":"Chapter 33 is eight definitions, and each one moves a prized thing from outside a person to inside.","ex":["The first pair ranks two kinds of knowing. 知人者智: knowing others is 智, cleverness. 自知者明: knowing oneself is 明, clarity. 智 is the faculty this book distrusts. 明 is its word for real seeing: chapter 16 gave it to knowing the constant.","The second pair does the same for power. 勝人者有力: whoever overcomes others has force. 自勝者強: whoever overcomes himself is strong. 強 is usually a bad word in this book. Here it is taken back and given a new owner.","知足者富: whoever knows what is enough is rich. The thought returns in chapters 44 and 46.","強行者有志 is the uncertain line. 強行 is to act with force, to press on. 志 is will, purpose. It may be praise: perseverance shows resolve. It may be faint praise: pressing on is only will, and chapter 3 wanted the will weakened. The globe’s “Pushing on is will” leaves it open, as the Chinese does.","不失其所者久: whoever does not lose his place lasts. 所 is where one belongs: station, ground, footing.","死而不亡者壽: to die and not 亡 is long life. 亡 is to be lost, to be gone. The silk copies write 忘, to be forgotten: to die and not be forgotten. The two characters were interchangeable, and the two readings are not the same promise."],"an":["The list covers what a Warring States lord wanted: intelligence about others, victory, wealth, drive, permanence, long life. Each is granted and relocated. Intelligence becomes self-knowledge. Victory becomes self-mastery. Wealth becomes sufficiency. Permanence becomes keeping one’s place.","The chapter has no cosmology and no ruler. It is the most portable in the book, and the least distinctive. Half of it could sit in the Analects. That is probably why it is quoted so often.","自勝, conquering oneself, is a martial figure in a book that has just spent two chapters on the grief of victory. It is the only victory the book praises. Even so it sits oddly beside chapter 55, where forcing the breath with the mind is called 強 and condemned. The word is doing two jobs.","The globe keeps the chapter as one passage of eight lines, which is its true shape. The English distinguishes 智 and 明 as “cleverness” and “clarity”, and uses “clarity” for 明 everywhere else too. That consistency is worth more than any single rendering."],"di":["The famous first pair hides a difficulty. Self-knowledge sounds like a possession. In this book it is more like a limit. Chapter 71 will say that knowing one does not know is best. To know oneself, on that account, is mostly to know where one’s knowing stops.","The last line is a riddle. What is it that dies and is not gone? Wang Bi says the body is lost and the Way remains. Heshang Gong reads it as bodily long life. The silk copies say memory: one lasts as long as one is not forgotten.","The old histories have a version of the third. They speak of three things that do not decay: virtue established, deeds established, words established. It is an odd doctrine to meet in a book that keeps warning against a name.","The line may be best left as a question put to the first line. If knowing oneself is clarity, then one should be able to say what in oneself could die and not be gone. The chapter does not answer. It ends there.","所, one’s place, deserves the same treatment. The chapter does not say where it is. The rest of the book has only ever named one kind of place as safe: low, plain, near the root."]},"34":{"lead":"Chapter 34 describes a source that gives everything and rules nothing, and that can be called small or great with equal justice.","ex":["氾 is a flood, water over its banks. 其可左右: it can go left or right. The great Way has no channel. Water was the nearest thing to the Way in chapter 8. Here the Way itself is water.","Three verbs of giving are each followed by a refusal. Things rely on it to live, and it does not 辭, turn them away. The work is done, and it does not 有, own it. It clothes and feeds them, and does not 為主, act as master.","不辭 is the phrase of chapter 2, and the globe translates it the same way in both places: “turns none of them away”. 不為主 is said twice. The Way is not a lord.","常無欲，可名於小: always without desire, it can be named among the small. It asks for nothing, so nothing draws attention to it. 萬物歸焉: the ten thousand things come home to it. So 可名為大, it can be named great.","The silk copies are shorter. They lack the lines about relying and about clothing and feeding. And they end on the sage, not the Way: the sage can become great because he does not act great. The received text gives that sentence to the sage in chapter 63."],"an":["The chapter is the cosmic original of an ethic the book has stated three times for people. Chapter 2: finish the work and do not settle in it. Chapter 9: step back. Chapter 10: raise without ruling. Here the Way is shown doing it first.","The two names cancel each other on purpose. 可名, “can be named”, is the phrase from the book’s first line, where the nameable name was not the constant one. Small and great are both available. Neither stays on.","The globe’s two passages divide the giving from the naming. The first is all verbs. The second is all predicates, and its last sentence turns them into a law: what does not make itself great becomes great.","“Because it never thinks itself great” moves the Chinese slightly inward. 不自為大 is closer to “does not make itself great”, or “does not act the great one”. The difference is between an opinion and a behaviour."],"di":["For a reader from a tradition with a creator, the chapter’s strangest feature is what the source does not do. It gives life and issues no commands. It feeds and takes no worship. Everything depends on it, and it is no one’s king.","The same pattern is hard for people, because provision usually produces mastery. Chapter 13 put it in three characters in the old copies: favour is the low thing. Whoever feeds becomes the one above. The chapter claims the Way escapes this, and says how: 常無欲. It wants nothing back, not even to be noticed.","That is the reason for “small”. A giver who needs acknowledgement is visible. One who needs none disappears into what was given. The chapter says such a thing could be called small before it says it could be called great, and the order matters.","Greatness in this chapter is a side effect, and it is lost by aiming at it. The last line is true of very few things. It is true of the ones that matter here: trust, authority, love."]},"35":{"lead":"Chapter 35 sets two attractions side by side. Music and food stop a traveller for an evening. The Way has no flavour at all, and the whole world goes to it.","ex":["大象, the great image, is the Way. Chapter 41 will say the great image has no shape. 執, to hold, is the verb of chapter 14. 天下往: the world goes there.","往而不害: they go and are not harmed. 安平太: three words for peace in a row. Safe, level, at ease.","樂與餌 is music and cakes. 過客止: the passing guest stops. A 過客 is someone on the way elsewhere. The pleasures halt him. They do not keep him.","道之出口, the Way as it comes out of the mouth, is 淡: thin, bland, without taste. This is the character chapter 31 used for the mood in which weapons should be handled.","Then comes a triad the reader knows from chapter 14. Look, and there is not enough to see. Listen, and there is not enough to hear. But the third term has changed. It is no longer touch. 用之不足既: use it, and it cannot be finished."],"an":["The chapter compares two kinds of pull. One works on the senses and is brief. The other gives the senses nothing and does not run out. The globe’s two passages are the two halves: the image that draws the world, and the test by flavour.","It answers chapter 12. There the five flavours spoiled the palate. Here the remedy is named: something with no flavour. After saturation, the only thing that can be tasted is the plain.","用之不足既 joins a line of statements that runs through the first half of the book. The cup that use never fills. The bellows that never runs out. The valley spirit that never wears out. The Way’s one advertised property is that it is still there after use.","The English marks the turn with one word: “But the Way, when it is spoken, is bland, with no flavour.” The Chinese has no “but”. The contrast is made by setting the two sentences together, and the translation is right to supply it."],"di":["淡 became a central value in Chinese taste: the painting with more paper than ink, the tea that tastes of almost nothing. The Zhuangzi applies it to people. The friendship of the worthy is bland as water, it says, and the friendship of the petty is sweet as new wine.","The point of that saying is duration. What is sweet cloys, and what is bland can be returned to every day. The chapter makes the same claim for the Way, and by extension for whatever in a life has the same quality.","There is a difficulty here for anything made to be beautiful, this book included. The chapter says the Way’s speech has no flavour, and says so in some of the most memorable lines in the language. The Laozi is not bland. It is music and cakes for the passing reader.","Its defence is the last line. The test it proposes is not how a thing tastes on the first evening. It is what is left after use."]},"36":{"lead":"Chapter 36 states a law of reversal in four lines and then advises concealment. It is the chapter that made the book’s enemies call it a manual for schemers, and they had grounds.","ex":["Four lines share one frame: 將欲 X 之，必固 Y 之. About to X it, one must first Y it. Contract and stretch. Weaken and strengthen. Abolish and raise up. Take and give.","Each verb has an object, 之, “it”. Someone is doing these things to something. The English keeps the someone: “To shrink something, first stretch it.” Wang Bi reads the four lines the same way, as a method for removing the violent. Turned impersonal, “What is about to shrink must first be stretched”, the sentence would become a law of nature.","The earliest commentary reads an agent. Han Feizi illustrates the lines with two stories. A king of Yue serves his conqueror and urges him on to further wars until he is spent. A duke of Jin sends jade and horses to the small state he means to attack. For Han Feizi the chapter is technique.","The lines were proverbial before they were Laozi’s. The Intrigues of the Warring States quotes them from a Book of Zhou: about to defeat him, first help him; about to take from him, first give to him.","In 微明, 微 is minute, barely visible, and 明 is clarity. The phrase can mean seeing what is still small: the contraction already present in the stretch. It can also mean light kept dim: insight not displayed.","魚不可脫於淵: a fish must not leave the deep. 淵 is the abyss of chapter 4. 國之利器不可以示人: the state’s sharp instruments must not be shown to people.","Antiquity gave 利器 three meanings. Weapons. Rewards and punishments, which is Han Feizi’s reading: the handles by which a ruler controls his ministers. And the sage himself, which is the Zhuangzi’s: the sage is the world’s sharp instrument, and not for showing."],"an":["Everything depends on how 將欲 is heard. As intention, the four lines are advice: to weaken him, strengthen him first. As imminence, they are observation: what is about to weaken has first been strong. Chapter 40 supports the second, since reversal is how the Way moves. The grammar and the oldest reader support the first.","The middle line links the halves either way. 柔弱勝剛強: the soft and weak overcome the hard and strong. On the tactical reading, by letting the strong overreach. On the descriptive one, because the strong are already at their turn.","The last two lines favour tactics. A law of nature needs no hiding. Sharp instruments do.","The globe’s passage break falls before 柔弱勝剛強, which sets the four reversals apart as their own unit. “Subtle clarity” keeps 明 as “clarity”, consistent with the rest of the translation."],"di":["Confucian readers singled this chapter out. To give in order to take is, on their account, the mark of a crooked mind, and the Song masters said so. The charge cannot be waved away, because the first readers on record used the chapter exactly that way.","There are three honest defences. The lines may describe and not prescribe. They may be the knowledge of the weak, who need to know that an aggressor’s expansion is the beginning of his end. And they are bounded by chapters 30 and 31: get the result and stop, and mourn.","None of the three removes the tactical use. A stratagem still current in Chinese, 欲擒故縱, to catch something first let it go, is this chapter in four characters. 微明 is a faculty: seeing the small sign. What a person does with what they see is not settled by the seeing.","The last line is the one a modern reader will resist. It recommends that the state’s real instruments stay out of sight. Set beside chapter 17’s unnoticed ruler and chapter 65’s refusal to enlighten the people, it makes a politics of opacity. That strand is in the book, and it should be read as it stands.","The fish is the gentler half of the same thought, and it can be kept. A creature out of its depth dies. Chapter 26 said not to leave the baggage, and chapter 33 not to lose one’s place. This one says: stay in the water you can breathe."]},"37":{"lead":"Chapter 37 closes the Dao half with a promise and a contingency. A ruler who keeps to not-doing will see things change by themselves, and for the moment wanting stirs he keeps one remedy, which has no name.","ex":["道常無為而無不為: the Way constantly does nothing, and there is nothing it does not do. The globe’s English sets “does” against “undone”: “The Way never does anything, and nothing is left undone.”","The next sentence is chapter 32’s with one word changed. There, if lords and kings could keep to it, the ten thousand things would 自賓, come as guests of their own accord. Here they 自化, “change by themselves”. 化 is the word for what seasons and teaching do to things.","化而欲作: in the course of changing, wanting rises. 作 is to stir, to start up. 吾將鎮之: I will press it down. 鎮 is weight, the word in “paperweight” and in “garrison”. The English keeps the weight: “I’ll steady them with the weight of nameless uncarved wood.”","The weight is 無名之樸, “nameless uncarved wood”. 樸 is timber before the tool, and this is the fifth chapter to use it. The phrase is then said again, alone, and followed by 夫亦將無欲: then there will also be no wanting.","The grammar does not say whose. It may be the people’s, pressed back into simplicity. It may be the ruler’s, who must not come to want even the uncarved wood. The globe leaves both open: “then there is no wanting.”","不欲以靜，天下將自定. “Without wanting, there is stillness, and the world settles by itself.”"],"an":["The first line is the best-known sentence on not-doing in the book, and the manuscripts do not agree that it belongs here. Both silk copies open 道恆無名, the Way is always nameless, which is how chapter 32 opens. The Guodian slips open 道恆亡為也, the Way always does nothing, and stop. “And nothing is left undone” is in none of the three.","The phrase is old all the same. It stands in chapter 48, and the Guodian slips have it there. What the manuscripts show is a formula that moved before it settled at the head of this chapter.","The ending moved too. The received text has 無欲, no wanting. The silk has 不辱, no disgrace. The Guodian slips have 知足, knowing what is enough. Chapter 44 will put the last two in one line.","The chapter has four steps: a claim about the Way, a condition for rulers, a plan for when the condition fails, and a result. Only the third has a first person in it, and only there does anyone act.","That is the difficulty. 鎮 is a deed. A chapter that opens on not-doing keeps one act in reserve. What saves the logic is the instrument. Laws, rewards and punishments all have names. This ruler answers wanting by taking names away, so that nothing is held up as worth wanting. Chapter 3 said as much in plainer terms.","The half ends where it began. Chapter 1 paired 無名 with 有名 and 無欲 with 有欲. Chapter 37 closes on 無名之樸 and 無欲. In the silk copies, which put the De half first, these are the last lines of the whole book."],"di":["Han Feizi took 無為 as a technique of rule. The ruler shows nothing and does nothing, the ministers do everything, and nothing is left undone because others do it. The received first line allows that reading. The Guodian line, which has the not-doing and no payoff, gives it less to hold.","The hard question is who decides that wanting has stirred too far. The “I” of the chapter does, and nothing checks him. A reader can accept the first line and still distrust the hand on the weight.","The best defence is the second reading of 夫亦將無欲. If the ruler is the one who must not want, the weight falls on him first. A ruler who wants a simple people is still wanting, and the uncarved wood is no use to him as a programme.","The globe’s own note glosses not-doing as “acting without forcing”. 鎮 tests the gloss. A weight forces nothing, and it holds things down.","The globe gives the chapter as one passage and adds something no page can. It sings 無 as a gap, and 無 comes five times in these fifty characters. A tenth of the chapter is rest."]},"38":{"lead":"Chapter 38 opens the De half by ranking virtue, kindness, justice and etiquette in descending order. What sinks a thing in the ranking is how much it knows about itself.","ex":["上德不德，是以有德. “The highest virtue isn’t virtuous, so it has virtue.” In eight characters 德 is a rank, then a pose, then a possession. The globe’s own note warns that the word is “less moral virtue than the power a thing has when it follows its own nature”.","Lower virtue 不失德, “never lets go of being virtuous”. It holds on, and the holding is the loss.","The second passage grades by two tests. Does it act, 為? Does it have something to act for, 以為? Highest virtue: no act, no motive. Highest kindness, 仁: act, no motive. Highest justice, 義: act and motive.","Highest etiquette, 禮, acts, and when no one answers, 攘臂而扔之. It bares its arms and drags them. The globe’s line is exact and funny: “it rolls up its sleeves and drags them into line.”","Then the ladder. The Way lost, there is virtue; virtue lost, kindness; then justice; then etiquette. 禮 is 忠信之薄, the thinning of loyalty and trust, and 亂之首, the head of disorder. The English keeps the thinning: etiquette is “where loyalty and trust wear thin”.","前識, knowing beforehand, is 道之華: the flower of the Way, showy and soon over, 而愚之始, and the beginning of foolishness.","The 大丈夫, the great man, stays with the thick and the fruit. The phrase occurs only here. The globe makes it “the truly grown-up”, which drops the gender and keeps the point. The last line, 去彼取此, also closes chapters 12 and 72."],"an":["The texts disagree in the second passage, and the disagreement is instructive. The globe has lower virtue do nothing with a motive. The Daozang text has it act with a motive, which makes it the same as highest justice two lines on. The silk copies have no lower-virtue line at all, and neither does Han Feizi, the earliest commentator.","So the line was probably added to balance the opening pair, and later repaired in more than one way. The silk series is cleaner. It has four rungs, and each adds one thing: an act, a motive, force.","Han Feizi’s copy differed in two more places. Its highest virtue does nothing “and leaves nothing undone”, the formula that heads chapter 37 on the globe. And its ladder repeats 失 at every step: lose the Way and then lose virtue, lose virtue and then lose kindness.","That is a cascade of losses. The received ladder is a row of substitutes, each arriving because the one above has failed. It is the harsher version. Kindness is what people reach for once virtue is gone.","In the silk copies this is the first chapter of the book, and Han Feizi’s commentary begins here too. On the globe it is where the southern hemisphere starts. 德 comes ten times in it, more than in any other chapter.","The globe’s four passages cut at the joints: paradox, grades, ladder, choice."],"di":["The target is Confucian 禮, and a Confucian has an answer. Confucius himself asked what ritual is worth in a person without kindness. He would grant that empty form is thin. His claim is that form is how sincerity gets a body, and that people without forms do not become spontaneous. They become rude. The chapter takes the degenerate case for the essence.","Its sharpest observation survives the objection. Courtesy that goes unanswered turns to force. Anyone who has watched a polite institution meet a refusal has seen the sleeves go up.","前識 can be heard as a warning about forecasters. Han Feizi tells of a man who guessed, from indoors, the colour of an ox at the gate. He was right. The comment is that a boy sent out to look would have known as much.","The opening paradox sets a trap for the reader. Try to be the highest virtue by not being virtuous, and you are holding on again. The chapter offers no technique. 去彼取此 only points: that, over there; this, here."]},"39":{"lead":"Chapter 39 says that whatever is high holds its place by something it does not own. From that it draws a rule for kings: build on the low.","ex":["昔之得一者: those that long ago got the One. Six follow, each with its gift. Heaven is clear, 清. Earth is calm, 寧. Spirits are 靈, potent, which the globe gives as “came alive”. The valley is full. The ten thousand things are born. Lords and kings become 天下貞, the standard that keeps the world true.","其致之 closes the list: it is this that brings them there. The globe supplies the noun: “All of this came from the one.”","The six gifts rhyme: 清 寧 靈 盈 生 貞. The list is then run again in the negative, and the six disasters rhyme on another sound: 裂 發 歇 竭 滅 蹶. Crack, quake, fade, run dry, die out, fall. Cantonese still ends all six on a clipped -t. The chapter is a chant on two rhymes, one for having and one for losing.","Wang Bi’s lists are complete: 谷得一以盈, the valley found the one and became full; 神無以靈, without what makes them alive, spirits would fade. (The first version of the globe had lost the 得 and turned the 無 into 為. Both are mended.)","The second list is audible. The globe sings 無 as a rest, so six times the voice says “without” by falling silent.","The sixth disaster breaks the pattern. For kings the text says not 無以貞 but 無以貴高, “Without what makes them high”. That prepares the turn: 貴以賤為本，高以下為基. “So the noble is rooted in the humble, and the high is built on the low.”","The proof offered is court usage. Kings call themselves 孤, 寡, 不穀: “orphaned, lonely, unworthy.” Then 致數譽無譽, “reaching for much praise brings no praise”, and the stones. Wang Bi reads the last line as a refusal of both: not to glitter like jade, nor to be hard as stone, since each is used up in its form."],"an":["The chapter never says 道. The globe’s own note takes the One as “the first thing the Way gives birth to”, which is chapter 42’s account. Chapter 39 does not need the distinction. Its One is whatever a thing cannot supply for itself.","That is the argument of the second list. 無以 is to lack the means. Heaven’s clarity is not heaven’s property: take away what makes it clear and it splits. Each thing’s defining excellence is held on loan.","The silk copies can be read as arguing something else. They write 毋已 where the received text has 無以. Taken as a loan for 無以, it changes nothing. Taken as it stands, it means “without stopping”: heaven clear without end would crack, and kings noble and high without end would fall. The danger is then not losing the One but pushing one’s own quality past its limit. The lesson about the low follows at once.","The silk copies also lack both lines about the ten thousand things. Their lists have five members each.","致數譽無譽 is Wang Bi’s reading in the Daozang print. The silk copies have 數輿無輿 and Heshang Gong has 數車無車: count up a cart and there is no cart. Spokes, wheel, hub, crossbar; no part is called “cart”. That reading suits the first half of the chapter, where a whole has what its parts lack. The praise reading suits the second half, on kings and modesty. Some editors print 至譽無譽, “the highest praise is no praise”, from a line in the Zhuangzi.","The globe’s two breaks fall at the turns: gift, loss, lesson."],"di":["The evidence from titles is weaker than the chapter thinks. Only a king may call himself 孤. Humility that no one else is allowed to use is a privilege, and the doubled question, 非乎, sounds as if the text half knows it. It is also odd that the chapter after the attack on etiquette proves its point from court etiquette.","The stronger half is the list of disasters. It reads as a statement of dependencies: for each thing that looks self-standing, say what it would be without what it rests on. Kings rest on those below them. That claim does not need the titles.","The last line is usually read as a choice: not rare and polished, but common and hard. Another reading takes 不欲 over both halves: wish to be neither prized like jade nor scorned like stone. Chapter 70 will bring jade back, carried under coarse cloth. The book has nothing against jade. Its quarrel is with the glitter."]},"40":{"lead":"Chapter 40 gives the Way’s motion, its method and the origin of things in twenty-one characters, the fewest of any chapter.","ex":["反者道之動. 反 is turning back. It covers two things English keeps apart: return, as in chapter 25, where the far-reaching comes back, and reversal, a thing becoming its opposite. The globe’s key note lists both, “returning; reversal”. Its English chooses the first: “Returning is how the Way moves.”","弱者道之用. 弱 is weak, and the English keeps it: “Weakness is how the Way works.” 用 is use or function. Chapter 36 said the soft and weak overcome the hard and strong. This line gives the reason: weakness is the Way’s own manner of working.","天下萬物生於有，有生於無. “The ten thousand things are born from being. Being is born from nothing.” 有 is having, what is there. 無 is not-having.","The chapter’s last character is 無. The globe sings that character as a gap, so its shortest chapter ends on a rest."],"an":["Two lines describe and one derives. The first two fit together as a physics and a practice. If things turn back at their extreme, the position that lasts is the one that has not gone to an extreme. Weakness is not a point on the wheel. It is a way of standing on it.","The third line is the one philosophy took up. Wang Bi built on it the claim that all being has its root in non-being. Half a century later Pei Wei answered that sheer nothing has no means of producing anything.","The Guodian slips may never have said it. Their line reads 天下之物生於有，生於亡: the things of the world are born from being, born from non-being. There is one 有, not two. Either a scribe dropped a repeat mark, or the oldest copy has things arise from being and non-being together.","The second possibility agrees with chapter 2, where the two give birth to each other: 有無相生. The received line makes a ladder. The Guodian line makes a pair.","The order differs as well. The silk copies put chapter 41 before this one, so that chapter 40 runs straight into 42 and the two origin stories meet: being from nothing, then the Way giving birth to one. On the Guodian slips it stands between chapters 44 and 9. The received order, which the globe follows, is the latest attested of the three."],"di":["What kind of nothing? If 無 is sheer absence, the line reports a miracle, and Pei Wei’s objection stands. Chapter 1 offers a smaller reading. There the pair is 無名 and 有名: the nameless is the beginning of heaven and earth, the named is the mother of the ten thousand things. On that reading 無 is what has no outline yet, and “born from nothing” means born from what was not yet anything in particular.","Reversal has a problem of its own. If everything turns back, weakness turns into strength and the advice undoes itself. The reply is in the second line. 弱 is not a state to arrive at. It is how the Way works at every point of the turning.","The first line can be tested without metaphysics. Things pushed to an extreme come back: prices, tempers, tides, empires. The chapter’s wager is that this is no accident of those things. It is the motion of everything."]},"41":{"lead":"Chapter 41 says the Way will always look wrong from outside, and it counts the laughter of those who dismiss it as part of the evidence.","ex":["Three kinds of 士 hear of the Way. The best 勤而行之: “they work hard to live it.” The middling 若存若亡: now it is there, now it is gone. The globe has “they half keep it, half lose it.” The lowest 大笑之, “laugh out loud”. Then 不笑不足以為道: “If they didn’t laugh, it wouldn’t be the Way.”","故建言有之 introduces a quotation. 建言 is either “established sayings” or the title of a lost collection. The globe’s “So the old sayings go” takes the first. Either way the book says here that it is quoting.","Thirteen lines follow, in two grammars. Eight use 若, “seems”. The bright Way seems dark, the Way forward seems to go back, and the level Way seems knotted: 纇 is a knot in silk thread. High virtue seems a valley. The whitest seems 辱, soiled. Vast virtue seems not enough. Robust virtue seems 偷, to be slacking, the globe’s “idle”. Solid truth seems 渝, to fade or shift.","Then the verb changes. Four lines pair 大 with a denial. 大方無隅: “The great square has no corners.” 大器晚成: “The great vessel is finished late.” 大音希聲: “The great sound is barely a sound.” 大象無形: “The great image has no shape.” A thirteenth line sums up: 道隱無名, “The Way is hidden and has no name.”","The close turns the other way. 夫唯道，善貸且成. Only the Way is good at lending and at bringing to completion. 貸 is to lend; the globe has “gives well”."],"an":["The list explains the laugh. Every line has one form: the real X looks like not-X. If that is so, the evidence is ambiguous by nature, and the three hearers are answering the same evidence. That is the sense of the title, 同異: one thing heard, three hearings.","What marks the best hearer is not belief. It is 行, walking it.","The first line is less confident in the old copies. The silk and the Guodian slips write 堇, which can stand for 勤, diligent, or for 僅, barely. The Guodian line runs 堇能行於其中: the best hearer is, perhaps, barely able to walk in it. “Barely manages”, “half keeps” and “laughs” make at least as good a descent as the received one.","大器晚成 is the best-known phrase in the chapter. In Chinese today it is the idiom for a late bloomer. Silk copy B has 大器免成: the great vessel is spared completion. The other three 大 lines deny or thin out their noun: no corners, hardly a sound, no shape. A vessel never finished belongs with them. A vessel finished late does not.","The last line differs too. The silk has 善始且善成, good at beginning and good at completing. The chapter then says, almost in one breath, that the great vessel is never completed and that the Way completes well. Both hold only if completing is something the Way does and no vessel ever possesses.","The silk copies place this chapter before chapter 40. The globe breaks it once, before 故建言, between the hearers and the sayings."],"di":["“If they didn’t laugh, it wouldn’t be the Way” is a dangerous sentence. Any teaching can use it to turn mockery into proof, and many have. Read strictly, it claims less. Laughter is necessary, not sufficient: what no one laughs at cannot be the Way, and that does not make everything laughed at true. The chapter’s own test is the first hearer’s, which is practice.","Even so, the sentence hands the insider a comfort the outsider cannot share. The middle hearer is the honest portrait. Most readers of this book are 若存若亡: it is there on some days.","大音希聲 has a particular claim on this site. A globe that sets the text to sound, and leaves a gap for every 無, has taken the line as an instruction."]},"42":{"lead":"Chapter 42 counts from the Way to everything in four steps. Then it turns, without warning, to kings, loss and a proverb about violent men.","ex":["道生一，一生二，二生三，三生萬物. “The Way gives birth to one. One gives birth to two. Two gives birth to three. Three gives birth to the ten thousand things.” 生 is to bear, as a mother does. The text does not say what the numbers are.","萬物負陰而抱陽. Things carry 陰 on their backs and hold 陽 in their arms. This is the only place in the book where either word occurs. Their plain senses are the shaded side and the sunlit side. A creature with shade behind it and sun in its arms is facing the light.","沖氣以為和. 氣 is breath or vapour. 沖 is the hard word. The globe’s key note gives “empty; flowing”, and its English takes a third sense, blending: “the mingling of their breath makes harmony.” The silk writes 中, middle.","Then the royal titles of chapter 39 return, 孤, 寡, 不穀, now as “What people hate most”. A rule is drawn from them: 物或損之而益，或益之而損. “So things may gain by losing, or lose by gaining.”","The end is a citation. 人之所教，我亦教之: “What others teach, I teach too”. What is taught is a proverb, 強梁者不得其死: “The violent don’t die a natural death.” 強梁 is the overbearing man, and the saying is found elsewhere, in an old inscription of warnings.","吾將以為教父: I will take it as the father of the teaching. The globe has “I’ll make that the first thing I teach.” The silk has 學父, father of learning."],"an":["The old explanations fill in the numbers. The Huainanzi takes Two as yin and yang and Three as their blending, which is how the second passage reads if it is a gloss on the first. Heshang Gong does much the same.","The Zhuangzi offers another arithmetic, and a wittier one. All things are one. But I have just said so, and the one and the saying make two. Two and the one make three. From there the best reckoner cannot keep up. On that account the numbers are no history of the cosmos. They are what happens when anything is named.","The two halves may once have been separate. Some editors suspect that the kings and the proverb belong with chapter 39, where the same titles occur. The silk copies already have them here, so the join is old if it is a join.","A thread does connect them. Harmony in the second passage comes of two opposed things blending. The third passage applies it: the high take the names of the low, and loss turns to gain. 損 and 益 come back in chapter 48, where learning adds daily and the Way subtracts.","The chapter is not on the Guodian slips. What was bundled with them is a different origin story, 太一生水, in which the Great One gives birth to water. In the earliest witness we have, the Laozi travelled with a cosmogony that is not this one.","This is the only 父 on the globe. Elsewhere the book’s parent is the mother, 母, in five chapters. In modern Chinese 教父 means godfather.","The globe cuts four passages: numbers, breath, kings, proverb."],"di":["As physics the first passage is too thin to assess. One, two, three and many is a schema and not a mechanism. Its lasting content is structural: no thing without a pair, and no pair without something passing between. The third term is the relation.","The popular picture of this book as the philosophy of yin and yang rests on this one line. The words are everywhere in later Daoism and absent from the other eighty chapters.","The proverb is false as stated. Violent men die in bed often enough. Either the line gives odds, or 不得其死 means something narrower: they do not get a death that is their own. The chapter stakes a good deal on it. It is called the father of the teaching, and it is the one claim here that experience can check.","There is candour in “What others teach, I teach too”. The book does not claim to have invented its ethics. It claims to know where the common sayings come from."]},"43":{"lead":"Chapter 43 argues from one observation, that soft things get through hard ones, to the worth of not-doing. Then it admits that almost no one manages it.","ex":["天下之至柔，馳騁天下之至堅. 馳騁 is to gallop, the verb for a rider driving horses. The softest thing in the world rides down the hardest. The globe changes the picture from riding over to passing through: “The softest thing in the world runs right through the hardest.”","The verb was last seen in chapter 12, where racing and hunting “drive the heart wild”. There it was a vice. Here it is what softness does.","The text does not name the softest thing. Heshang Gong says water, against metal and stone. Wang Bi adds breath, which gets in everywhere. Chapter 78 will say water outright.","無有入無間. 無有 is what has no being, the globe’s “What has no substance”. 無間 is what has no crack: “enters where there is no gap.” The step goes beyond water. Water needs a crack. What is not there at all needs none.","吾是以知無為之有益. “That is how I know the good of not-doing.” 益 is gain, the word chapter 42 set against loss.","不言之教 repeats chapter 2’s phrase exactly: “Teaching without words”. 天下希及之: “few in the world ever reach it.” 希 is rare. It was the faint sound of chapter 41."],"an":["This is one of the few chapters that shows its working. 吾是以知, by this I know. Three other chapters ask how the speaker knows and answer 以此, “by this”, without saying what “this” is. Chapter 43 gives the premise.","The argument runs to a limit. The softest thing overruns the hardest. Softer than soft is nothing at all. So what is nothing gets in everywhere, and acting in its manner, 無為, must have its use. 無有 is the limit of 柔.","The chapter also answers the one before it. Chapter 42 ended on 教, with a proverb as the father of the teaching. Chapter 43 names a teaching that has no words in it. 益 carries over as well. Neighbouring chapters are often linked this way, by a shared word.","On the globe the line about gaps performs itself. 無 is sung as a rest, and it comes twice in five characters: rest, 有, 入, rest, 間. The chapter has four such rests in thirty-nine characters.","The chapter is not on the Guodian slips. The globe breaks it once, before 不言之教, between the inference and the verdict."],"di":["The observation is true and the inference is loose. Water wears stone by weight, time and repetition, and those are forces. Softness wins by offering nothing to push against and by not stopping. That describes persistence. It does not obviously describe doing nothing.","The repair is to take 無為 as the globe’s note does: “acting without forcing”. Then the chapter says something exact. The action that gets through is the one that meets no resistance, because nothing in it is rigid enough to be resisted.","“Few in the world ever reach it” can be heard as pride. It is better heard as a report. Teaching without words cannot be written down, examined or certified, so no institution can prefer it. It has to be done in person, by someone who already is what is taught. That is rare for ordinary reasons.","The book knows the irony. It is five thousand characters in praise of saying nothing, and this chapter is thirty-nine of them."]},"44":{"lead":"Chapter 44 asks three questions about what a person will trade the self for. Its answer is the book’s plainest advice: know what is enough, and stop.","ex":["Each question turns on 孰, “which”. 名與身孰親: name or body, which is 親? 親 is near, dear, kin. “Your name or your body: which is closer?”","身與貨孰多: body or goods, which is more? The globe: “which is worth more?”","得與亡孰病: getting or losing, which is the sickness? 病 is illness, and the globe softens it to “does more harm”. The line does not say what is got and lost. Wang Bi reads it across the first two questions: getting name and goods, and losing the body.","是故甚愛必大費. 愛 is to love, and also to grudge, to hold tight. The globe’s “cling” covers both: “So the more you cling, the more it costs.” 多藏必厚亡: much stored, heavily lost. “The more you hoard, the more you lose.”","Then two kinds of knowing. 知足不辱: “Know what is enough, and you won’t be disgraced.” 知止不殆: “Know when to stop, and you won’t be in danger.” The reward comes last, and it is modest: 可以長久. “You can last a long time.”"],"an":["The questions rank. The body outranks reputation and it outranks property. The third question then turns the obvious answer over. Loss looks like the harm. The chapter implies that gain is, because gain is what the body gets spent on.","The two rules that follow are a theory of cost. Price rises with attachment. Exposure rises with holdings. Neither needs a moral premise. A person who wanted nothing but a long life could accept both.","知足 concerns amount and 知止 concerns motion. Enough is a quantity; stopping is an act. Chapter 33 called knowing enough wealth. Chapter 32 said, in nearly these words, that knowing when to stop keeps one out of danger. Chapter 46 will make not knowing enough the greatest of disasters.","The line also settles a small matter from chapter 37. There the Guodian slips end on 知足 and the silk copies on 不辱. Here the two stand together.","Nothing in the chapter is metaphysical. There is no Way, no virtue, no sage, no ruler, no heaven. There is no 無 either, so the globe sings it through without a rest.","The Guodian slips have the chapter nearly word for word. They set it in a run with chapter 9, where a hall full of gold and jade cannot be guarded, with chapter 40 between. Whoever ordered those slips read this as a chapter about wealth.","The globe breaks it once, before 是故, between the questions and the answers."],"di":["The chapter speaks to someone who has a name and a store. Said to a person with too little, “know what is enough” is an insult, and it has served rulers as a sedative. The text gives no warrant for that. Its warnings are all about the cost of having, and its audience is the people who have.","A second objection is that this is prudence and not ethics. Avoid disgrace, avoid danger, last. The fair answer is yes. The body is the measure here, as in chapter 13, and the thought is close to Yang Zhu, who was said to refuse to give one hair to profit the world. The book does not hide this strain. It does not rest on it alone either: in chapter 7 the sage puts the body last.","The first question has changed its feel. A name can now be managed, measured and sold by the hour, and it often seems nearer than the body does. 親 is the right word to hold against that. The body is kin. The name is an acquaintance."]},"45":{"lead":"Chapter 45 says that the best of anything looks unfinished and is known by its use. It ends by naming clear stillness as what keeps the world straight.","ex":["Five lines share the frame of chapter 41: great X seems not-X. 大成若缺: great completion seems lacking. The globe has “The greatest perfection seems flawed”. 大盈若沖: great fullness seems 沖, empty like the Way of chapter 4.","The first two lines carry a test. 其用不弊, its use does not wear out. 其用不窮, its use does not run out. The globe: “yet using it never wears it out”, “yet using it never uses it up.”","Three follow without the test. 大直若屈, where the globe adds a noun: “The straightest line seems bent.” 大巧若拙: “The greatest skill seems clumsy.” 大辯若訥: “The greatest eloquence seems tongue-tied.” 訥 is slow of speech.","The second passage is two short claims and a conclusion. 躁勝寒，靜勝熱: “Movement overcomes cold. Stillness overcomes heat.” 清靜為天下正: clear stillness is the standard of the world. The English makes it an address: “Clear and still, you set the world right.”"],"an":["其用 is the hinge. Appearance and substance come apart, so appearance cannot be the measure. Use over time can. A thing that looks finished and wears out was not great. A thing that looks lacking and does not wear out was. That answers anyone who takes “seems clumsy” as a licence. Clumsiness wears out.","Chapter 41’s vessel belongs here. 大成若缺 says great completion looks incomplete, which supports the silk reading there: the vessel spared completion.","The line about eloquence is in neither early manuscript. The silk has 大贏如絀 in its place: great surplus seems like shortfall. The received line may have come in from the book’s last chapter, where the good do not argue.","Every old witness reads the second passage this way round, and so does Wang Bi: 躁勝寒，靜勝熱. Movement overcomes cold; stillness overcomes heat. In the twentieth century the line was reordered to 靜勝躁，寒勝熱, to agree with chapter 26, where stillness is the master of restlessness, and many modern editions print the reordering. No manuscript has it.","The old reading is plain observation. Stamp your feet in winter; sit still in summer. The Guodian slips, on one reading, are plainer yet: dry beats cold, cool beats hot. Either way the two halves are symmetrical, and a conclusion in favour of stillness needs one more premise.","One is at hand. The condition the book diagnoses everywhere is heat: ambition, haste, contention. For a fevered world the still remedy is the right one. The reordered text makes stillness a universal victor. The old text makes it the medicine for this patient.","為天下正 answers chapter 39, where kings who find the One become 天下貞. The globe’s English uses one phrase for both, “set the world right”, and so shows a link its Chinese hides behind two characters. It answers chapter 37 as well, which ended the first half on stillness and a world that settles by itself."],"di":["大巧若拙 became a principle of Chinese art. Calligraphers and potters prized the awkward stroke and the uneven glaze, and Fu Shan wrote that he would rather be clumsy than clever. The risk is the one the chapter fences off. Studied clumsiness is another kind of cleverness. The test is still use.","清靜 was once tried as policy. Early in the Han, the chancellor Cao Can governed by changing nothing his predecessor had set up. A song recorded by Sima Qian praised him: he kept things clear and still, and the people were at peace. It worked after a civil war, in a country that needed rest. It is harder to defend as a rule for every season, and the old text of this chapter does not ask for that.","That is the best case for the witnesses’ reading, which the globe carries. “Cold overcomes heat” is not true in general, and the book does not need it to be."]},"46":{"lead":"Chapter 46 measures a country by where its horses are. Then it traces war to one cause: not knowing when there is enough.","ex":["天下有道，卻走馬以糞. 卻 is to send back. 走馬 are running horses, the fast ones kept for war and for couriers. 糞 is dung, used here as a verb: to manure the fields. The globe adds carts: “racehorses are sent back to pull dung carts in the fields.”","天下無道，戎馬生於郊. 戎馬 are warhorses. 生 is to give birth, and 郊 is the open ground outside the walls. The picture is of mares foaling in the field, because even the pregnant ones have been taken for the army. The English keeps the foal: “warhorses foal outside the city walls.”","Two superlatives follow. 禍莫大於不知足: “No disaster is greater than not knowing when you have enough.” 咎莫大於欲得: “No fault is greater than wanting more.” 咎 is blame that comes home. 欲得 is wanting to get.","故知足之足，常足矣. “So the enough that knows it is enough is always enough.” 足 comes three times in seven characters."],"an":["The first passage is a diagnostic and contains no doctrine. Look at the horses. Are they in the furrow or on the frontier? Chapter 53 will offer a second test of the same kind: the court against the granaries.","The second passage names the cause. A state goes to war because someone in it does not know what is enough. The step from horses to appetite is the chapter’s whole argument.","One line is missing on the globe. The Daozang text, both silk copies and Han Feizi have three superlatives, and the first is 罪莫大於可欲: no crime is greater than what stirs desire. Wang Bi’s transmitted text lost it, and the globe follows that text. The lost line points back to chapter 3, which told rulers not to display 可欲. It also makes the series climb: crime, disaster, blame.","The Guodian slips have the superlatives and the closing sentence, and no horses. The two passages the globe separates began as separate sayings.","This is the third 知足 in the book. Chapter 33 called it wealth. Chapter 44 said it keeps off disgrace. Here its absence is the worst of disasters, and the last line adds a turn. Enough is a judgement and not an amount. Only the enough that comes of knowing lasts."],"di":["Han Feizi gives each line a story. For not knowing enough he chooses Zhi Bo, who swallowed two great houses of Jin and went on to attack a third. His allies turned, his army was destroyed, and his lacquered skull ended as a chamber pot.","The objection is that wanting more also drains marshes and cures diseases. A world that knew what was enough would have stayed where it was. The chapter’s reply is in its first image. Its subject is 欲得 with an army behind it. The horses show whose appetite is meant.","It does not follow that every war comes from greed. Chapters 31 and 69 allow for the war one is forced into. This chapter makes the narrower claim, and it can be checked: when the mares are foaling at the walls, look at what the court wants.","The last line also works at a smaller scale than a state. The globe’s note on 足 quotes chapter 33: “Knowing you have enough is wealth.” Chapter 46 gives the converse. No amount is enough for someone who does not know it."]},"47":{"lead":"Chapter 47 claims that the world can be known from inside the house, and that going farther makes knowledge smaller.","ex":["不出戶，知天下. “Without going out the door, you can know the world.” 不窺牖，見天道. 窺 is to peep and 牖 is the window. “Without looking out the window, you can see the Way of heaven.”","Door and window were last seen in chapter 11, cut into a wall to make a room. There their emptiness made the room usable. Here the sage declines to use them.","其出彌遠，其知彌少. 彌 is “the more”. “The farther you go, the less you know.”","Three results close the chapter. 不行而知: the sage “knows without going”. 不見而名: “names without seeing”. 不為而成: “gets it done without doing.”","名 is “name”. Han Feizi quotes the line with 明, to see clearly, and many readers take 名 as a loan for it. The globe translates the character as written."],"an":["The chapter names two objects of knowledge. 天下 is the world of human affairs. 天道 is the pattern of the sky and the seasons. Neither is a list of facts. No one learns the price of grain in the next state without going there. One can learn how prices move.","The book says elsewhere how this is done. Chapter 52: find the mother and you know the children. Chapter 54: look at a self through a self, and ask how the world is known. The answer there is 以此, “by this”. Chapter 47 states the conclusion, and those chapters supply the premises.","On that reading the middle line is exact. Particulars multiply with distance, and a mind full of cases can lose the rule.","The silk copies have 知 in both opening lines: know the world, know the Way of heaven. Seeing came into the text later.","The last clause has a history. Wang Bi’s text, the Daozang plain text and the silk all read 不為 or 弗為: without acting, it is completed. Some modern editions print 無為而成, a phrase from the Doctrine of the Mean, and the first version of the globe did too, singing one rest that no witness has. In Wang Bi’s text the chapter has no 無, and the globe sings it through.","The globe breaks the chapter before 是以聖人, between the claim and the sage."],"di":["The plain objection is that this is a charter for the armchair. Whole sciences exist because people went and looked. The far side of the world was not deducible from the near side. Taken as a claim about facts, the chapter is false.","It is better taken as a claim about a kind of going. The traveller who collects places and the reader who collects opinions both go far, and both can end with less than they started with. The chapter’s sage is not ignorant of the world. He is not looking for it out there.","“Names without seeing” is the clause to distrust. Naming what one has not seen is also the definition of prejudice. If 名 is a loan for 明, the danger shrinks: the sage is clear without looking. The globe’s literal reading keeps the risk in view.","The window has changed. In the chapter it is a hole in a wall, and not looking through it is easy. Now it is carried in a pocket and shows the whole world all day. “The farther you go, the less you know” was written about roads. It reads as if it were about that window."]},"48":{"lead":"Chapter 48 sets two arithmetics side by side. Learning adds every day, the Way subtracts every day, and the subtraction ends in not-doing.","ex":["為學日益，為道日損. 為 is to pursue. 益 and 損 are gain and loss, the pair from chapter 42. “Learning, you gain something every day. Following the Way, you lose something every day.”","損之又損，以至於無為. “Lose, and lose again, until you arrive at not-doing.” The phrase has the shape of chapter 1’s 玄之又玄: a word, and the same word again.","無為而無不為. “Not-doing, and nothing is left undone.” This is the sentence that heads chapter 37 on the globe. Its place here is secure. The Guodian slips have it, written 亡為而亡不為.","The second passage changes subject. 取天下常以無事. 取 is to take or win. 無事 is no business: nothing made into a matter. “The world is won by leaving it alone.” 及其有事，不足以取天下: once there is business, it is not enough to take the world. The globe: “Meddle with it, and you can never win it.”"],"an":["The first line is not a rejection of learning. It describes two activities that run in opposite directions. 學 in this period is a programme: texts, rites and precedents, taken in day by day. The chapter grants that it works. Learning does add. The claim is that the Way is not reached by adding.","What is subtracted is not said. The book’s other chapters suggest the list: wants, cleverness, names, purposes. The end is defined by what is left, which is action with nothing added to it.","The order matters. Subtraction needs something to subtract. A person who never learned has nothing to lose, and the chapter does not praise that person. Chapter 64 has the exact phrase for its sage: 學不學, which the globe renders “learns to unlearn”.","日 stands in both lines. Losing is as daily as study. That answers the reader who hears the chapter as permission to stop working.","Silk copy B has 聞道者 where the globe has 為道: the one who has heard the Way loses daily. That ties the line to the hearers of chapter 41. The best of them practises, and this is the practice.","The Guodian slips stop at 亡不為. The lines about winning the world are a separate saying, with a twin in chapter 57. The globe’s break falls on the seam."],"di":["The second passage is addressed to someone who wants the world. 取天下 is the vocabulary of conquest. Not-meddling is offered as the way to win, which is a strange advertisement for a teaching about wanting less. Chapter 29 said the same from the other side: try to take the world by acting on it, and it cannot be done.","The book is consistent on the mechanism. It is silent on whether the wish is a good one. A reader may take the first passage and decline the second.","The first passage has a risk of its own. “Lose something every day” has served as a slogan against study, and the chapter’s title, 忘知, forgetting knowledge, leans that way. The text does not. It asks for a second labour after the first.","Any craft shows both. There are years of adding technique, and then years of taking out what the work does not need. The second stage cannot be had first, and it does not look like progress from outside."]},"49":{"lead":"Chapter 49 describes a ruler with no mind of his own, who is good to the bad and trusts the untrustworthy. Its last line can be heard as tenderness or as condescension.","ex":["聖人無常心. 常 is constant and 心 is the heart that thinks. “The sage has no fixed heart.” 以百姓心為心: 百姓, the hundred surnames, are the people. “The people’s hearts are the sage’s heart.”","善者吾善之，不善者吾亦善之. “To the good, I am good. To those who aren’t good, I am good too.” 德善 follows, and the globe reads it as a noun phrase: “That is the goodness of virtue.” Antiquity glossed 德 with 得, to get, and some editions print 得善: in this way goodness is got. The same pattern is then run with 信, trust.","聖人在天下歙歙. 歙 is to draw in, the first verb of chapter 36. The English: “The sage lives in the world quietly, drawing in”. 為天下渾其心: 渾 is muddy, unsorted. For the world’s sake he muddies his heart. Wang Bi’s commentary says the heart has no master, and the English takes the mud as impartiality: “a heart that doesn’t take sides.”","百姓皆注其耳目. 注 is to pour or fix. “The people all strain their eyes and ears.”","聖人皆孩之. “The sage treats them all like children.” 孩 is used as a verb, and it is the word chapter 20 used for a baby’s smile."],"an":["The first line is stronger in silk copy B: 恆無心. The same two ideas stand in a different order. The received text says the sage has no fixed heart. The silk says he is always without one. The silk fits the next line better: having none of his own, he takes theirs.","The middle passage draws a consequence from chapter 17, which the globe renders “Trust too little, and you won’t be trusted.” If distrust produces the untrustworthy, the ruler must trust first and regardless. Chapter 27 said no one is thrown away. Chapter 63 will say to answer resentment with virtue.","The silk has no 吾 in these lines. The first person, four times on the globe, came into the text later.","The last passage sets two kinds of attention against each other. The people sharpen eyes and ears, sorting and comparing. The sage blurs his heart. The chapter treats discrimination as the people’s habit and its absence as his work.","Three readings of 孩之 are possible. He treats them as children. He makes children of them, returning them to a simpler state. Or he smiles on them all as a child would: the first silk copy writes 咳, the infant’s laugh. The globe takes the first.","There is no 道 in the chapter. The globe breaks it twice, into heart, conduct and bearing."],"di":["Confucius was asked about exactly this. Someone proposed answering resentment with virtue. He replied: then what will you answer virtue with? Answer resentment with straightness, and virtue with virtue.","The objection is about fairness. If the bad receive what the good receive, goodness goes unanswered. The chapter’s reply is that the sage is not paying anyone. His goodness is not a wage, so it cannot be owed more to one than to another.","As policy the idea has two names, amnesty and impunity, and the chapter does not say which it means. Chapter 27’s wording suggests the first: no one is written off. It does not say no one is stopped.","“The people’s hearts are the sage’s heart” sounds like government by consent. The last line does not. A ruler who treats all his subjects as children has decided something about them, and chapter 65 confirms the strand: the ancients, the globe says there, “kept them simple.”","The third reading of 孩 changes the politics. If the sage is the one who is childlike, the people strain and he smiles. That is chapter 20’s speaker, the baby who has not yet learned to smile while everyone else is at the feast. The character allows it. The globe’s choice is the more usual one, and the harder to like."]},"50":{"lead":"Chapter 50 says that many people die of how hard they try to live. Then it reports a rumour of someone in whom death finds nowhere to land.","ex":["出生入死: out into life, in to death. “Coming out is life. Going in is death.” In modern Chinese the four characters are an idiom for risking one’s life. Here they only name two directions.","生之徒十有三，死之徒十有三. 徒 is a follower, one of a company. “Three in ten follow life. Three in ten follow death.”","人之生，動之死地，十有三. Wang Bi’s text has no 亦, “also”, here. A third group is alive, and its every movement goes toward 死地, the death-ground. “And three in ten, alive, move toward the place of death.”","夫何故？以其生生之厚. 生生 is living one’s living, and 厚 is thick. They lay life on too thick. “Why? Because they cling to living too hard.”","蓋聞 marks hearsay: “I have heard”. 善攝生者 are those good at tending life. On land they do not meet 兕 or tiger. 兕 is a horned beast, rhinoceros or wild ox. In the army no weapon touches them.","Three clauses say where the danger fails. The horn, the claws and the blade each find 無所, nowhere. 以其無死地: “Because there is no place of death in them.”"],"an":["The sum is nine. Three, three and three leave one in ten uncounted. Commentators assign the tenth to those who are good at tending life. The chapter does not say so.","Han Feizi counts differently. He reads 十有三 as thirteen: four limbs and nine openings, the same thirteen serving life and death alike. The grammar allows both. The globe follows Wang Bi.","The third group is the chapter’s point. These are not people fated to die young. They walk there, and the cause given is an excess of living. Chapter 75 uses nearly the same phrase of rulers, 求生之厚, and ends by ranking those who make nothing of life above those who 貴生, prize it.","貴生 is this chapter’s title. Either the title names what the chapter warns against, or it means that life is to be valued by not squeezing it.","The silk copies sharpen two things. They put 生生 into the description of the third group and give the reason without 厚: because they live for living. And where the received text says the adept does not meet rhinoceros and tiger, silk copy B says he does not avoid them. The received version makes him protected. The silk makes him unafraid.","The globe breaks the chapter before 蓋聞, between the count and the rumour. 生 comes six times."],"di":["Taken literally the second passage is false, and later Daoism took it literally. There were talismans for entering the mountains. Tigers are not moved by equanimity.","A soberer reading starts from 死地. It is a soldier’s term for ground on which an army must fight or die. A person with no death-ground has not put himself where the blow lands. Horn, claw and blade need a place to enter, and the third group spends its life offering one.","The first passage needs no rescue. A rough third of people shorten their lives by the effort to enlarge them. The tradition that revered this book supplied its own proof: several Tang emperors died of elixirs taken to live longer.","The count ends at ten in ten for everyone. The chapter does not promise otherwise. 蓋聞 is an honest hedge, and “no place of death” is said of the living."]},"51":{"lead":"Chapter 51 gives the book’s plainest account of how the Way and virtue divide the work. It defines the highest virtue as raising what one does not own.","ex":["Four verbs have four subjects. 道生之: “The Way gives them life.” 德畜之: “Virtue raises them.” 畜 is to rear, as one rears livestock. 物形之: “Matter shapes them.” 勢成之: “Circumstance completes them.” 勢 is the lie of things, the force of a situation.","是以萬物莫不尊道而貴德. All things honour the Way and value virtue. 夫莫之命常自然: “not because anyone orders it but because it is so by itself.”","Then eight verbs in a row. The Way gives life and virtue raises; then growing, nurturing, 亭, 毒, feeding, covering. 亭之毒之 is a famous pair of hard words. 亭 is to settle or shelter. 毒 is ordinarily poison, and is read here as ripening or making firm. The globe: “shelters them, ripens them”. The Daozang text prints the plain equivalents, 成之熟之.","Three refusals close the list. 生而不有，為而不恃，長而不宰: “Giving life without owning, acting without leaning on it, leading without ruling”. 宰 is to carve meat, and from that to preside. 是謂玄德: “this is called dark virtue.”"],"an":["The first line is a division of labour. The Way is what a thing comes from. Virtue is what keeps it going afterwards, its own share of the Way. The globe’s note on 德 says the same: “the power a thing has when it follows its own nature”.","之 comes fifteen times in seventy-two characters. Nearly every verb takes the same small object, “them”. The chapter is a string of verbs of care and one pronoun.","The honour is unforced. Where the globe has 命, command, the silk copies and the Daozang text have 爵, to confer rank. That is the sharper word. Way and virtue are honoured though no one gave them a title, which is how they differ from lords.","Where the globe has 勢, the silk has 器: vessels complete them. A thing is finished by becoming a tool in use, as chapter 28’s uncarved wood becomes vessels. 勢 is a word the Legalists made their own, and it looks like the later reading.","The silk copies and the Daozang text also lack the second 德. In their second list the Way does all eight things. The globe’s text names virtue again and so keeps mother and nurse apart.","The three refusals also end chapter 10, where the globe prints them in brackets, and the first two stand in chapter 2. Here they are at home, since they sum up the verbs before them. 玄德 returns in chapter 65."],"di":["The refusals describe the stance of anyone who makes a thing that goes on to have its own life: a parent, a teacher, a gardener, a founder. The first two are hard. The third is where care usually fails. 長而不宰 is to be the elder and not the carver.","One objection is to the cosmology. Things do not honour anything. 尊 and 貴 are court words. But the chapter borrows the language of rank only to cancel it: no one conferred this rank. The cancellation is the point.","The harder objection is practical. Owning nothing and taking no credit is easy for a Way that has no needs. A human carer has them. 玄德 is called dark because it goes unseen, and unseen care is also unpaid care. The chapter praises it. A reader may still ask who gains when the best care is defined as the kind that claims nothing.","Chapter 34 answers in part. The Way there feeds the ten thousand things and does not act as their master, and because it never thinks itself great, it becomes great. The refusals are offered as what makes the work last, and not only as what makes it modest."]},"52":{"lead":"Chapter 52 gives a method in three parts: know things from their source, keep the senses from leaking, and bring outward light back to inward clarity.","ex":["天下有始，以為天下母. “The world has a beginning, and it is the mother of the world.” 既知其母，復知其子, in Wang Bi’s Daozang text: “Know the mother, and come to know the children.” Most editions read 既得其母，以知其子: find the mother, and so know the children. 既知其子，復守其母: 復 is back, again. “Know the children, and keep to the mother”.","沒身不殆 is also the last line of chapter 16. The globe gives both the same English: “until your body ends, nothing can harm you.”","塞其兌，閉其門. 兌 is an opening, and in the Changes it is the sign of the mouth. “Close the openings, shut the doors”. 終身不勤: 勤 is toil and wear. “all your life you won’t be worn out.”","開其兌，濟其事. 濟 is to carry across, to see a thing through. “Open the openings, get busy with things”. 終身不救: “all your life you can’t be saved.”","見小曰明，守柔曰強. “Seeing the small is called clarity. Keeping to the soft is called strength.”","用其光，復歸其明. 光 is light given off. 明 is clarity within. “Use the light, come back to clarity”. 無遺身殃: leave the body no calamity. 是謂習常: “This is called practising the constant.”"],"an":["The globe’s three passages are three kinds of statement: a genealogy, a discipline and a pair of definitions.","The Guodian slips have only the middle one. It travelled alone, a two-line instruction with a promise and a threat. Chapter 56 repeats its first six characters, and the globe gives them the same English there. The breaks on the globe fall on the seams of that history.","The first passage moves twice. Down, from the mother to the children: things are known by their source. Then back: knowing the children, return and keep the mother. This is the premise chapter 47 needed. The world can be known indoors by someone who holds its beginning.","The second passage treats the senses as exits. What leaves through them is one’s own store. 不勤 is the phrase chapter 6 used of the valley spirit, whose use never wears it out.","The third passage redefines two words. Clarity is seeing what is small, which is chapter 36’s subtle clarity. Strength is keeping to the soft. In chapter 55 the same character 強 is a fault, and the globe renders it “forcing”. The book uses the word both ways and here reclaims it.","One word here records an imperial taboo. Both silk copies write 啟 where the received text has 開. 啟 was the personal name of the Han emperor Jing, and later copies avoided it. Chapter 54 has a second case.","The last words differ as well. Silk copy A and the Daozang text have 襲常: to wear the constant, or to inherit it. The globe has 習, to practise. Chapter 27 had 襲明, the globe’s “following the light”."],"di":["“Close the openings” has been read as a meditation instruction and as advice to a ruler to give nothing away. As a rule of life it meets chapter 47’s objection again: a person with shut senses learns nothing. The chapter’s own wording limits it. 用其光 says to use the light. The doors are shut against leaking, not against the world.","The Confucian objection is in 濟其事. To see affairs through is what a responsible person does. The globe’s “get busy” tilts the phrase toward fuss, and the Chinese is neutral, even honourable. That makes the claim harder. The chapter writes off accomplishment as a way of life, and not only busyness.","The second passage can be tested. Attention is finite and leaves through the openings. Anyone can note what is left at the end of a day spent with every door open. The chapter’s word for that state is 勤, worn.","The first passage keeps the teaching from becoming a refusal of the many. It says to know the children. Then it says where to stand while knowing them."]},"53":{"lead":"Chapter 53 is the angriest in the book. It looks at a polished court and empty granaries, and calls the people in fine clothes thieves.","ex":["使我介然有知. 介然 is slight, a speck. “If I had the least bit of sense”. 行於大道，惟施是畏. 施 is read as 迤, to slant off. “I’d walk the great Way and fear only straying from it.” Silk copy B supports the reading. An older one takes 施 as acting or showing: what I would fear is doing anything.","大道甚夷，而民好徑. 夷 is level and 徑 is a footpath, a shortcut. “The great Way is very smooth, but people love the side paths.” Chapter 41 used the same 夷 and warned that the level Way seems knotted.","Then three observations with 甚, “very”. 朝甚除: the court is very 除, swept and kept up. “The court is spotless”. 田甚蕪: “the fields are full of weeds”. 倉甚虛: “the granaries are empty.”","Four more describe persons. 服文綵: patterned silks. 帶利劍: sharp swords at the belt. 厭飲食: glutted with drink and food. 財貨有餘: goods to spare.","是謂盜夸. 夸 is boasting, display. “This is called the bragging of thieves.” 非道也哉: “It is not the Way!”"],"an":["The second passage is an inventory, and the title, 益證, calls it evidence. Three things anyone can see, then four more, and the verdict held to the end. Chapter 46 argued the same way from horses.","The cause is not stated, and it is plain. The court is spotless because the granaries are empty. Chapter 75 says it outright: the people are hungry because those above eat too much in taxes. Chapter 77 reuses this chapter’s 有餘 for those who have too much and are given more.","The last two lines turn on a pun. 盜, thief, and 道, the Way, sound alike. Thieves' boasting: not the Way.","Han Feizi’s text has 盜竽. The 竽 is the reed organ that leads an ensemble. When it sounds, the other instruments follow. The great thieves set the tune and the small ones join. It is a better figure than boasting, and it has a certain claim on a globe that turns the book into music.","The silk has 甚 a fifth time: the people very much love shortcuts. The globe’s 而民好徑 is milder. Some editors change 民 to 人, so that the men at court are meant. As it stands, nobody takes the level road, high or low.","The chapter speaks as 我, in the first person, and ends on 哉, an exclamation that six chapters use. The globe breaks it before 朝甚除, between the road and the inventory."],"di":["The tone is at odds with chapter 49, four chapters back, which was good to the not-good. Both are in the book. They can be held together. Chapter 49 describes a stance toward persons: no one is thrown away. Chapter 53 describes an arrangement and names it. One can refuse to write off thieves and still call theft theft.","Is it fair? A swept court is no crime. The evidence is the conjunction, polish beside weeds. That gives a test that still works on any institution: compare the lobby with the storeroom.","The first line protects the last. “If I had the least bit of sense” is self-mockery. The speaker does not claim to walk the road, only to know what he would fear on it. The accusation comes from someone who has just admitted he may be on a side path himself.","Mencius drew up the same charge: fat meat in the kitchens, fat horses in the stables, and the people with the look of hunger. On the inventory the schools agree. They part at the remedy. Mencius wants a better king. This book wants a court that stops wanting."]},"54":{"lead":"Chapter 54 carries virtue from one body to the whole world in five steps. Then it insists that each scale be looked at by its own measure.","ex":["善建者不拔，善抱者不脫. 建 is to set up or plant and 拔 is to uproot. 抱 is to hold in the arms and 脫 is to slip off. The Chinese speaks of persons: the good planter, the good holder. The globe speaks of things: “What is well planted can’t be pulled up. What is well held can’t slip away.”","子孫以祭祀不輟. By this, sons and grandsons will not break off the sacrifices. 祭祀 are the offerings to ancestors. Lasting meant an unbroken line of descendants at the altar. The globe’s “Children and grandchildren will honour it without end” keeps the lasting and lets the altar go.","Five scales follow: 身 the body or self, 家 the household, 鄉 the village, 邦 the state, 天下 the world. Virtue cultivated at each gets its own word. 真 is real. 餘 is surplus, the globe’s “overflows”. 長 is long: “lasts”. 豐 is “abundant”. 普 is “everywhere”. The globe’s Chinese drops one comma, in the fourth of the five lines.","故以身觀身. 觀 is to look at steadily, the verb of chapters 1 and 16. “So look at a self through a self, a family through a family”, and so on up.","吾何以知天下然哉？以此. “How do I know the world is like this? By this.” It is the second of three chapters to answer that question with 以此."],"an":["The five lines rhyme in pairs: 身 with 真, 家 with 餘, 鄉 with 長, 邦 with 豐, 下 with 普. The fourth pair settles a question of text. 邦 rhymes with 豐. 國, which the Daozang text prints, does not.","國 is a Han substitution. 邦 was the personal name of the dynasty’s founder, Liu Bang, and copyists avoided it. Silk copy A has 邦, copy B has 國, and the Guodian slips, older than the dynasty, have 邦.","On the globe this is the only chapter that says 邦. Its Chinese has 國 in ten others. An editor restored the old word where the rhyme demanded it, and nowhere else.","The two lists follow two logics. The first is continuous: one cultivation, wider and wider. The second separates. A family is to be read as a family and a state as a state. The Guanzi gives the rule in the negative: run a village as a household and the village cannot be run; run a state as a village and the state cannot be run.","The Confucian ladder in the Great Learning climbs the same way, from the person to the family, the state and the world. There each rung is the means to the next, and each is governed. Here nothing is governed. Virtue is cultivated at each scale and each is then observed. The village, 鄉, is a rung the Confucian ladder does not have.","以此, “by this”, points to what is at hand, and the nearest scale is the body. That is the answer chapter 47 left open.","The globe breaks the chapter twice: roots, scales, observations. 德 comes five times, and there is no 道 and no 無."],"di":["The first three lines promise permanence through descendants. That belongs to one society’s idea of lasting. A reader without that altar can keep the structure: what is well founded is measured in generations and not in the founder’s lifetime.","The second list is the sharper teaching. Much political error is error of scale. The state is imagined as a household with the ruler as father. The world is imagined as one’s own country enlarged. “A country through a country” refuses the family metaphor, on which most Chinese political thought ran.","The lists seem to pull apart. If virtue carries from body to world, why may one scale not be read through another? What carries is 之, “it”, the thing cultivated. What does not carry is the measure. That is why each scale gets a different adjective.","The smallest scale gets the strongest one. Only in one person can virtue be checked for being 真, real. Beyond that it is counted by amount and reach. The chapter’s foundation is the body, as it was in chapter 44."]},"55":{"lead":"Chapter 55 takes a newborn as the measure of virtue held in full. It reads the baby’s grip, its arousal and its tireless cry as signs of undivided energy.","ex":["含德之厚，比於赤子. 含 is to hold inside, as in the mouth. 厚 is thick. 赤子 is the “red child”, the newborn, and the word occurs only here. “Whoever is full of virtue is like a newborn.”","Three clauses of safety follow. 蜂蠆虺蛇不螫: wasps, scorpions, vipers and snakes do not sting it; fierce beasts do not seize it; birds of prey do not strike. That is Wang Bi’s list. Heshang Gong has 毒蟲, venomous creatures.","Then three observations. 骨弱筋柔而握固: “Its bones are soft, its muscles weak, yet its grip is firm.” 未知牝牡之合而全作: it knows nothing of female and male joining, yet 全作, its wholeness rises. Wang Bi glosses 作 as growth: nothing harms it, so it grows whole. 精之至也: 精 is seed and vital essence. “its life force is complete.”","終日號而不嗄. It howls all day and does not go hoarse. 和之至也: “its harmony is complete.”","The second passage is four definitions and a verdict. 知和曰常，知常曰明: “Knowing harmony is called the constant. Knowing the constant is called clarity.” 益生曰祥: adding to life is called 祥, an omen. 心使氣曰強: “The mind driving the breath is called forcing.”","The last three lines repeat the end of chapter 30 word for word, and the globe gives them the same English: “Things that peak grow old.”"],"an":["The three observations are accurate. A newborn’s grasp is strong enough to bear weight. In the other editions the second observation is an infant’s erection, which is ordinary physiology; Wang Bi’s text turns it into growth. A baby can cry for hours on a voice an adult would soon lose. The chapter looked at real infants.","What it reads off them is economy. Nothing in the baby works against anything else. The grip is firm because no second thought loosens it. The cry lasts because nothing in the throat is forced. 精 and 和 name the two sides of this: full charge, and no internal friction.","The second passage defines failure as the opposite. 益生 is adding to life, the mistake of chapter 50’s third group. 心使氣 is the mind ordering the breath about. In both, one part of the person commandeers the rest.","Two words are used against their usual sense. 祥 normally means a good omen. Elsewhere on the globe it appears only as 不祥, inauspicious. Here it stands alone and must mean a bad sign, and the globe says “ill-omened”. 強 was reclaimed as “strength” in chapter 52. Here it is the fault again.","The chain of definitions is looser in the early copies. The first silk copy has 和曰常，知和曰明: harmony is called the constant, and knowing harmony is called clarity. The received text has adjusted the lines to match chapter 16, where knowing the constant is clarity.","Here the editions scatter. Wang Bi’s text has 全, wholeness. Heshang Gong and Fu Yi have 朘, the infant’s penis: it rises. The silk has 朘怒, blunter still: the penis is roused. The Guodian slips have the chapter. The globe breaks it before 知和, between the infant and the definitions."],"di":["The safety clauses are false. Wasps sting babies. The chapter is describing an adult, 比於, “comparable to” a newborn, and the claim is the one chapter 50 made about the adept: there is nowhere for the blow to land. A person with no forced part offers little to provoke.","The harder objection is that the infant is helpless. Its unity is bought by doing nothing for itself. An ideal modelled on it can become a wish to be carried. The chapter’s answer is in what it selects. It praises the grip and the cry, the baby’s strengths. It says nothing of being fed.","The globe’s note calls the newborn “the book’s picture of full life: soft, whole, unforced”. The last word is the working one. 強, forcing, is what the chapter condemns, and 物壯則老 gives the reason. Whatever is pushed to its peak has started to age.","There is no 無 in the chapter. On the globe it runs through without a rest, which suits a text about a cry that does not break."]},"56":{"lead":"Chapter 56 moves from a famous paradox about speech to a practice of six verbs. It ends with a person nobody can get a hold on.","ex":["知者不言，言者不知. “Those who know don’t talk. Those who talk don’t know.”","Six short commands follow. The first two repeat chapter 52: “Close the openings, shut the doors”. The next four also stand in chapter 4, where the English gives them to the Way as its own acts. Here they are instructions: “blunt the sharp, dissolve the divisions, soften the glare, become one with the dust.” Wang Bi’s text reads 解其分 here, where chapter 4 has 解其紛; his commentary: remove the source of contention.","和其光，同其塵 has shrunk into an idiom, 和光同塵: to dim one’s light and move among ordinary people.","是謂玄同. 玄 is the dark of the night sky and 同 is sameness. “This is called the dark oneness.”","Then six impossibilities in three pairs, each built on 不可得而, “cannot be got at so as to”. Such a person cannot be made an intimate, 親, or a stranger, 疏. Cannot be profited or harmed. Cannot be raised or lowered. The globe makes it second person: “Then no one can get close to you, and no one can push you away.”","故為天下貴. “So you are the treasure of the world.” Chapter 62 ends on the same five characters."],"an":["The three passages on the globe are a thesis, a method and a result. The method works on four things a person shows: an edge, a tangle, a shine, a difference from the dust. Each is reduced. What is left gives others no handle, and the third passage lists the handles that are gone.","The order of the six commands differs in both silk copies. They put light and dust before the sharp and the knotted. The received order follows chapter 4.","The first line is narrower in the Guodian slips: 知之者弗言，言之者弗知. One who knows it does not speak of it; one who speaks of it does not know it. With the pronoun the line is about a particular knowledge. It is not a rule that the wise are mute.","The title is 玄德, dark virtue. The phrase is not in the chapter, which has 玄同 instead. There is no 德 in it at all, and no 道.","Guodian keeps chapters 56 and 57 together, in that order, as the globe does. The private practice is followed directly by its politics."],"di":["The first line has a classic objection, and a Tang poet put it best. Bai Juyi wrote that he had heard from Laozi that those who speak do not know. If Laozi was one who knew, he asked, how did he come to write five thousand characters?","There are three replies. The Guodian wording is one: the line is about speaking of it, and the book mostly talks around it. A second is chapter 1, which says at the start that the Way that can be spoken is not the constant one. The book declares its own limit before the poet does. The third is to accept the charge. The globe’s note calls this “a book of words that distrusts words”, and that is accurate.","The result has a cost that the chapter states as a gain. A person who cannot be harmed cannot be helped. One who cannot be pushed away cannot be drawn close. 不可得而親 rules out intimacy along with injury. That is a fair description of some people one admires and cannot reach.","同其塵 saves the chapter from the hermit’s reading. The doors are shut and the light is dimmed, and then the person is in the dust with everyone else. The oneness is with the world, and what is given up is standing out in it."]},"57":{"lead":"Chapter 57 argues that every added control produces the disorder it was meant to cure. Then it lets a ruler say what happens when he stops.","ex":["以正治國，以奇用兵，以無事取天下. 正 is the straight and regular. 奇 is the odd, the irregular, the surprise. The pair is a soldier’s: engage with the regular, win with the irregular. “Govern a country by the straight, fight a war by surprise, win the world by leaving it alone.”","吾何以知其然哉？以此. “How do I know it is so? By this”. It is the third and last use of the formula, and the only one where “this” is then spelled out. The globe marks it with a colon.","Four observations follow, each with 多, “many”, and a result that grows. 忌諱 are prohibitions and taboos: the more of them, the poorer the people. 利器 are sharp tools or weapons: the more the people have, the more muddled the state. 伎巧 are skills and clever tricks: the more of them, the more 奇物, strange things, appear. 法令 are laws and orders: “The more laws are posted, the more thieves there are.”","故聖人云 introduces a quotation in four lines, each beginning 我. 我無為而民自化: “I do nothing, and the people change by themselves.” Stillness gives 自正, straightening themselves. No business gives 自富, growing rich. No wanting gives 自樸: the people “become plain as uncarved wood.”"],"an":["The first line ranks three methods by their objects. The straight is for a state and the crooked for a battle. For the world neither will do, and the third method is the absence of method. Chapter 48 said so in the same words.","The second passage is offered as evidence. Each line names a tool of order and reports the opposite of its purpose. The mechanism is not given, but it can be supplied. Prohibitions close off livelihoods. A law defines a new offence and with it a new offender.","The fourth line is the most quoted and the least secure. Heshang Gong has 法物, fine goods, where the globe has 法令, and what survives of silk copy B agrees with him. The more fine things are displayed, the more thieves there are. That is chapter 3’s thought, where not prizing rare goods keeps people from stealing. It is a line about luxury. The reading with laws is old all the same. Sima Qian quotes it that way at the head of his chapter on harsh officials.","The silk copies also lack 以此. The question is followed at once by the four observations. And silk copy B ends 我欲不欲: I want not to want. The globe’s 無欲 is the smoother, later form. Chapter 64 keeps the older phrase.","The four 自 answer chapter 37, which promised that things would 自化. That chapter held a weight in reserve for when wanting stirred. Here nothing is held in reserve, and the people grow rich besides. It is the only time the book promises that."],"di":["The evidence runs both ways. Tacitus observed that laws were most numerous when the state was most corrupt. He read it as a symptom. The chapter reads it as a cause. Thieves multiply laws at least as surely as laws multiply thieves, and the text gives no way to tell.","The second line is the one that dates. “The more sharp tools the people have, the more confused the country” is a ruler’s worry about an armed or equipped population. Chapter 36 wanted the state’s sharp tools hidden. Chapter 80 will let tools go unused. The book is consistent here, and it is not the part most readers keep.","The sage’s four sentences are testimony, and what they claim is specific. He does not say the people became good. He says they changed, straightened, prospered and grew plain “by themselves”. The whole chapter rests on that phrase, and it is the claim that an experiment could test.","One such experiment was run. The early Han cut back the Qin’s laws and levies and governed on this text for two generations, and the granaries filled. Then the next emperor spent what had been saved."]},"58":{"lead":"Chapter 58 says that fortune and misfortune rest on each other and that the right keeps turning into the strange. Its answer is to hold a quality without its edge.","ex":["Four doubled words open the chapter. 其政悶悶，其民淳淳: when the government is 悶悶, shut-in and dull, the people are 淳淳, whole and plain. “When government is dull and quiet, the people are simple and honest.” 其政察察，其民缺缺: when it is 察察, inspecting everything, they are 缺缺, lacking. Wang Bi: the people split into factions and compete. “When government is sharp and prying, the people come apart.”","悶悶 and 察察 come from chapter 20. There the speaker called himself 悶悶 while everyone else was 察察, and the globe used the same two English words, “dull” and “sharp”. The fool’s self-portrait has become a programme for government.","禍兮福之所倚，福兮禍之所伏. 倚 is to lean and 伏 is to crouch hidden. “Disaster is what good fortune leans on. Good fortune is where disaster hides.”","孰知其極？其無正. “Who knows where it ends? There is no fixed right.” 正復為奇，善復為妖: the straight turns back into the odd, and the good into the uncanny. 正 and 奇 are the pair from the first line of chapter 57.","人之迷，其日固久. “People have been confused about this for a very long time.”","Four lines on the sage close it, each a quality and a refusal. 方而不割: “square but doesn’t cut”. 廉而不劌: “sharp but doesn’t wound”. 直而不肆: “straight but not reckless”. 光而不耀: “bright but doesn’t dazzle.”"],"an":["The three passages are a political observation, a law and a way of living under the law.","The law is chapter 40’s reversal, applied twice. First to events: what looks like luck is carrying its opposite. Then to standards: what counts as straight today is the oddity of tomorrow. The second application is the bolder. It is one thing to say fortunes change. It is another to say that the measure changes.","The last passage is the response, and it is exact. If every quality tends to turn into its opposite, the turning starts at the edge. A square has corners, and corners cut, and what cuts gets resisted. So keep the square and drop the cutting. Chapter 41 said the great square has no corners.","The first passage belongs with the others for the same reason. 察察 government is government with an edge. People who are inspected learn to present, and 缺缺 is the result: something is missing in them. The dull government has the quality, order, without the edge.","The silk has no sage in the last passage. It reads 是以方而不割, “and so: square but not cutting”, as a general maxim. It also confirms 直 in the third line.","The Daozang text ends the question with a particle, 其無正邪: is there then no standard? The globe’s text makes it a statement. The question is the more careful form."],"di":["The proverbial illustration is the old man at the frontier whose horse ran off. It came back with a second horse; his son rode it and broke his leg; the broken leg kept the son out of a war. The story is usually told to console. Told that way it turns the chapter into a shrug.","The chapter does not shrug. It asks 孰知其極, who knows where it ends, and the honest answer is that no one does. That is a reason for care with verdicts. It is no reason to stop caring which way things go.","“There is no fixed right” is a dangerous line as the globe has it. The last passage shows it is not relativism. The sage is square, incorruptible, straight and bright. Four standards are affirmed in four lines. What is denied is that a standard stays right when it is used as a blade.","The first passage has acquired a modern application. Institutions that measure everything find that people start working for the measurement. The Chinese has had the word for the result for a very long time: 缺缺."]},"59":{"lead":"Chapter 59 names one policy for ruling people and serving heaven, which is to spend little. It follows that policy link by link to a state that lasts.","ex":["治人，事天，莫若嗇. 嗇 is sparingness. The character began with the harvest: to gather grain in and store it. “In governing people and serving heaven, nothing beats restraint.”","A chain follows, each link picking up the last words of the one before. 嗇 leads to 早服. 服 is to submit or to make ready, and the globe takes the first: “Restraint means yielding early.” 早服 is 重積德, piling virtue up thick: “storing up virtue”.","The next link: 重積德則無不克, store up virtue and nothing is not overcome. The following clause repeats the phrase. (The first version of the globe had 為 for 無 here, a slip now mended.)","無不克則莫知其極: “no one knows your limits.” Then 可以有國, “you can hold a country”, and 有國之母，可以長久: “Hold the mother of the country, and you can last.”","是謂深根固柢. 根 is the spreading root and 柢 is the taproot. “This is called deep roots and a firm base”. 長生久視之道: “the Way of long life and lasting sight.”"],"an":["The chapter is one sentence in six links. Its logic is a farmer’s. What is not spent accumulates. What accumulates gives a margin. A margin that no one can measure is security. The image at the end, roots, is where the first word began.","嗇 has two objects, and commentators divide on them. Han Feizi reads it of the person: be sparing of spirit and of thought. Others read it of the state: be sparing of the people’s labour and goods. The opening names both fields, people and heaven, and the chapter does not choose.","Mending that slip, as in chapter 39, gives the globe back a rest. 無 is sung as silence, and this chapter now has two.","極 links this chapter to the last. Chapter 58 asked who knows where the turning ends. Chapter 59 describes a ruler whose own limit no one knows. The unknowable has moved from the world to the person.","The Daozang text has 早復, returning early, for 早服. The silk agrees with the globe. The Guodian slips have the chapter, so the chain is old.","The globe gives it as one passage. Its last character is 道, so on the globe the chapter closes on the bowl."],"di":["長生久視 was later read as a promise of bodily immortality, and four characters of this chapter became a programme of breathing, diet and elixirs. The chapter promises less. 可以長久 is what chapter 44 offered the person who knows when to stop: you can last. Here it is said of a state.","Thrift as a principle of rule has an obvious abuse. A court can be sparing with the people’s welfare and call it 嗇. The text guards against this only by its direction. The sparing is of the ruler’s own spending and doing, and the store built up is 德, which cannot be taxed out of anyone.","“No one knows your limits” is the uncomfortable link. It is chapter 36’s hidden instrument again: security through being unreadable. A reader can admire deep roots and still notice that the chapter wants them out of sight.","積 raises a question the book answers at the very end. Chapter 81 says the sage does not hoard. The verb is the same. The difference is the thing stored. Virtue is the one store that grows by being given away, and chapter 81 says so."]},"60":{"lead":"Chapter 60 gives the book’s most quoted line on government, about a small fish. Then it gives its least quoted: where rule follows the Way, the ghosts stop doing harm.","ex":["治大國，若烹小鮮. 烹 is to cook and 小鮮 is small fish. “Govern a great country the way you cook a small fish.”","The line does not say what the way is. The commentators agree on it. Han Feizi: turn small fish often in the pan and you ruin them, and change a great state’s laws often and the people suffer. Heshang Gong adds that one does not gut or scale them, and does not dare stir, for fear they fall apart.","以道蒞天下，其鬼不神. 蒞 is to preside over. 鬼 are the ghosts of the dead. 神 is numinous power, used here as a verb: to work as a spirit, to haunt. “Rule the world by the Way, and ghosts lose their power.”","The sentence then corrects itself twice. 非其鬼不神，其神不傷人: “It isn’t that ghosts lose their power: their power doesn’t harm people.” 非其神不傷人，聖人亦不傷人: it is not only that; “the sage doesn’t harm people either.”","夫兩不相傷，故德交歸焉. The two do not harm each other, so virtue comes home on both sides. “When neither harms the other, virtue flows back to both.”"],"an":["The two passages are one thought. The cook who does not stir and the ruler who does not harm are the same person. What rises from the bottom of a stirred pot, in the second passage, is the dead.","The self-corrections matter. The chapter does not deny that there are ghosts or that they have power. It moves the cause. Ghosts harm where people are already being harmed. Remove the harm done by the living ruler and the harm done by the dead stops with it.","Who are “the two”? The grammar allows ghosts and sage, or ghosts and people, or sage and people. Han Feizi takes the last: ruler and people do not injure each other. On any reading the claim is the same. Harm circulates, and so does its absence.","These are the only ghosts in the book. Elsewhere 神 is the valley spirit of chapter 6, the world as a sacred vessel in chapter 29, and the spirits who found the One in chapter 39. Only here is it something to be feared.","The Daozang text has 民, the people, for 人 in all three places. The silk agrees with the globe. In silk copy B the sage’s clause has no object: he, too, does no harm.","The globe breaks the chapter before 以道蒞, between the fish and the ghosts."],"di":["The fish has had a long career. An American president quoted it to Congress in 1988 as an argument for smaller government. That is the usual use: the line as a case for doing less.","It makes a smaller claim than that. A fish left alone on the fire burns. The cook is still cooking, with attention and low heat and few movements. The line asks for a light hand and a steady one, and it concedes that government is a craft with a result.","The ghost passage asks more of a modern reader, and repays it. Take the ghosts as what a society’s dead leave behind when they were wronged: grievance that outlives its occasion and returns. The chapter says such things lose their force under a rule that harms no one now. That is at least arguable. Old injuries are most dangerous where new ones keep them fed.","What the chapter leaves out is repair. It says that harm stops circulating when no one adds to it. It does not say the dead are owed anything. A reader may think they are."]},"61":{"lead":"Chapter 61 applies the low position to relations between states. Its advice is to the strong: be the one who stoops.","ex":["大國者下流. 下流 is the lower reach of a river, where the waters gather. “A great country is the low ground where the rivers run”. 天下之交 is the meeting place of the world. 天下之牝 is the female of the world.","牝常以靜勝牡，以靜為下. 牝 and 牡 are female and male animals. “The female always overcomes the male by stillness, by being still and lying low.” The image is of mating, stated without comment.","Two conditionals follow. A great state that puts itself below a small one 取, takes, the small one. A small state that puts itself below a great one 取 the great one. The globe tells them apart by a preposition: the great “wins the small one”; the small “wins over the great one.”","故或下以取，或下而取: “So one lowers itself to win, the other is low and wins.”","Then the interests are named. 大國不過欲兼畜人: the great state wants no more than to 兼, bring together, and 畜, keep, people. 畜 is the verb for rearing livestock, and for what virtue did in chapter 51. The globe softens both: “to gather and care for people.” 小國不過欲入事人: the small one wants “to join and serve.”","夫兩者各得其所欲，大者宜為下. “For both to get what they want, the great one should lie low.”"],"an":["The chapter is frank about ends. No one in it is asked to stop wanting. The great state wants more people under it, and the small one wants a protector. The teaching concerns the means only, and it is the book’s usual one. The low place receives.","The asymmetry of the two conditionals is clearer in the silk. There the small state 取於 the great: it is taken in by it. The received text dropped a word and left two identical verbs. The summary line preserves the difference: one lowers itself in order to take, the other lowers itself and is taken.","Why must the great one stoop, if both gain? Because it is the one that is feared. A small state’s deference is expected and proves nothing. A great state’s deference is the only signal the small one can trust.","Mencius has the same pair of relations, the great serving the small and the small serving the great. He assigns the first to the benevolent and the second to the wise. This chapter needs neither virtue. It rests the arrangement on interest.","Silk copy A writes 邦 throughout and copy B 國, the taboo again. On the globe 國 comes nine times in the chapter, and 下 nine times too.","The globe breaks it once, before 故大國, between the image and the application."],"di":["The humility recommended here is an instrument. “Lowers itself to win” is not modesty. The chapter says so, and the honesty is its merit. Readers who want the low place to be a moral position will find it used as a position of advantage.","The weak point is the small state’s line. It “only wants to join and serve”. That is the great state’s account of what small states want. The book knows another account. Chapter 80 describes a small country with few people that serves no one, joins nothing, and can hear its neighbour’s roosters without ever going there.","The two chapters do not cancel. Chapter 61 is addressed to a world where great states exist and will absorb. Given that, it asks them to do it by attraction. Chapter 80 imagines a world without them.","下流 has gone the other way since. In modern Chinese it means low in the bad sense: vulgar, indecent. The chapter’s highest political term has become an insult, which is roughly what the chapter would predict of a low place seen from above."]},"62":{"lead":"Chapter 62 presents the Way as a refuge that is open to the bad as well as the good, and as the best gift that can be offered to a king.","ex":["道者萬物之奧. 奧 is the south-west corner of a house, the innermost and most honoured place, where offerings were made. The globe: “The Way is the hidden heart of the ten thousand things”.","善人之寶，不善人之所保. 寶, treasure, and 保, to protect, sound alike. “the good person’s treasure, the not-good person’s shelter.”","美言可以市，尊行可以加人. Wang Bi punctuates it this way, and his commentary explains: speak the Way beautifully and it outbids every good in the market; practise it with honour and people answer from a thousand miles off. 市 is the market, and as a verb, to trade. “Fine words have their market. Honoured deeds can lift a person up.”","人之不善，何棄之有. “And the people who aren’t good: why throw them away?”","故立天子，置三公. When the Son of Heaven is enthroned and the three highest ministers installed, tribute is presented: 拱璧, a jade disc so large it is carried in both arms, going ahead of 駟馬, a team of four horses. 不如坐進此道: better to kneel and present this Way. The globe: “to sit still and offer this Way.”","古之所以貴此道者何. Why did the ancients prize it? 不曰以求得，有罪以免: “by it the seeker finds, and the wrongdoer is forgiven”. 故為天下貴: “So it is the treasure of the world.”"],"an":["The first passage sets two relations to the Way side by side. The good person holds it as a treasure. The not-good person is held by it. It is where such a person is kept safe. The pun carries the thought.","The lines on fine words are easier in the globe’s text than in the witnesses. The Daozang text and the silk have no second 美, and can be cut differently: fine words can be sold; honoured conduct can raise a person. The globe follows a quotation in the Huainanzi. Either way the tone is dry. Words have a market price. Chapter 81 will say that beautiful words are not true.","The second passage describes the book’s own occasion. A text of advice offered to a ruler in place of jade and horses is what the Daodejing is. The chapter says which gift is worth more.","The third passage is the strangest. 有罪以免, one with guilt is thereby let off, has no parallel in the book, and little in early Chinese thought, where wrongdoing is answered by punishment or by reform. Later it had consequences. The first organised Daoist church, in the second century, made confession of faults a central rite, and it read this book as scripture.","The silk has 注 for 奧, usually read as 主, master. It lacks 道 in 進此 and in 貴此: present this, prized this. The same closing line ended chapter 56, said there of a person. The globe breaks the chapter twice."],"di":["“The wrongdoer is forgiven” invites the charge of cheap pardon. If the Way shelters the not-good, what happens to those they wronged? The chapter does not ask. It is written from the side of the one who needs the shelter.","The best that can be said for it is practical. The alternative to shelter is casting out, and those cast out do not disappear. Chapter 27 called the not-good the material that the good work with. A teaching that throws no one away has to offer the worst person a way back in, and this is where the book does it.","Some translations make this line echo another scripture: seek and you shall find. The resemblance is partly made by translation. 以求得 says that by this, what is sought is obtained. It does not say who answers.","The second passage contains the book’s estimate of itself. Jade and horses are what one gives a king who has everything. Advice is what he lacks, and what he least wants at his enthronement."]},"63":{"lead":"Chapter 63 turns not-doing into a rule of timing: act on things while they are small, and treat nothing as easy.","ex":["為無為，事無事，味無味. Three commands of one shape: a verb, 無, and the verb again. “Do not-doing. Work at not-working. Taste what has no taste.” The third looks back to chapter 35, where the Way, spoken, has no flavour.","大小多少. Four bare adjectives: great, small, many, few. The globe supplies verbs: “Make the small great and the few many.” That is, treat the small as great.","報怨以德. “Answer resentment with virtue.”","圖難於其易，為大於其細. 圖 is to plan. “Plan the hard while it is easy. Do the great while it is small.” The next two lines state the fact behind the advice: hard things 作於易, start out easy, and great things start out fine as a hair.","是以聖人終不為大，故能成其大. “So the sage never does anything great, and so achieves greatness.” Chapter 34 ended on nearly the same words, said of the Way.","夫輕諾必寡信，多易必多難. “Easy promises earn little trust. Take everything as easy, and everything gets hard.” 是以聖人猶難之: “the sage treats things as hard”. 故終無難矣: “and in the end nothing is hard.”"],"an":["The chapter answers a question the book has been raising since chapter 2. If the sage does not act, how does anything get done? Here the answer is timing. There is a moment when the difficult thing is easy and the great thing is small. Action at that moment is nearly nothing. 無為 is doing it then.","The middle passage and the last are one argument seen from two ends. Start early, because size and difficulty grow. Never assume ease, because the assumption is what lets them grow. The sage’s greatness is what small acts at the right time add up to.","The Guodian slips have a much shorter chapter. They run from the three opening commands to 大小之 and then straight to 多易必多難 and the end. Everything between is missing, including 報怨以德. A copyist’s eye may have jumped from one 多 to the next. Or the middle was added later.","If it was added, the most famous line here is the newcomer. The saying itself is old, since Confucius was asked about it. Its place in this chapter may not be.","Where it stands, the line takes its sense from its neighbours. In a chapter about acting while things are small, answering resentment with virtue is advice about a grudge at its beginning. Meet it early, with a kindness, before it is large. The title, 恩始, kindness at the start, reads the chapter this way.","The globe breaks it twice, before 圖難 and before 夫輕諾. 難 comes five times and 易 three."],"di":["The advice assumes one can tell which small things will grow. Mostly one cannot. Of a hundred small troubles a few become great, and they look like the rest. The chapter’s reply is its last passage: treat them all as hard. That is a tax on attention, and the text admits it.","It also sits oddly beside chapter 48’s daily losing. A mind that treats everything as hard is not an emptied mind. The two fit if 難 names a manner and not a mood: handle the thing fully now, so that it need not be handled again.","輕諾寡信 has survived as an idiom, and it is the chapter’s most checkable claim. People who promise quickly are trusted less. Anyone can run that test on their own acquaintance.","On the globe the first line performs its grammar. 無 is sung as a gap, so each command is a word, a rest and the same word again. Doing, with a rest in the middle of it, is close to what the chapter means."]},"64":{"lead":"Chapter 64 joins two sayings that point in opposite directions: deal with things before they exist, and whoever works on things ruins them. What holds them together is the size of the thing acted on.","ex":["Four lines open on one pattern, built on 易, “easy”. What is at rest is easy to hold. What has shown no sign, 未兆, is easy to plan for. What is brittle is easy to break up, and what is tiny is easy to scatter. Then the rule: 為之於未有，治之於未亂. “Deal with it before it exists. Put it in order before it gets confused.”","Three images of growth follow. 合抱之木，生於毫末: “A tree you can’t get your arms around grows from a tiny shoot.” 毫末 is the tip of a hair. 九層之臺，起於累土: “A tower nine storeys high starts from a heap of earth.” 千里之行，始於足下: “A journey of a thousand miles begins beneath your feet.” The usual English has “a single step”. The globe keeps the Chinese: the ground under the foot.","為者敗之，執者失之. “Work on it and you ruin it. Hold it and you lose it.” The lines are chapter 29’s, in the same English.","民之從事，常於幾成而敗之. “People often ruin things just when they’re almost done.” 慎終如始: “Be as careful at the end as at the beginning”.","The last passage gives the sage four habits. 欲不欲: he “wants not to want”. 學不學: he “learns to unlearn”. 復眾人之所過: he goes back to what the crowd has passed. And 以輔萬物之自然，而不敢為: he “helps the ten thousand things be what they are by themselves, not daring to act.”"],"an":["The first half says act, and the second says acting ruins. The Guodian slips explain the seam. They have the two halves in different places, and the second half twice, in two versions. These were separate sayings before they were one chapter. The globe’s four passages mark that join and two more.","Read together they still make sense, by chapter 63’s rule. The thing to act on is the thing that barely exists. 為 in the first half has an object that is not there yet. 為 in the second half has an object that is fully formed, and that is the 為 that ruins.","The first passage is about stopping and the second about growing. One fact serves both. Whatever is large was small, so trouble can be scattered while tiny and a tower can be begun with a heap. Posters keep the second lesson. The chapter gives the first more room.","The best-known line may be a late variant. The silk copies do not have the journey. They read 百仞之高，始於足下: a height of a hundred fathoms begins under the foot. All three images then rise: tree, tower, climb.","輔 is the chapter’s one positive verb for what the sage does, and it is a modest one. He assists. The silk adds 能: he is able to assist and does not dare act. This is the last 自然 in the book."],"di":["“Deal with it before it exists” seems to need the foresight that chapter 38 called the flower of the Way and the beginning of foolishness. The two are different things. 前識 guesses what cannot be seen. 未兆 is what is present and has not yet shown, and seeing it is chapter 52’s clarity: seeing the small.","The third passage is the most practical in the chapter and the least quoted. Things fail near the end. Attention goes when the finish is in sight, and 慎終如始 names the remedy without pretending it is easy.","不敢, not daring, begins a run here. It returns in chapters 67, 69 and 73. The sage’s last word in this chapter is that he does not dare. A modern reader hears timidity. The chapter means respect for a thing’s own course, held by someone who could interfere.","The chapter has 易 four times and 敗 four times. Easy, and ruin: the two words are its argument in small."]},"65":{"lead":"Chapter 65 says that the old masters did not enlighten the people and kept them unclever. It is the hardest chapter for the book’s modern friends.","ex":["古之善為道者. “The ancient masters of the Way”. Chapter 15 opens on the same six characters and the same English.","非以明民，將以愚之. 明 is to make bright, to enlighten. 愚 is to make simple or stupid. The globe softens the second half: they “didn’t try to make people clever. They kept them simple.”","民之難治，以其智多. 智 is cleverness, knowing how to work things. “People are hard to govern when they’re too clever.”","故以智治國，國之賊；不以智治國，國之福. 賊 is a robber and 福 a blessing. “So ruling a country with cleverness robs the country. Ruling a country without cleverness blesses the country.”","知此兩者亦稽式. 稽式 is a standard to measure by, the globe’s “pattern”. 常知稽式，是謂玄德: “Always knowing the pattern is called dark virtue.”","玄德深矣遠矣；與物反矣. Dark virtue is deep and far and 反, turned back, against the grain of things: it “runs the opposite way to things”. 然後乃至大順: “and then arrives at the great accord.”"],"an":["The chapter has two targets, and the second is easy to miss. The people are not to be made clever. The ruler is not to rule by cleverness either. 以智治國 is called robbery. What is rejected is a clever government managing a population, as much as a clever population.","That fits the word. 智 on the globe is always “clever” or “cleverness”, and never wisdom. Chapter 18 says that when cleverness appears there is great pretending. Chapter 19 says to throw it away. And 愚 is what the speaker of chapter 20 calls himself: his is “the heart of a fool”. To make the people 愚 is to make them what the sage already is.","The silk gives the fourth line a better ending. Both copies read 國之德, the state’s virtue, where the globe has 福. Not ruling by cleverness is the country’s virtue, and the chapter then goes straight to 玄德. The received text has broken that link.","This is the third and last 玄德. In chapters 10 and 51 it was giving life without owning. Here it is holding a standard steadily. What joins them is restraint by the one who could do more.","與物反 can mean opposed to things, or returning with them. The globe takes the first. Either way the phrase admits that the policy looks wrong, and claims that it comes out right.","The globe breaks the chapter once, before 知此兩者, between the history and the principle."],"di":["The defence above is true and not sufficient. The sentence is written from the governor’s chair. “People are hard to govern when they’re too clever” names governability as the good. The Legalists said the same without the poetry: ignorant people are easy to rule. Modern Chinese has a word for the policy, 愚民, and it is this line’s.","The chapter can be read as a critique of sophistication and as a charter for keeping people in the dark. The text does not choose, and its history includes both. These essays have said before that the strand should be read as it stands.","What the charitable reading has in its favour is the symmetry. A ruler who keeps the people simple while being clever himself has done what the chapter calls robbery. The teaching binds the top first. Whether rulers who cite the first sentence observe the second is a matter of record.","There is also a question the chapter cannot have foreseen. It assumes cleverness is something a ruler can withhold. Where people can find things out for themselves, the first sentence is not a policy any longer. The second still is."]},"66":{"lead":"Chapter 66 explains leadership by water: the sea rules the streams because it lies below them.","ex":["江海所以能為百谷王者，以其善下之. 百谷 are the hundred valleys, that is, the streams that run down them. 王 is king. “Rivers and seas are kings of a hundred valleys because they are good at staying low.”","是以聖人欲上民，必以言下之. To be above the people he must put himself below them in speech. The globe: “the sage speaks as one below them.” 欲先民，必以身後之: to go before them he must put his person behind. “the sage walks behind them.”","是以聖人處上而民不重；處前而民不害. 重 is heavy. “So the sage is above, and the people don’t feel the weight; in front, and the people aren’t harmed.”","是以天下樂推而不厭. 推 is to push forward, and 厭 is to have had enough. “The whole world gladly pushes the sage forward and never tires of it.”","以其不爭，故天下莫能與之爭. “Because the sage doesn’t compete, no one in the world can compete with the sage.”"],"an":["The argument has three steps. An image: water gathers at the lowest point, and the lowest point therefore commands the most water. An application: speak from below, walk behind. A test: the people feel no weight and suffer no harm.","The test is the important part. The first two steps could describe a manner. The third is measured on the governed. A ruler is above without being heavy only if those under him do not feel pressed, and they are the judges of that.","The last sentence also stands in chapter 22, where Wang Bi has it too. 不爭 is the book’s shortest summary of how the sage wins, and chapter 8 has its root: “Because it doesn’t compete, nothing can fault it.”","The Guodian slips have the chapter with one word missing, and the word matters. Their sage does not 欲, want, to be above. They say only that his being in front is by putting himself behind, and his being above is by speaking from below. The received text has added the wanting, twice.","The Daozang text has 人 for 民 throughout. That is another taboo: 民 was part of the personal name of the Tang emperor Taizong. The silk has 民.","The globe breaks the chapter once, before 是以聖人欲上, between the water and the sage."],"di":["With 欲 in it, the chapter reads as method. Whoever wants to be on top should talk humbly. That is advice a demagogue can use, and “speaks as one below them” describes a good many of them.","Two things in the text resist that use. The first is the test. Flattery from above is followed by weight, and the people feel weight. The second is 不厭. Acclaim that is worked up wears out. The chapter claims the world does not tire of this ruler, which is a claim about years.","Chapter 7 made the same move about the self: the sage “stays back and ends up in front”. It asked there, with a question mark, whether having no private end is how a private end is fulfilled. The book knows the paradox it is using and does not pretend the gain is unwanted.","The Guodian wording is the cleaner teaching. Being below is how one is above. No wanting is needed, and the sea does not want to be king."]},"67":{"lead":"Chapter 67 answers the complaint that the Way resembles nothing by naming three plain things the speaker keeps: compassion, frugality, and not daring to go first.","ex":["天下皆謂我道大，似不肖. 肖 is to resemble. 不肖 is unlike, and it is also the ordinary word for a good-for-nothing, a son who does not take after his father. The world says: your Way is big, and it seems to be like nothing. The globe keeps the first sense: “seems like nothing else.”","夫唯大，故似不肖. Because it is great, it seems so. 若肖，久矣其細也夫: “If it were like anything, it would have become small long ago.”","我有三寶，持而保之. “I have three treasures. I hold them and keep them.” 慈 is the tenderness of a parent, “compassion”. 儉 is “frugality”. The third takes six characters, 不敢為天下先: “not daring to be first in the world.”","Each has a fruit. 慈故能勇: “With compassion, you can be brave.” 儉故能廣: with frugality one can be broad, the globe’s “generous”. Not daring to be first, one can become 器長, chief among the vessels: “you can lead.”","今舍慈且勇: now people drop the compassion and keep the courage, drop the frugality and keep the largesse, drop the rear and take the front. 死矣: “that is death!”","夫慈，以戰則勝，以守則固. “Compassion wins when it fights and holds firm when it defends.” 天將救之，以慈衛之: “When heaven wants to save someone, it protects them with compassion.”"],"an":["The first passage is a reply to critics, and the pun is the reply. They say the Way is 不肖, good for nothing. He agrees it is 不肖, like nothing. Anything it resembled would be a thing among things, and small.","Then the tone drops on purpose. After a Way that is like nothing come three treasures that are like what any household knows. The chapter gives the grand claim and the kitchen virtues in one breath, and does not explain how the second follows from the first.","Each treasure is defined by what it makes possible, and each fruit looks like the treasure’s opposite. Tenderness yields courage, thrift yields largesse, and hanging back yields command. The third passage states the same law in the negative. Take the fruit without the root and it kills.","慈 has changed sides. In chapters 18 and 19 it was half of 孝慈, a virtue that appears when the family has already failed. Here it is the first treasure, and the globe’s notes mark it and 儉 as such.","The silk differs at both ends. It has 我大, I am great, with no 道, and its logic runs the other way: because unlike, therefore able to be great. Its last line has 建 and 垣 for 救 and 衛. When heaven means to set someone up, it walls him round with compassion.","The globe breaks the chapter three times. The fourth passage, on compassion in war, leads into the two chapters that follow."],"di":["不敢為天下先 has been turned inside out. Modern Chinese praises the pioneer with 敢為天下先, daring to be first in the world, the same phrase with the 不 removed. The slogan is the third passage’s 舍後且先, almost word for word, and the chapter’s comment on it is two characters long.","That is unfair to pioneers, and the unfairness is the objection. Someone has to go first. The chapter’s reply is 器長. The one who does not push forward is the one others put in front, and chapter 66 has just described how.","Compassion as a military asset is the claim most open to test. People fight hardest for what they love, and defend best. The last passage says only that. It does not say that the tender-hearted are spared.","三寶 had a second life. When Buddhism came to China, its translators needed a term for the Buddha, the teaching and the community, and took this one."]},"68":{"lead":"Chapter 68 lists four kinds of excellence in conflict and finds that each consists in not doing the obvious thing.","ex":["Four lines share a frame: 善…者不…. 善為士者不武: 士 here is the fighting man, and 武 is martial display. “A good soldier isn’t warlike.” 善戰者不怒: “A good fighter isn’t angry.” 善勝敵者不與: 與 is to engage, to close with. “A good winner doesn’t engage.” 善用人者為之下: “A good leader of people goes below them.”","Three names follow, each introduced by 是謂. 不爭之德: “the virtue of not competing.” 用人之力: “using the strength of others.” 配天古之極: 配 is to pair with or be a match for. The English: “being equal to heaven, the highest thing of old.”"],"an":["The four lines climb from the person to the command. The soldier governs his bearing, the fighter his temper, the victor his engagement, and the commander his position relative to his men. The fourth line is chapter 66 again, applied to an army.","Each excellence removes something that looks essential to the activity. A soldier without ferocity, a fight without anger, a victory without contact. What is left is the result alone. The book’s word for acting with nothing added is 無為, and this is its military form.","The strategists agree in their own terms. Sunzi ranks subduing an enemy without battle above a hundred victories. He warns that a ruler must not raise an army out of anger, since anger passes and the dead do not return.","The three names are less tidy than modern editions make them. Wang Bi’s text, the Daozang plain text and the silk read 配天古之極: matching heaven, the ultimate of antiquity. The silk also has 是謂用人, with no 之力. Editors removed 古 to make three matching phrases of four characters. The globe keeps Wang Bi’s 古, and the silk suggests the symmetry was never there.","配天 is a ritual term. A founding ancestor was said to match heaven when he received sacrifice beside it. The chapter gives that rank to a commander who stays below his men.","The globe gives it as one passage. 善 comes four times, 不 four times and 是謂 three."],"di":["The second line is the one a reader can use tomorrow. Anger makes a person legible and hasty, and both are costs in any contest, including an argument. The line does not say anger is wrong. It says the good fighter does not fight from it.","That leaves a real objection. Some things ought to make a person angry, and a teaching that prizes calm can shade into not caring. The chapter is about skill in conflict. It should not be stretched into a rule for the feelings, and chapter 31 shows the book is capable of grief.","“Using the strength of others” is candid. 用人 is to employ people, and the line treats their strength as the leader’s instrument. The fourth line says how he earns the use of it: by going below. Whether that is service or technique depends, as in chapter 66, on what those below feel."]},"69":{"lead":"Chapter 69 quotes a soldier’s maxim about yielding ground and turns it into four paradoxes. It ends with a prediction: when armies meet, the one that grieves wins.","ex":["用兵有言 introduces a quotation, as chapters 41 and 42 did. The globe marks it with quotation marks in the Chinese and the English alike. 吾不敢為主而為客: 主 is the host, the side that makes the first move, and 客 is the guest, who answers. “I don’t dare to be the host; I’d rather be the guest.” 不敢進寸而退尺: “I don’t dare advance an inch; I’d rather retreat a foot.”","Four phrases follow, each a verb, 無, and a noun. 行無行: to march with no ranks. The globe has “marching without marching”. 攘無臂: baring an arm that is not there. 執無兵: gripping a weapon that is not there. 扔無敵: closing with an enemy who is not there, the globe’s “charging where there is no enemy.”","攘 and 扔 are the two verbs of chapter 38, where etiquette rolled up its sleeves and dragged people into line. Here both are emptied.","禍莫大於輕敵. “No disaster is greater than underestimating the enemy.” The frame is chapter 46’s. 輕敵幾喪吾寶: 寶 are the three treasures of chapter 67. “Underestimate the enemy, and you nearly lose my treasures.”","故抗兵相加，哀者勝矣. 哀 is grief. “So when armies meet, the side that grieves will win.”"],"an":["The maxim is defensive and the paradoxes take it further. To be the guest is to let the other side commit first. The four phrases then describe a force that offers nothing to strike: no formation to break, no arm to seize, no weapon to parry, no opponent to find. It is chapter 50’s person with no place of death, in the field.","The second passage guards against the obvious misreading. Yielding is not contempt for the enemy. The worst mistake is to think him slight, and the reason given is the treasures. Compassion is the first of them. A commander who holds the enemy cheap has stopped seeing him as someone who can die and kill.","The silk has a different and stranger word. Both copies read 禍莫大於無敵: no disaster is greater than having no enemy. It can mean thinking oneself unmatched, which is the received sense. Read plainly, it says that a state with no rival is in the greatest danger. Mencius says so outright: a state with no hostile neighbours and no outside threat will perish.","The silk also has 乃 for 扔: gripping no weapon, then there is no enemy. And the Daozang text puts the last two paradoxes in the other order.","哀 ties the chapter to 31, where victory is to be treated as a funeral. The grieving side is the one that did not want the war and knows what it costs.","The globe breaks the chapter before 禍莫大, between the maxim and the warning."],"di":["The last line became a proverb, 哀兵必勝, and proverbs lose their conditions. Grieving armies lose all the time. The chapter’s claim is comparative and has a clause: when the forces are matched. The silk makes that explicit with 相若, of equal strength. Between equals, the side with more to mourn fights harder.","The maxim has a cost that the chapter does not count. Whoever always lets the other move first cedes the choice of time and place. A foot of retreat is cheap once. As a habit it is how territory is lost. The chapter trusts that the aggressor overreaches. Chapter 36 gave the theory. It is true often enough to be a strategy and not often enough to be a law.","On the globe the four paradoxes have a sound. 無 is sung as a rest, so each is a note, a rest and a note. The march, the arm, the weapon and the enemy each have a hole in the middle."]},"70":{"lead":"Chapter 70 is the speaker’s complaint: the teaching is easy, and no one takes it up.","ex":["吾言甚易知，甚易行. “My words are very easy to understand and very easy to practise.” 天下莫能知，莫能行: “No one in the world can understand them, no one can practise them.”","言有宗，事有君. 宗 is an ancestor, the head of a line. 君 is a lord. “Words have an ancestor. Deeds have a master.” What is said and done here comes from one source and is not a heap of sayings.","夫唯無知，是以不我知. Because of 無知, not knowing, they do not know me. The globe supplies an object: “It’s because people don’t understand this that they don’t understand me.”","知我者希，則我者貴. 希 is few. 則 is to take as a rule, and 貴 is precious, so rare. “Those who understand me are few, and those who follow me are rare.”","是以聖人被褐懷玉. 褐 is coarse cloth, the dress of the poor. “So the sage wears rough cloth and carries jade inside.”"],"an":["The first two lines are not a contradiction if 易 and 能 are heard exactly. The words are easy: there is nothing technical in them. No one is able: the obstacle is somewhere other than difficulty. Chapter 78 names it by changing one word. There everyone knows that the weak overcome the strong, and no one can live it.","The second passage says what people miss. They take the sayings one at a time, as maxims, and maxims are easy to nod at. They do not see the ancestor. A reader who has the source can practise the sayings. A reader who has only the sayings has a collection.","知 comes five times and 我 three, and the chapter also opens on 吾. Only chapter 20 is more personal. Both show a speaker who is alone and says so.","The silk gives the fifth line another sense. It reads 知者希，則我貴矣: those who know are few, and so I am precious. 則 is “then”, and the value belongs to the speaker. It is drier than the received line, and nearer to a joke.","Chapter 39 said not to glitter like jade. This chapter puts the jade inside the coat. The two agree. The objection was to the glitter.","The globe breaks the chapter once, before 言有宗."],"di":["Confucius made the same complaint in nearly the same words: no one knows me. His consolation was that heaven did. The speaker here names no one who knows him. He has the jade.","A plain objection: if no one in the world can understand the words, perhaps the words are not easy. The claim of ease can be a teacher’s vanity. The test is whether the trouble is in grasping or in wanting. “Know when to stop” is grasped by a child. The chapter is right that it is easy to understand, and right that it is rarely done.","被褐懷玉 is now an idiom for unrecognised worth, and it flatters whoever uses it of himself. In the chapter the rough cloth is not a disguise that the sage hopes will be seen through. Chapter 56 described a person no one can raise up or bring down. This is his coat.","The silk’s reading has the more honest psychology. To be understood by few and prized on that account is a real comfort, and a teacher is wise to notice that he takes it."]},"71":{"lead":"Chapter 71 ranks two conditions: knowing that one does not know, and not knowing while thinking one knows. It says the cure for the second is to feel it as an illness.","ex":["The globe punctuates the first eight characters heavily: 知，不知，上；不知，知，病. To know, and not know: best. Not to know, and know: sickness. The grammar needs filling in, and the globe’s English does it: “To know that you don’t know is best. Not to know, and think you know, is a sickness.”","Another filling is as old: to know and yet hold oneself not to know. The first reading is about the limits of knowledge. The second is about the bearing of someone who has it.","聖人不病，以其病病. 病病 is the noun used as verb and object: to treat the sickness as a sickness, to be pained by it. The globe finds an English idiom that does the same work: “The sage isn’t sick, because the sage is sick of the sickness.”","夫唯病病，是以不病. “Only by being sick of the sickness are you not sick.” In Wang Bi’s order the general rule comes first, and the sage after it."],"an":["At twenty-four characters this is the second-shortest chapter on the globe, and seven of the twenty-four are 病. It is built almost entirely from three words: 知, 不 and 病.","The first sentence sets a rank and not a definition. It does not say what knowledge is. It says which of two states is higher, and the higher one includes an admission. Confucius gives the neighbouring rule: to hold what you know as known and what you do not know as not known is knowing.","The second sentence is the unusual one. The remedy for false knowledge is not more knowledge. It is a reaction: finding the condition unbearable. A person who is merely aware of overconfidence stays overconfident. The chapter asks for 病, the discomfort that makes one do something.","The logic then closes on itself. Whoever is sick of the sickness is not sick. The cure is the symptom taken seriously.","The first silk copy reads the second clause as 不知不知: not to know that one does not know. That makes the two clauses exact opposites. Wang Bi’s text, which the globe follows, puts the general rule before the sage and repeats 是以不病; other editions put the sage first.","There is no 道 and no 無. The globe gives it as one passage."],"di":["The first line has a famous Greek twin. Socrates at his trial says he is wiser than another man by this much, that he does not think he knows what he does not know. The two texts are independent and agree on the ranking.","Psychologists have since measured the second line. People who perform worst at a task tend to rate themselves well above their results, and the least able are the most mistaken about it. The chapter adds what the studies do not: a recommendation about how it should feel.","The objection is to the cure. Feeling bad about one’s ignorance is not the same as mending it, and can replace mending it. There are people who are permanently sick of their sickness and no better. The chapter’s 病病 has to be read as the kind of distress that ends in a change. The text does not say how to tell the two apart.","Still, the alternative is worse. The person in the second clause feels fine. That is the whole of the sickness, and it is why the chapter can define health as the loss of that comfort."]},"72":{"lead":"Chapter 72 warns rulers that fear has a limit and tells them to leave people room. Then it turns to what a ruler should do with his regard for himself.","ex":["民不畏威，則大威至. 威 is awe, the power to frighten. “When people no longer fear power, a greater power arrives.” The line does not say whose.","無狎其所居，無厭其所生. 狎 is read as 狹, to make narrow, and 厭 as 壓, to press down. “Don’t crowd them where they live. Don’t weigh down their lives.”","夫唯不厭，是以不厭. The same character twice, in two senses: to press, and to be sick of. Only because you do not press on them, they are not sick of you. The globe keeps both: “Because you don’t weigh on them, they won’t grow weary of you.”","是以聖人自知不自見，自愛不自貴. 自見 is to show oneself and 自貴 is to set a high price on oneself. The sage has “self-knowledge without self-display, self-love without self-importance”.","故去彼取此. “lets go of that, and takes this.” It is the third and last time the book closes a chapter on these four characters."],"an":["The first line states a threshold. Fear governs up to a point. Past that point the governed stop fearing, and what comes next is not obedience. 大威 is left unnamed. It can be heaven’s, or the people’s, or simply what happens. Chapter 74 will put the same thought as a question: if people do not fear death, why threaten them with death?","The second and third lines say how to stay below the threshold. They name two things a ruler must not take: room and livelihood. The pun on 厭 states the exchange. Weight from above becomes weariness from below.","The second passage seems to change the subject, from the people to the sage’s self. The link is that self-display and self-importance take up room. A ruler who must be seen and must be prized is the one who crowds dwellings and presses on lives. The chapter treats oppression as an overflow of the ruler’s self-regard.","It does not ask him to think little of himself. 自知 and 自愛 are affirmed, and this is the only place the book says 自愛. Chapter 33 called self-knowledge clarity. What is cut away is the outward half of each: showing, and pricing. The title, 愛已, is the Daozang’s spelling of 愛己, loving oneself.","The silk agrees with the globe almost throughout. It writes 畏 for both 威, so that the line is about fear from start to finish: when the people do not fear what is fearful, the great fear arrives.","The globe breaks the chapter before 是以聖人, between the warning and the sage. 自 comes four times."],"di":["The first line is among the most accurate political sentences in the book. Governments that rule by fear are stable until an hour when they are not, and the hour is not announced. The chapter does not say fear is wrong. It says it runs out.","The remedy is modest to the point of being a minimum. Do not narrow where they live. Do not press on what they live by. It is a list of two things not to do, and regimes have fallen for failing at either.","自愛不自貴 is a distinction a modern reader can use at once. Looking after oneself is one thing. Setting a price on oneself and requiring others to pay it is another. Chapter 44 said the body is nearer than the name. This chapter says that one may love what is near without advertising it.","That and this, 彼 and 此, are left unglossed as before. Here the chapter makes them plain enough. That is being seen and being prized. This is knowing oneself and caring for oneself, with no audience."]},"73":{"lead":"Chapter 73 says that courage kills or saves depending on what it dares. It admits that heaven’s reasons are not known, and trusts heaven’s wide net anyway.","ex":["勇於敢則殺，勇於不敢則活. 勇 is courage and 敢 is daring. “Brave in daring, you get killed. Brave in not daring, you stay alive.” Both people are brave. They differ in what the courage is for.","此兩者，或利或害. “Of these two, one helps and one harms.” 天之所惡，孰知其故: “What heaven hates, who knows why?” 是以聖人猶難之: “Even the sage finds it hard.”","Then four lines on 天之道, each a 不 and a 善. It “doesn’t compete and is good at winning”. It does not speak and answers well. It “isn’t called and comes by itself”. 繟然 is slack, at ease: it “is unhurried and good at planning.”","天網恢恢，疏而不失. 恢恢 is vast and 疏 is wide-meshed. “Heaven’s net is vast, so vast. Its mesh is wide, and nothing slips through.”"],"an":["The run of 不敢 that began in chapter 64 ends here, and here it gets a virtue of its own. 勇於不敢 is courage in not daring. Holding back is treated as something that takes nerve.","The middle of the first passage is an admission that wisdom books rarely make. One course helps and one harms. Which one heaven hates, and why, no one knows. The chapter does not claim that the cautious are always spared.","The line about the sage is not in the silk. It is chapter 63’s sentence, copied in. Without it the chapter goes straight from the question to the Way of heaven, and the question is answered with a description and no reason.","The silk also reads 不戰而善勝: it does not fight and is good at winning. The globe’s 不爭 is the book’s commoner phrase.","Heaven’s four traits are the ones the book has asked of people: chapter 68’s soldier, chapter 66’s ruler, chapter 43’s wordless teacher. Heaven is the model they were copied from.","The globe breaks the chapter before 天之道."],"di":["天網恢恢 has become a proverb about crime: no one gets away in the end. That is a promise of justice, and the chapter does not quite make it. It has just said that no one knows what heaven hates. The net misses nothing. It is not said to sort by desert.","The obvious objection is that a great deal slips through, in plain sight. The chapter’s time scale is heaven’s, which no one can check. The last line is trust, and it is placed after an admission of ignorance. The order is the honest part.","“Brave in not daring” is the usable line. Refusing takes more courage than charging when everyone else is charging."]},"74":{"lead":"Chapter 74 argues against rule by execution. The threat fails on people who do not fear death, and the killing is someone else’s office.","ex":["民不畏死，奈何以死懼之. “If people don’t fear death, why threaten them with death?”","若使民常畏死，而為奇者，吾得執而殺之，孰敢. 奇 is the irregular, chapter 57’s word. “If people always feared death, and I could seize and kill those who do wrong, who would dare?”","常有司殺者殺. 司 is to have charge of. “There is always an executioner who kills.”","夫代司殺者殺，是謂代大匠斲. 大匠 is the master carpenter and 斲 is to hew. “To kill in the executioner’s place is like cutting wood in the master carpenter’s place.” Whoever does it “rarely escapes without hurting their hands.”"],"an":["There are two arguments. The first is a dilemma. If the people do not fear death, the threat is empty. If they did fear it, a few executions would end all wrongdoing, and no one would dare. People do dare. So they are in the first condition, and chapters 72 and 75 say how they got there.","The second argument is about office. The one in charge of killing is not named. The commentators say heaven: death comes to everyone in its time, and a ruler who kills has taken over a post that is filled. The carpenter makes the point concrete. An amateur with the master’s axe cuts himself.","The silk is fuller and clearer. It has three conditions: if the people never fear death, if they always fear it, and if they must always fear it. In the third, it says, there is always the one in charge of killing.","Chapter 73 said that heaven kills the daring. This chapter draws the conclusion for rulers: leave it there.","The globe breaks the chapter before 常有司殺, between the two arguments."],"di":["This is the oldest argument against the death penalty in Chinese. It does not rest on the value of life. It rests on whether the threat works and on whose job the killing is. Modern debate about deterrence still turns on the first passage: the threat does least to those with least to lose.","The objection is that “leave it to heaven” protects no one. The chapter does not forbid punishment. Its claim is narrower and is in the last line: the one who kills is seldom unhurt. The cost falls on the hand that holds the axe.","History has cases enough. Governments that kill freely end by killing their own officers, and the officers know it."]},"75":{"lead":"Chapter 75 gives three reasons for three kinds of misery, and all three point upward.","ex":["民之饑，以其上食稅之多. 上 is those above, and 食稅 is to eat taxes. “The people are hungry because those above them eat up too much in taxes.”","民之難治，以其上之有為. 有為 is the opposite of 無為. “The people are hard to govern because those above them meddle.”","民之輕死，以其求生之厚. 輕死 is to hold death light. 求生之厚 is seeking life thickly, chapter 50’s phrase. Wang Bi’s text has no 上 here: “The people take death lightly because they grab too hard at life.”","夫唯無以生為者，是賢於貴生. “Those who don’t make a business of living are wiser than those who prize life too much.”"],"an":["Three sentences share one frame, and each states its conclusion twice. The globe keeps the repetition: “That is why they are hungry.” The form is a charge sheet.","Each cause is an excess at the top: of eating, of doing and of living. The third explains the premise of chapter 74. People stop fearing death when their rulers' appetite for life has left theirs not worth holding on to.","The last sentence turns the charge into a principle that reaches past rulers. 以生為 is making life one’s project. The chapter prefers the person who does not, and it uses 貴生, chapter 50’s title, as the name of the mistake.","The third sentence is different in the witnesses. Wang Bi, the Daozang plain text and the silk have no 上 there: the people take death lightly because they themselves seek life too thickly. That is chapter 50’s diagnosis, turned on the people. Heshang Gong and Fu Yi add 上, which keeps all three blames pointing upward.","The silk’s first sentence says the people starve because “they take” too much in grain tax, with no 上 either. Its second has 百姓, the hundred surnames, for 民.","The globe gives the chapter as one passage. 上 comes twice and 民 three times."],"di":["This is the plainest political chapter in the book. Hunger has a cause, and the cause is taxation. There is no metaphysics in it, and a ruler could check it against his own accounts.","The objection is that taxes also build granaries and dikes. The chapter’s verb answers it. The tax here is eaten. The complaint is about consumption at the top, and chapter 53 has already listed what it buys.","The variant matters more than most. With 上, the people’s recklessness is their rulers’ doing. Without it, it is their own. Both readings are old. The globe follows Wang Bi and lets the third blame fall on the people; the comparison square shows the other reading."]},"76":{"lead":"Chapter 76 reads the difference between a living body and a corpse as a law. What is soft is alive, and what has gone hard is on its way out.","ex":["人之生也柔弱，其死也堅強. “People are born soft and weak. They die hard and stiff.” 生 is both being born and being alive. Plants are the same: 柔脆, tender, while they live, and 枯槁, dry and brittle, when dead.","故堅強者死之徒，柔弱者生之徒. 徒 is chapter 50’s word. There the globe had “follow”, and here it has “companions”: “So the hard and stiff are companions of death. The soft and weak are companions of life.”","是以兵強則不勝，木強則共. “So an army that is too strong doesn’t win. A tree that is too hard takes every blow.” Wang Bi glosses 共 as 物所加也: what everything presses down on.","強大處下，柔弱處上. “The hard and great go below. The soft and weak rise above.”"],"an":["The argument is an observation and an extension. The observation is exact. Rigor is the mark of a dead body, and dry wood snaps where green wood bends. The extension is to armies and to rank.","The last line is taken from the tree as well. The trunk is hard and stays below. The twigs are soft and are on top. A reader need not take it on faith.","木強則共 is unsettled in every witness. Wang Bi, Heshang Gong and Fu Yi have 共; the silk copies have 恆 and 兢; other editions have 兵, the axe, or 折, it snaps. The sense is steady: hard wood comes to grief.","The globe breaks the chapter before 是以兵強, between the law and its uses."],"di":["The step from bodies to armies is an analogy and no more. Strong armies often win. The point holds for a narrower thing, which is rigidity. A force that cannot bend has one way to fail and no way to adapt.","The chapter also passes over the life between the soft newborn and the stiff corpse, in which some hardness is useful. Bones are hard. Chapter 55 praised a firm grip in a soft hand. What the book warns against is the hardening of what should move."]},"77":{"lead":"Chapter 77 says that heaven levels and people do the reverse. Heaven takes from too much to give to too little. People take from too little to give to too much.","ex":["天之道，其猶張弓與. “The Way of heaven is like drawing a bow.” When a bow is strung, the top is pulled down and the bottom comes up. 有餘者損之，不足者補之: “Where there’s too much, it takes away. Where there’s not enough, it gives.”","Then the contrast. 人之道，則不然: “The way of people is not like that.” 損不足以奉有餘: “It takes from those who have too little to give to those who have too much.” 奉 is to offer up, the verb for tribute.","孰能有餘以奉天下？唯有道者. “Who can take what they have too much of and give it to the world? Only someone with the Way.”","The sage closes it with three refusals. Two are known from chapters 2 and 51: he “acts without leaning on it, finishes the work and doesn’t stay”. The third is new. 其不欲見賢: “and has no wish to look wise.”"],"an":["This is the book’s clearest statement about distribution. 損 was paired with gain in chapters 42 and 48. Here it gets a direction and a moral: heaven subtracts from surplus.","Nature evens out and society concentrates. Chapter 53 gave the picture and chapter 75 the mechanism. This chapter gives the law.","The question in the third passage is not answered with a policy. It is answered with a person. Whoever has more than enough and gives it up has the Way. The test is as simple as that.","The Daozang text words the question differently: who can use surplus to serve what is lacking in the world? The silk is close to the globe. The globe breaks the chapter twice: bow, contrast, sage."],"di":["Is heaven a leveller? Rivers fill low ground and peaks wear down. But nature also concentrates, and big fish eat small ones. The chapter chooses its examples. The bow is a made thing, and its levelling is done by an archer.","As a statement about people, the second passage needs no defence. Left alone, wealth flows toward wealth. The chapter says so some twenty centuries before anyone measured it.","The third refusal is the price of the remedy. Whoever gives surplus away must not want credit for it, or the gift becomes a purchase of reputation. 不欲見賢 closes that door."]},"78":{"lead":"Chapter 78 names water as the softest and strongest thing, and admits that everyone knows this and no one acts on it. It ends with four characters that describe the whole book.","ex":["天下莫柔弱於水，而攻堅強者莫之能勝. “Nothing in the world is softer or weaker than water, yet nothing is better at wearing down the hard and strong.” 其無以易之: “Nothing can take its place.”","天下莫不知，莫能行. “Everyone in the world knows it, and no one can live it.”","是以聖人云 introduces a quotation, marked as one on the globe. 受國之垢 is to take on the state’s filth. 社稷 are the altars of soil and grain, which stand for the state. “Whoever takes on the country’s dirt is lord of the land. Whoever takes on the country’s misfortune is king of the world.”","正言若反. “True words sound backwards.”"],"an":["Chapter 43 left the softest thing unnamed. This chapter names it, as chapter 8 had praised it. The globe’s note calls water “the book’s favourite image of the Way: soft, low, and unbeatable.”","The second passage differs from chapter 70 by one word. There no one could understand. Here everyone understands and no one can do it. The trouble was never in the understanding.","The third passage applies water to a king. Water goes to the low and dirty places. The ruler who can take the blame and the bad luck on himself is the one fit to rule.","正言若反 is the book’s account of its own method, and 反 is chapter 40’s word. Every 若 line in chapters 41 and 45 is an instance.","The Daozang text orders the middle the other way: the soft overcomes the hard, then the weak the strong. The silk is close to the globe. The globe breaks the chapter twice."],"di":["“No one can live it” should be taken as data. If a truth is known to all and practised by none, knowledge is not what moves people. The book has no theory of why. It reports the gap twice and leaves it open.","The king who takes on the dirt has a long history as an ideal and a short one as a practice. Rulers have more often found others to carry the filth. The saying sets the test the other way round: the dirt is the office.","True words sound backwards, and so do many false ones. Paradox is no mark of truth. The line warns how this book will sound. It does not license whatever sounds strange."]},"79":{"lead":"Chapter 79 is about what is left when a quarrel has been settled. Its answer is a lender who keeps the proof of the debt and never calls it in.","ex":["和大怨，必有餘怨，安可以為善. “When a great grievance is settled, some grievance is always left. How can that be good?”","是以聖人執左契，而不責於人. A 契 was a contract cut in two, with each party keeping a half. The halves were matched when the debt fell due. “So the sage holds the creditor’s half of the tally and makes no demands on anyone.”","有德司契，無德司徹. 司 is to have charge of. 徹 is the hard word: it is the name of a Zhou tithe, and the globe reads it as collection. “Those with virtue keep the tally. Those without virtue collect the debts.”","天道無親，常與善人. “The Way of heaven has no favourites. It is always with the good.”"],"an":["The first passage is a diagnosis. A settlement closes the account and leaves the feeling. Chapter 63 said to answer grievance with virtue, and this chapter shows how.","The sage keeps the claim and does not press it. The tally stays in his hand, so nothing is denied or forgiven for show. What he gives up is the demand, and without a demand no grievance starts.","Which half the lender kept is an old dispute. Some early texts put the claim in the right hand, and then the sage holds the debtor’s half. The globe’s “creditor’s half” is the common reading.","The witnesses differ little. Silk copy B adds one word, 而不以責於人: he does not use the tally to press people. In the silk order this is the last chapter of the De half, since 80 and 81 stand earlier.","The globe breaks the chapter before 是以聖人."],"di":["The oldest objection to the last line is Sima Qian’s. He quotes it in his life of Bo Yi and asks why the good starve while a robber dies old. If 與 means reward, the chapter has no answer.","It has one if the line says less. 無親 rules out reward. What is left is that a person who presses no claims meets less resistance.","The tally is also not torn up. A debt held and never called is a strong position, and the debtor knows it. The sage’s restraint keeps the upper hand, as the sea’s did in chapter 66."]},"80":{"lead":"Chapter 80 is the book’s one picture of a good society. It is small, it owns every tool, and it uses almost none of them.","ex":["小國寡民. “A small country with few people.” 什伯之器 are tools that do the work of many hands, and they sit idle: “Let there be tools that do the work of ten or a hundred, and let them go unused.”","使民重死而不遠徙. 重死 is to give death its weight. “Let people take death seriously and not wander far away.” There are boats, carts, armour and weapons, with “nowhere to ride them” and “no reason to show them”.","使人復結繩而用之. Wang Bi’s text has 人 here, where others have 民; the sense is the same. Knotted cords were the records said to come before writing. “Let people go back to knotting cords to keep count.”","甘其食，美其服，安其居，樂其俗. “Let their food taste sweet, their clothes look fine, their homes feel safe, their customs bring joy.”","鄰國相望，雞犬之聲相聞，民至老死不相往來. “The next country is in sight, its roosters and dogs can be heard, and people grow old and die without ever going there.”"],"an":["The grammar matters. 使 is to let or to make, and it comes three times. This is a programme, and someone is arranging the simplicity.","It is not poverty, and it is not ignorance of technique. The tools exist and are declined. Chapter 57 blamed sharp tools for a darkened state, and here they are kept on the shelf beside the weapons.","Chapter 61 set small states among great ones. This chapter takes one small state alone, with a neighbour near enough to hear.","Silk copy B writes 十百人器, tools of ten and a hundred men, which supports the globe’s reading of 什伯. It also has 使民重死而遠徙, with no 不. There 遠 is a verb: people keep migration at a distance, and the sense is the same.","The globe breaks the chapter before 甘其食, between the refusals and the contentments."],"di":["This is the chapter most often held against the book. The people are to give up writing, travel and curiosity, and it is done by design. Chapter 65 had already said the old masters kept the people simple.","The other reading takes it as a counter-image. It was written while great states were swallowing small ones and every surplus went to war. Against that, a place where weapons have no reason to be shown is the whole point.","Tao Yuanming borrowed the roosters and dogs for his Peach Blossom Spring, some seven centuries later. He made the village impossible to find twice. That may be the fairest comment on it.","The last line is not hostile. The neighbours are heard and left alone, because nothing is wanted from them. Whether a life with no wish to go and see is a full one is the question the chapter leaves open."]},"81":{"lead":"The last chapter distrusts fine words, at the end of a book made of them, and then turns to giving. Its last character is 爭, with 不 in front of it.","ex":["信言不美，美言不信. 信 is said of words that can be relied on. “True words aren’t beautiful. Beautiful words aren’t true.”","Two pairs follow in the same mould. 善者不辯，辯者不善: “The good don’t argue. Those who argue aren’t good.” 知者不博，博者不知: “Those who know aren’t learned. The learned don’t know.”","聖人不積. 積 is to pile up and store. “The sage doesn’t hoard.” 既以為人己愈有，既以與人己愈多: “The more the sage does for others, the more the sage has. The more the sage gives to others, the more the sage gains.”","天之道，利而不害；聖人之道，為而不爭. “The Way of heaven helps and doesn’t harm. The Way of the sage acts and doesn’t compete.”"],"an":["The three pairs are a parting word on the book’s own medium. Chapter 1 began by doubting names. Chapter 81 ends by doubting eloquence, argument and learning.","Each pair has an earlier form. Chapter 62 gave fine words a market value. Chapter 45 said great eloquence seems to stammer, and chapter 56 said those who know do not speak.","The second passage answers chapter 77, which asked who could give surplus to the world. The sage stores nothing and gains by giving. 積 was a good word in chapter 59, but what was piled up there was virtue.","Silk copy B has 善者不多，多者不善 for the globe’s second pair, and puts it third: the good do not have much. There it leads straight into the hoarding line. In its last line the silk has 人之道 where the globe has 聖人之道: the way of people, with no sage.","不爭 has run through chapters 3, 8, 66, 68 and 73, and it closes the book. The silk does not end here. There chapters 80 and 81 stand between 66 and 67, and the book ends with chapter 37.","The globe breaks the chapter before 聖人不積."],"di":["The first line is a well-made sentence saying that well-made sentences are not to be trusted. Chapter 78 has already given the defence: true words sound backwards. 美 here is polish meant to please, and the line is plain.","“The good don’t argue” is harder to accept. The book argues on every page, and a refusal to argue can be a refusal to be checked. The pairs are better read as warnings about motive: arguing to win, and learning to display.","The gift lines are false as accounting. Give away grain and there is less grain. They are true of what grows by being shared, such as trust, skill and goodwill, and 為人, acting for others, is the right verb for those.","The book ends with no summary and no last definition of the Way. Its final words say how to act: do the work and do not fight over it. A book that began by saying the Way cannot be told never said it could not be walked."]}},"edition":{"edits":[{"ch":2,"old":"長短相形","new":"長短相較","kind":"wb","note":"形 is the silk and Guodian reading; Wang Bi has 較."},{"ch":2,"old":"功成而不居","new":"功成而弗居","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":3,"old":"使民心不亂","new":"使心不亂","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 使民心不亂."},{"ch":3,"old":"使夫智者","new":"使夫知者","kind":"dz","note":"知 for 智; Wang Bi glosses 知者謂知為也."},{"ch":8,"old":"政善治","new":"正善治","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":9,"old":"天之道也。","new":"天之道。","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":10,"old":"愛國治民，能無為乎","new":"愛民治國，能無知乎","kind":"slip","note":"愛國治民 was a slip; Wang Bi reads 無知 here (commentary: 治國無以智)."},{"ch":10,"old":"明白四達，能無知乎","new":"明白四達，能無為乎","kind":"wb","note":"The Daozang text repeats 無知, but Wang Bi's own commentary (能無以為乎) shows he read 無為."},{"ch":10,"old":"？（生之畜之，生而不有，為而不恃，長而不宰，是謂玄德）。","new":"？生之畜之，生而不有，為而不恃，長而不宰，是謂玄德。","kind":"ed","note":"Brackets were a modern editor's judgement; Wang Bi has the lines as text."},{"ch":13,"old":"寵為上，辱為下，得之若驚","new":"寵為下，得之若驚","kind":"wb","note":"Also the Guodian and silk reading."},{"ch":15,"old":"古之善為道者","new":"古之善為士者","kind":"wb","note":"善為道 is the silk B reading."},{"ch":15,"old":"儼兮其若客","new":"儼兮其若容","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":15,"old":"渾兮其若濁","new":"混兮其若濁","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":15,"old":"孰能安以動之徐生","new":"孰能安以久動之徐生","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":15,"old":"故能蔽而新成","new":"故能蔽不新成","kind":"wb","note":"蔽而新成 was a modern emendation."},{"ch":16,"old":"公乃全，全乃天","new":"公乃王，王乃天","kind":"wb","note":"全 was a twentieth-century conjecture."},{"ch":17,"old":"太上，不知有之","new":"太上，下知有之","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":20,"old":"我獨昏昏","new":"我獨若昏","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 我獨昏昏."},{"ch":20,"old":"我獨頑且鄙","new":"我獨頑似鄙","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":21,"old":"自今及古","new":"自古及今","kind":"wb","note":"Wang Bi's commentary quotes 自古及今; 自今及古 is the silk reading."},{"ch":22,"old":"不自矜，故長。","new":"不自矜，故長。夫唯不爭，故天下莫能與之爭。","kind":"slip","note":"The sentence was missing in Chinese and English."},{"ch":23,"old":"故從事於道者，同於道","new":"故從事於道者，道者同於道","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":24,"old":"其於道也","new":"其在道也","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":25,"old":"獨立而不改","new":"獨立不改","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 獨立而不改."},{"ch":25,"old":"人亦大","new":"王亦大","kind":"wb","note":"人 is Fu Yi's reading."},{"ch":25,"old":"而人居其一焉","new":"而王居其一焉","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":26,"old":"是以君子終日行","new":"是以聖人終日行","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":26,"old":"輕則失根","new":"輕則失本","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":29,"old":"不可為也，不可執也。","new":"不可為也。","kind":"wb","note":"不可執也 was restored by modern editors."},{"ch":29,"old":"或載或隳","new":"或挫或隳","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":30,"old":"善者果而已","new":"善有果而已","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":31,"old":"則不可得志於天下矣","new":"則不可以得志於天下矣","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":32,"old":"知止可以不殆","new":"知止所以不殆","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":32,"old":"猶川谷之於江海","new":"猶川谷之與江海","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 於."},{"ch":34,"old":"功成而不有","new":"功成不名有","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":36,"old":"必固舉之","new":"必固興之","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":38,"old":"下德無為而有以為","new":"下德為之而有以為","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":38,"old":"道之華，萬愚之始","new":"道之華，而愚之始","kind":"slip","note":""},{"ch":39,"old":"谷一以盈","new":"谷得一以盈","kind":"slip","note":""},{"ch":39,"old":"神為以靈","new":"神無以靈","kind":"slip","note":"Audible: a note was sung where 無 is a rest."},{"ch":39,"old":"非歟？故至譽無譽","new":"非乎？故致數譽無譽","kind":"wb","note":"Zhang transcription: 致數輿無輿."},{"ch":45,"old":"靜勝躁，寒勝熱","new":"躁勝寒，靜勝熱","kind":"wb","note":"The reordering was a twentieth-century emendation."},{"ch":47,"old":"無為而成","new":"不為而成","kind":"wb","note":"Removes a rest no witness has."},{"ch":49,"old":"聖人在天下，歙歙焉；為天下，渾其心","new":"聖人在天下歙歙，為天下渾其心","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":50,"old":"動之死地，亦十有三","new":"動之死地，十有三","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 亦十有三."},{"ch":50,"old":"入軍不被兵甲","new":"入軍不被甲兵","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":51,"old":"夫莫之命而常自然","new":"夫莫之命常自然","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 而常自然."},{"ch":52,"old":"既得其母，以知其子","new":"既知其母，復知其子","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 既得其母，以知其子."},{"ch":52,"old":"是為習常","new":"是謂習常","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 是為."},{"ch":54,"old":"修之於邦其德乃豐","new":"修之於國，其德乃豐","kind":"wb","note":"國 is the Han taboo substitution for 邦; Wang Bi has 國."},{"ch":54,"old":"以邦觀邦","new":"以國觀國","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":55,"old":"毒蟲不螫","new":"蜂蠆虺蛇不螫","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":55,"old":"而脧作","new":"而全作","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":55,"old":"物壯則老，是謂不道","new":"物壯則老，謂之不道","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":56,"old":"解其紛","new":"解其分","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":58,"old":"真而不肆","new":"直而不肆","kind":"slip","note":""},{"ch":59,"old":"是以早服","new":"是謂早服","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":59,"old":"則為不克","new":"則無不克","kind":"slip","note":"Audible: a note was sung where 無 is a rest."},{"ch":61,"old":"各得所欲","new":"各得其所欲","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":62,"old":"美言可以市尊，美行可以加人","new":"美言可以市，尊行可以加人","kind":"wb","note":"Wang Bi's commentary punctuates 美言可以市，尊行可以加人."},{"ch":62,"old":"不曰：求以得，有罪以免邪","new":"不曰以求得，有罪以免邪","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":68,"old":"配天之極","new":"配天古之極","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":69,"old":"攘無臂，執無兵，扔無敵","new":"攘無臂，扔無敵，執無兵","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":71,"old":"知，不知，上；不知，知，病。聖人不病，以其病病。夫唯病病，是以不病。","new":"知不知，上；不知知，病。夫唯病病，是以不病。聖人不病，以其病病，是以不病。","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":75,"old":"以其上求生之厚","new":"以其求生之厚","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 以其上求生之厚. The silk also lacks 上."},{"ch":76,"old":"木強則兵","new":"木強則共","kind":"dz","note":"Zhang transcription: 兵. Wang Bi glosses 共 as 物所加也."},{"ch":78,"old":"莫之能勝。以其無以易之","new":"莫之能勝，其無以易之","kind":"wb","note":""},{"ch":80,"old":"使民復結繩","new":"使人復結繩","kind":"wb","note":""}],"not_followed":[{"ch":9,"keep":"富貴而驕","daozang":"DZ 當貴而驕","why":"當 for 富: graphic error."},{"ch":31,"keep":"物或惡之","daozang":"DZ 物惑惡之","why":"惑 for 或."},{"ch":32,"keep":"樸雖小","daozang":"DZ omits","why":"Wang Bi's commentary explains 樸 at this point, so the text had it."},{"ch":48,"keep":"為道日損","daozang":"DZ 為道曰損","why":"曰 for 日."},{"ch":52,"keep":"沒身不殆","daozang":"DZ 沒其不殆","why":"其 for 身; ch. 16 has 沒身不殆."},{"ch":54,"keep":"善建者不拔","daozang":"DZ 善建不拔","why":"者 dropped; the parallel 善抱者 keeps it."},{"ch":62,"keep":"貴此道者","daozang":"DZ 貴比道者","why":"比 for 此."},{"ch":62,"keep":"何不曰","daozang":"DZ 何不日","why":"日 for 曰."},{"ch":63,"keep":"是以聖人猶難之","daozang":"DZ 是以聖人曰猶難之","why":"曰 intrusive."},{"ch":74,"keep":"夫代司殺者殺，是謂代大匠斲","daozang":"DZ 夫司殺者，是大匠斲","why":"Eye-skip: the next clause 夫代大匠斲者 needs 代."}],"sources":{"hsg":{"name":"Heshang Gong","text":"老子河上公章句, Wikisource (道經 rev. 2019760, 德經 rev. 1552800)","checked":"Each variant checked against the Song print in Sibu congkan, Kanripo KR5c0065@735c2ce; 29 transcription errors corrected (listed under fixes).","fixes":[[3,"慾","欲"],[4,"淵乎","淵兮"],[13,"吾何有患","吾有何患"],[15,"若朴","若樸"],[16,"覆","復"],[17,"親之譽之","親而譽之"],[21,"怳","恍"],[21,"其中有像","其中有象"],[27,"讁","謫"],[31,"以哀悲泣之","以悲哀泣之"],[39,"不轂","不穀"],[42,"不轂","不穀"],[50,"蓋以聞","蓋聞"],[50,"路行","陸行"],[50,"不披","不被"],[51,"孰之","熟之"],[52,"守柔日強","守柔曰強"],[55,"玃鳥","攫鳥"],[55,"不啞","不嗄"],[55,"知和日常","知和曰常"],[55,"知常日明","知常曰明"],[55,"益生日祥","益生曰祥"],[55,"心使氣日強","心使氣曰強"],[56,"踈","疏"],[59,"夫為嗇","夫唯嗇"],[61,"女所以勝屈於男陰勝陽以安盡不先求之也",""],[65,"乃至於大順","乃至大順"],[67,"捨","舍"],[73,"踈","疏"]]},"fuyi":{"name":"Fu Yi","text":"道德經古本篇, Zhengtong Daozang DZ 665, Kanripo KR5c0046@8c4cceb; inline pronunciation notes removed."},"silkA":{"name":"Silk A","text":"老子 (帛書甲本), Wikisource rev. 11135289: diplomatic transcription after 《長沙馬王堆漢墓簡帛集成》 (2014). □ = lost."},"silkB":{"name":"Silk B","text":"老子 (帛書乙本), Wikisource rev. 11135282, same basis. □ = lost."},"guodian":"Not included: no reliable digital transcription found (the free one replaces untypeable characters with ○)."}}};
